</clause>

<clause id="_6f0f2c95-1f3e-0b02-5bd7-992a8ecece20" obligation="normative" semx-id="_6f0f2c95-1f3e-0b02-5bd7-992a8ecece20">
<title id="_a826f507-0700-bfa7-ab8a-5e3c9b81bb99" semx-id="_b9d30a57-9846-3136-ab76-7aa747079a8d">Vocabulary</title><fmt-title depth="4" id="_7b7f05eb-5adf-2d84-fa9f-48ced2e07d11"><span class="fmt-caption-label">8<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>1<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>1<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>2<span class="fmt-clause-delim">.</span></span><span class="fmt-caption-delim"><tab/></span><semx element="title" source="_a826f507-0700-bfa7-ab8a-5e3c9b81bb99">Vocabulary</semx></fmt-title><fmt-xref-label><span class="fmt-element-name">Clause</span> 8<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>1<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>1<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>2</fmt-xref-label>
<p id="_807d45df-8d8a-c3e2-5677-b44d62ea7832" semx-id="_807d45df-8d8a-c3e2-5677-b44d62ea7832">GeoSPARQL version 1.1 consists of a Core module, a Topology Vocabulary extension, a Geometry extension, a Geometry Topology extension, an RDFS Entailment extension, and a Query Rewrite extension. Each of these modules will be briefly explained below.</p>
</clause>

<clause id="_dc1ad602-e780-e2fe-d0f1-1bb042347971" obligation="normative" semx-id="_dc1ad602-e780-e2fe-d0f1-1bb042347971">
<title id="_1657ebfc-abfd-ca6a-cc74-89c7de904186" semx-id="_c91868c4-770c-3b22-ebc2-fcf962583b0b">Core Module</title><fmt-title depth="4" id="_12ec88cf-5532-4d38-c684-ddb85b95b3e5"><span class="fmt-caption-label">8<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>1<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>1<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>3<span class="fmt-clause-delim">.</span></span><span class="fmt-caption-delim"><tab/></span><semx element="title" source="_1657ebfc-abfd-ca6a-cc74-89c7de904186">Core Module</semx></fmt-title><fmt-xref-label><span class="fmt-element-name">Clause</span> 8<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>1<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>1<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>3</fmt-xref-label>
<p id="_32420580-0a95-2f66-6634-8d74a3d283b7" semx-id="_32420580-0a95-2f66-6634-8d74a3d283b7">GeoSPARQL version 1.1 includes a CORE module. This defines the basic classes, relationships, and literals. These classes are suitable for both 2D and 3D.</p>

<clause id="_c2966864-c56a-f69a-d065-bca32d60fd7f" obligation="normative" semx-id="_c2966864-c56a-f69a-d065-bca32d60fd7f">
<title id="_6a0e4a0a-075d-933c-e68d-eb4aad915f9e" semx-id="_fa62f6a8-3d51-7438-9476-ca2ff585d19e">Topology Vocabulary Extension</title><fmt-title depth="5" id="_58fc8a4e-e577-bfda-1410-9882774c666c"><span class="fmt-caption-label">8<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>1<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>1<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>3<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>1<span class="fmt-clause-delim">.</span></span><span class="fmt-caption-delim"><tab/></span><semx element="title" source="_6a0e4a0a-075d-933c-e68d-eb4aad915f9e">Topology Vocabulary Extension</semx></fmt-title><fmt-xref-label><span class="fmt-element-name">Clause</span> 8<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>1<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>1<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>3<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>1</fmt-xref-label>
<p id="_e085f563-ab91-7754-d5fa-d73f494b1a4d" semx-id="_e085f563-ab91-7754-d5fa-d73f494b1a4d">This extension provides standard definitions for spatial relationships that can exist between one spatial object and another.</p>

<p id="_120c713b-c43b-3d41-f7f0-c26fe587bdcc" semx-id="_120c713b-c43b-3d41-f7f0-c26fe587bdcc">Relations between geometries have been defined using three different sets of rules:</p>

<ul id="_8feded93-23d4-986b-d485-174c204c9728" semx-id="_8feded93-23d4-986b-d485-174c204c9728"><li id="_4887185e-b09b-88cf-2f1a-170e6c6d7a49"><fmt-name id="_e43a3ba9-8cb1-bf57-e3a7-da2d1b4a4e87">—</fmt-name><p id="_2c2eb9c3-4db9-5794-82f9-aa20638a25b7" semx-id="_2c2eb9c3-4db9-5794-82f9-aa20638a25b7">Simple Features Relations</p>
</li>
<li id="_759b282b-4fd7-ee1a-5a47-8ca0e68256fb"><fmt-name id="_eade7d55-46b3-644c-20a6-1f7f29494bb1">—</fmt-name><p id="_c886eab1-1c90-44be-9230-dbd94b63d1af" semx-id="_c886eab1-1c90-44be-9230-dbd94b63d1af">Egenhofer Relations</p>
</li>
<li id="_fc15ebd1-7769-fb7b-ca08-15a88737e81a"><fmt-name id="_aa90d0a9-a807-e476-79b9-e008ee6fdd57">—</fmt-name><p id="_c2de236c-6dcb-7af1-f0bd-594793562a18" semx-id="_c2de236c-6dcb-7af1-f0bd-594793562a18">Region Connection Calculus RCC8</p>
</li>
</ul>

<p id="_932a5605-aad7-c53c-f2d2-b34e5cab10f0" semx-id="_932a5605-aad7-c53c-f2d2-b34e5cab10f0">These topological definitions specify topological relations in a 2D-context.</p>
</clause>

<clause id="_f6a98000-6eb9-e61e-3a4c-2d3c06d7a31a" obligation="normative" semx-id="_f6a98000-6eb9-e61e-3a4c-2d3c06d7a31a">
<title id="_27dcfc9d-d01e-ec8c-b155-527a9a2ce87d" semx-id="_33d8e1b0-ab84-365f-373c-667da04b1dd7">Geometry Extension</title><fmt-title depth="5" id="_cf5022d3-15f7-4104-07ef-7a2ae9f27358"><span class="fmt-caption-label">8<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>1<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>1<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>3<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>2<span class="fmt-clause-delim">.</span></span><span class="fmt-caption-delim"><tab/></span><semx element="title" source="_27dcfc9d-d01e-ec8c-b155-527a9a2ce87d">Geometry Extension</semx></fmt-title><fmt-xref-label><span class="fmt-element-name">Clause</span> 8<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>1<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>1<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>3<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>2</fmt-xref-label>
<p id="_abc7712f-abe6-2ea6-04c8-497e91802da0" semx-id="_abc7712f-abe6-2ea6-04c8-497e91802da0">GeoSPARQL 1.1 geometry module defines a class <tt>Geometry</tt> as a subclass of <tt>SpatialObject</tt>.</p>

<p id="_e3d6cb50-8eeb-f4c1-7f6a-671b1f10caff" semx-id="_e3d6cb50-8eeb-f4c1-7f6a-671b1f10caff">An instance of <tt>Geometry</tt> is not restricted to two dimensions.</p>

<p id="_3395be95-a6b1-246e-f43b-2a4f008ecf37" semx-id="_3395be95-a6b1-246e-f43b-2a4f008ecf37">A fine-grained classification of <tt>Geometry</tt> can use the <link target="https://opengeospatial.github.io/ogc-geosparql/geosparql11/sf_geometries.ttl" id="_5149cd03-9a82-3de3-ef92-c019a0d44b34">Simple Feature Vocabulary</link><fmt-link target="https://opengeospatial.github.io/ogc-geosparql/geosparql11/sf_geometries.ttl">Simple Feature Vocabulary</fmt-link> which extends the class <tt>Geometry</tt> with further types, such as <em>Point</em>, <em>Polygon</em> etc.</p>

<p id="_976764e4-d50d-eed9-e7ac-77e521ca7485" semx-id="_976764e4-d50d-eed9-e7ac-77e521ca7485">The Simple Features vocabulary also  does not restrict the definition of 3D variants of e.g.:</p>

<ul id="_f3b07dd4-8694-d12d-068d-2ada028e15ed" semx-id="_f3b07dd4-8694-d12d-068d-2ada028e15ed"><li id="_9c2b5d6f-5a6b-c74d-8f76-dda90ce8f7b4"><fmt-name id="_31bd753e-f555-b3be-2a6d-0cb12e295e65">—</fmt-name><p id="_281220ce-4038-95d3-dff4-6019efa716f1" semx-id="_281220ce-4038-95d3-dff4-6019efa716f1">(Multi)Points</p>
</li>
<li id="_80874816-4afa-942a-9514-9ed249ebb357"><fmt-name id="_741bd57f-914b-66ae-fc7b-75651bc29fcd">—</fmt-name><p id="_275a801c-22c2-a91c-2b46-13d3e0b8edda" semx-id="_275a801c-22c2-a91c-2b46-13d3e0b8edda">(Multi)LineStrings</p>
</li>
<li id="_58024f4c-2aea-9773-58a1-a6a622f652eb"><fmt-name id="_0bb3547d-fe16-062e-42f0-9c96687bd1ba">—</fmt-name><p id="_33727bdc-2d82-376b-8f94-e223d48a7bb8" semx-id="_33727bdc-2d82-376b-8f94-e223d48a7bb8">(Multi)Polygons</p>
</li>
<li id="_93271697-cfcf-1c59-faed-b2a98c54cd95"><fmt-name id="_15bd97bb-62e2-b4eb-39e9-433c10bcaac1">—</fmt-name><p id="_dbdf39a7-b29b-71e5-17d3-980ca77041af" semx-id="_dbdf39a7-b29b-71e5-17d3-980ca77041af">Polyhedralsurfaces</p>
</li>
</ul>

<p id="_8f9470f9-0efe-d6a1-f5d1-83f7e2b82e46" semx-id="_8f9470f9-0efe-d6a1-f5d1-83f7e2b82e46">In this sense, the GeoSPARQL geometry class hierarchy is already prepared for the inclusion of 3D geometries, even though some 3D geometry types have not been defined in teh Geometry class hierarchy provided by GeoSPARQL 1.1.</p>

<p id="_e16d972e-de8f-a24a-dff5-9a2fbc0b8ada" semx-id="_e16d972e-de8f-a24a-dff5-9a2fbc0b8ada">Examples of missing geometry types include common 3D primitives, such as <em>Cube</em> or <em>Mesh</em> surfaces which are integral parts of 3D representations, nor does it include extruded geometry types.</p>

<figure id="img_3dinifc" anchor="img_3dinifc" semx-id="_e7e6f5ff-602b-9754-12f6-fdcbd2b38824" autonum="1">
<name id="_c18d52c8-1f91-a23a-4c1a-b899b7c94646" semx-id="_a0dba9c2-89bc-0857-c9e2-cb473a2017f4">Possible approaches for representing 3D objects in IFC</name><fmt-name id="_23844e9f-c377-7560-0f79-3374c2c86eae"><span class="fmt-caption-label"><span class="fmt-element-name">Figure</span> 1</span><span class="fmt-caption-delim"> — </span><semx element="name" source="_c18d52c8-1f91-a23a-4c1a-b899b7c94646">Possible approaches for representing 3D objects in IFC</semx></fmt-name><fmt-xref-label><span class="fmt-element-name">Figure</span> 1</fmt-xref-label>
<image id="_40c43be8-5801-ed5f-d448-b33420878876" src="/home/runner/work/geosparql3dwhitepaper/geosparql3dwhitepaper/site/documents/document/_tmp_images_b23e76d0-eda9-450a-84ad-5c91026cd3da/7bc25ee0-0b73-4e19-8e55-c2b98d3c376f.png" extracted="true" mimetype="image/png" height="auto" width="auto" filename="figures/3DinIFC_FIG01.png" semx-id="_40c43be8-5801-ed5f-d448-b33420878876"/></figure>

<p id="_f61dcc9d-fede-0739-6480-dfc7c878aeac" semx-id="_f61dcc9d-fede-0739-6480-dfc7c878aeac">In addition, while boundary representations are supported in principle, their relation to aggregations of boundary surfaces is unsufficiently supported. Boundary surface aggregations may only be treated as GeometryCollections.</p>
</clause>

<clause id="_0f77f7a7-c588-49f7-76b7-3f065c302cb4" obligation="normative" semx-id="_0f77f7a7-c588-49f7-76b7-3f065c302cb4">
<title id="_25bf8aaf-44a1-aed3-0713-e4d003ffbf14" semx-id="_92dfbbb5-3e9a-724f-d92f-7032fae48fda">Geometry Metadata</title><fmt-title depth="5" id="_ebbbf880-718f-8608-9aff-265977c923b6"><span class="fmt-caption-label">8<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>1<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>1<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>3<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>3<span class="fmt-clause-delim">.</span></span><span class="fmt-caption-delim"><tab/></span><semx element="title" source="_25bf8aaf-44a1-aed3-0713-e4d003ffbf14">Geometry Metadata</semx></fmt-title><fmt-xref-label><span class="fmt-element-name">Clause</span> 8<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>1<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>1<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>3<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>3</fmt-xref-label>
<p id="_204c1fdc-53dd-e939-d4ec-d46d43285b48" semx-id="_204c1fdc-53dd-e939-d4ec-d46d43285b48">Concerning metadata of 3D models, GeoSPARQL 1.1 provides properties which can be reused in 3D contexts. In particular, the properties are:</p>

<ul id="_c72fa725-a7f7-5dbf-fd9b-474197df7b95" semx-id="_c72fa725-a7f7-5dbf-fd9b-474197df7b95"><li id="_b556d315-357a-3d1e-0c09-1ca0a4d0deca"><fmt-name id="_d41ceda5-52fa-7100-b661-28ba05ec6d3a">—</fmt-name><p id="_cb9880ed-7fa1-015a-3bf7-e49e623b636d" semx-id="_cb9880ed-7fa1-015a-3bf7-e49e623b636d"><tt>geo:hasVolume</tt></p>
</li>
<li id="_364db773-fac9-7540-9920-3e27dd980113"><fmt-name id="_40fcfff7-07ac-e942-c2e9-e3548fa48c63">—</fmt-name><p id="_45c99a90-f32a-f14e-80e2-55b958674062" semx-id="_45c99a90-f32a-f14e-80e2-55b958674062"><tt>geo:hasMetricVolume</tt></p>
</li>
</ul>

<p id="_52bf8398-7e79-e2c8-7fb5-38b20401c78b" semx-id="_52bf8398-7e79-e2c8-7fb5-38b20401c78b">While these properties may describe features of a 3D solid, specific parameters of 3D models such as projection matrices, the material of a 3D model and its reflectivity which are commonplace in 3D rendering applications cannot be represented in GeoSPARQL 1.1.</p>

<p id="_e6fec17c-1455-cf8e-530a-45859fd60fdd" semx-id="_e6fec17c-1455-cf8e-530a-45859fd60fdd">A further requirement for 3D support is the ability to save 3D data in a knowledge graph.</p>

<p id="_1e70b470-c91d-7a14-b51c-be9d664c7845" semx-id="_1e70b470-c91d-7a14-b51c-be9d664c7845">The Geometry extension enables geometries to be defined within the GeoSPARQL framework. Supported geometry formats include RDF literals in WKT, GML, GeoJSON, KML and DGGS.</p>

<p id="_ef6ce84b-ec18-b77d-f8f2-50500056c9d4" semx-id="_ef6ce84b-ec18-b77d-f8f2-50500056c9d4">These literals are investigated for the storage of 3D data.</p>

<figure id="img_dimensions" anchor="img_dimensions" semx-id="_be5dc2c9-6213-2c58-ba88-162727983e19" autonum="2">
<name id="_aaf0feca-c615-681d-36e8-57cf042c5029" semx-id="_483a9a99-bd8e-f7dc-452e-3d843eec7056">Difference between 2D, 2D with height attribute 2.5D and 3D geometry</name><fmt-name id="_39c8190a-8acd-c942-2087-ab535b7dddce"><span class="fmt-caption-label"><span class="fmt-element-name">Figure</span> 2</span><span class="fmt-caption-delim"> — </span><semx element="name" source="_aaf0feca-c615-681d-36e8-57cf042c5029">Difference between 2D, 2D with height attribute 2.5D and 3D geometry</semx></fmt-name><fmt-xref-label><span class="fmt-element-name">Figure</span> 2</fmt-xref-label>
<image id="_80253516-92ab-e209-f8cc-fd93ae6afcca" src="/home/runner/work/geosparql3dwhitepaper/geosparql3dwhitepaper/site/documents/document/_tmp_images_b23e76d0-eda9-450a-84ad-5c91026cd3da/2bce282b-29ca-4d97-a950-32a9b7881aa6.png" extracted="true" mimetype="image/png" height="auto" width="auto" filename="figures/2d-2d--2andahalfd-3d-difference.png" semx-id="_80253516-92ab-e209-f8cc-fd93ae6afcca"/></figure>

