</tr></tbody>
</table>

<p id="_26f4da62-79fc-fa53-cf0f-52f2383c66bf" semx-id="_26f4da62-79fc-fa53-cf0f-52f2383c66bf">*The support of 3D for DGGS is dependent on the DGGS grid definition being used.</p>

<p id="_557eaaad-86e7-6950-e540-6eb720c2e954" semx-id="_557eaaad-86e7-6950-e540-6eb720c2e954">The comparison of capabilities in <eref bibitemid="tab_capabilitieslittypes" citeas="" id="_b2b6aaa1-9ab9-dac9-dc1a-320a9231a865"/><fmt-xref target="tab_capabilitieslittypes">tab/capabilitieslittypes</fmt-xref> shows limited support for 3D of some of GeoSPARQL 1.1 literal formats. While these existing capabilities are an important information, one might also take into account the option to not save the 3D model in the knowledge graph directly for performance reasons. GeoSPARQL 1.1 currently does not allow geometries to be defined as a external resource, so the need may arise to find a solution in GeoSPARQL 3D.</p>

<p id="_d025106e-7333-bc3e-a393-4f6170eafc15" semx-id="_d025106e-7333-bc3e-a393-4f6170eafc15">GeoSPARQL 1.1 also does not restrict the usage of coordinate reference systems with 3D support. There are currently almost 300 coordinate reference systems in the database  <link target="https://epsg.io/?q=%20kind%3AGEOG3DCRS" id="_9d9812b6-9fa6-0b3c-8735-ef2f3b181356">epsg.io</link><fmt-link target="https://epsg.io/?q=%20kind%3AGEOG3DCRS">epsg.io</fmt-link> which can be used to describe 3D data encoded in the GeoSPARQL graph literals listed above.</p>

<p id="_b7085516-a816-61dd-16b5-38df11c79463" semx-id="_b7085516-a816-61dd-16b5-38df11c79463">GeoSPARQL already supports linking multiple geometries (e.g., 2D and 2.5D) to a single feature. We aim to extend this with 3D geometries.</p>

<p id="_bfb0da8c-12bf-c7d8-f06b-7993ccb103cb" semx-id="_bfb0da8c-12bf-c7d8-f06b-7993ccb103cb">For example, a tree in the real world may be represented in databases with both a 2D and a 3D geometry.</p>

<p id="_21ba72b6-7176-bfa5-48ef-f29ba2802cfa" semx-id="_21ba72b6-7176-bfa5-48ef-f29ba2802cfa">This should not require duplication of the tree entity — there can be one tree instance with both geometries linked, avoiding redundancy in the data model.</p>
</clause>
</clause>

<clause id="_49f14c67-1f1e-1b91-994f-fd19d02afd3e" obligation="normative" semx-id="_49f14c67-1f1e-1b91-994f-fd19d02afd3e">
<title id="_c45a042a-7211-2083-252b-b6aa6bca1562" semx-id="_9a2f713b-c8f9-287c-57b7-b0b208e81088">Query functions with 3D support</title><fmt-title depth="4" id="_7d1b8315-fc90-7507-8e41-8e621a370390"><span class="fmt-caption-label">8<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>1<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>1<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>4<span class="fmt-clause-delim">.</span></span><span class="fmt-caption-delim"><tab/></span><semx element="title" source="_c45a042a-7211-2083-252b-b6aa6bca1562">Query functions with 3D support</semx></fmt-title><fmt-xref-label><span class="fmt-element-name">Clause</span> 8<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>1<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>1<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>4</fmt-xref-label>
<p id="_71ced540-d467-54e3-c64b-2b9bbd81e9fb" semx-id="_71ced540-d467-54e3-c64b-2b9bbd81e9fb">GeoSPARQL 1.1 functions currently do not offer fully-featured 3D support. However, the functions listed in  <eref bibitemid="tab_queryfuncs3d" citeas="" id="_f3401679-8898-347c-37c2-a9e99fa74fa3"/><fmt-xref target="tab_queryfuncs3d">tab/queryfuncs3d</fmt-xref> are functions which may take into account the Z coordinate, if they are available.</p>

<table id="tab_queryfuncs3d" anchor="tab:queryfuncs3d" semx-id="_5e9069d1-c1a8-9858-41f0-c9c3d94e4ad1"><colgroup><col width="25%"/><col width="25%"/><col width="25%"/><col width="25%"/></colgroup>
<name id="_d10c8cb0-749b-293a-37c5-44b642f3aebb" semx-id="_689ce13c-01e4-d74a-dc91-5c72eb1a57be">Query functions with 3D support</name><fmt-name id="_df8139e5-9e29-91e3-6f30-a996518b5ce2"><span class="fmt-caption-label"><span class="fmt-element-name">Table</span></span><span class="fmt-caption-delim"> — </span><semx element="name" source="_d10c8cb0-749b-293a-37c5-44b642f3aebb">Query functions with 3D support</semx></fmt-name><fmt-xref-label>tab/queryfuncs3d</fmt-xref-label>
<tbody><tr id="_f6836beb-ee7e-4b06-2090-a84f9b4711f3" semx-id="_f6836beb-ee7e-4b06-2090-a84f9b4711f3"><td id="_ca824fb9-e1a5-9f1d-439e-ba8bcdc51450" valign="middle" align="left" semx-id="_ca824fb9-e1a5-9f1d-439e-ba8bcdc51450">GeoSPARQL function</td>
<td id="_186468a7-bd5c-5ecf-1df3-93f0bb707c73" valign="middle" align="left" semx-id="_186468a7-bd5c-5ecf-1df3-93f0bb707c73">Z-Coordinate Supported</td>
<td id="_699daa95-9e39-afcf-138c-87c70da183a4" valign="middle" align="left" semx-id="_699daa95-9e39-afcf-138c-87c70da183a4">2.5D</td>
<td id="_91588768-90f3-937d-60e7-42386a483c86" valign="middle" align="left" semx-id="_91588768-90f3-937d-60e7-42386a483c86">3D</td>
</tr><tr id="_c2665b05-e334-9f8b-d6da-d1cfc72cbdb9" semx-id="_c2665b05-e334-9f8b-d6da-d1cfc72cbdb9"><td id="_d8a5eddd-2e4d-72ab-9653-78cbff5b34e3" valign="middle" align="left" semx-id="_d8a5eddd-2e4d-72ab-9653-78cbff5b34e3">geof:is3D</td>
<td id="_fc33a6c5-afaa-8979-7baa-6447c09c9120" valign="middle" align="left" semx-id="_fc33a6c5-afaa-8979-7baa-6447c09c9120">Yes</td>
<td id="_39274d8a-8077-7091-f2e9-c70cf31b5923" valign="middle" align="left" semx-id="_39274d8a-8077-7091-f2e9-c70cf31b5923">Yes</td>
<td id="_4490bf42-a23b-cf91-da93-1bc5e3f4ddda" valign="middle" align="left" semx-id="_4490bf42-a23b-cf91-da93-1bc5e3f4ddda">Yes</td>
</tr><tr id="_c57d107a-73d5-18db-1827-7a68727ca94a" semx-id="_c57d107a-73d5-18db-1827-7a68727ca94a"><td id="_760e6ecd-1f4b-0c94-0394-1fc51d1764f4" valign="middle" align="left" semx-id="_760e6ecd-1f4b-0c94-0394-1fc51d1764f4">geof:minZ</td>
<td id="_573bf14b-5a15-ae8a-7d1f-4cebcc5f870d" valign="middle" align="left" semx-id="_573bf14b-5a15-ae8a-7d1f-4cebcc5f870d">Yes</td>
<td id="_f0e0ab38-fbee-1396-b7e5-53824230109b" valign="middle" align="left" semx-id="_f0e0ab38-fbee-1396-b7e5-53824230109b">Yes</td>
<td id="_0eb8ca6f-d373-c9c3-a0f9-fa42ff3643f1" valign="middle" align="left" semx-id="_0eb8ca6f-d373-c9c3-a0f9-fa42ff3643f1">Yes</td>
</tr><tr id="_4b2e0d52-e3ea-f73e-9c07-143c93a0c6de" semx-id="_4b2e0d52-e3ea-f73e-9c07-143c93a0c6de"><td id="_677bf9d8-a11c-5890-c94b-5325dabb4a42" valign="middle" align="left" semx-id="_677bf9d8-a11c-5890-c94b-5325dabb4a42">geof:maxZ</td>
<td id="_e622f932-419b-d637-e5d7-fdb71f8a2cf9" valign="middle" align="left" semx-id="_e622f932-419b-d637-e5d7-fdb71f8a2cf9">Yes</td>
<td id="_f7b1dfef-8eeb-a5c3-26a8-bd2ce4521bc1" valign="middle" align="left" semx-id="_f7b1dfef-8eeb-a5c3-26a8-bd2ce4521bc1">Yes</td>
<td id="_abcbdc09-90b4-2ed4-e92a-2f6286836a8a" valign="middle" align="left" semx-id="_abcbdc09-90b4-2ed4-e92a-2f6286836a8a">Yes</td>
</tr></tbody>
</table>

<p id="_cdb6e304-26b1-a04a-16fc-ebad234e4305" semx-id="_cdb6e304-26b1-a04a-16fc-ebad234e4305">These functions check for the presence of Z coordinates or filter out maximum and minimum Z coordinates of the given geometry.</p>

<p id="_73c81cb0-f740-f205-3e46-b9e8080b09be" semx-id="_73c81cb0-f740-f205-3e46-b9e8080b09be">Other query functions such as geof:intersection may exhibit an undefined und hence unwanted behavior when executed with 3D geometries, i.e. ignore the Z coordinate, reduce the 3D geometry to a (faulty) 2D geometry among others. The behavior currently depends on the specific implementation and has not been spelled out in the GeoSPARQL 1.1 specification.</p>
</clause>

<clause id="_0a74fa15-6756-7677-8f94-985124d486e7" obligation="normative" semx-id="_0a74fa15-6756-7677-8f94-985124d486e7">
<title id="_6bbd7845-f24a-78d9-91b7-4247371e87eb" semx-id="_c7e1c57e-3d37-b796-9242-fda5581b0f55">Geometry Topology Extension</title><fmt-title depth="4" id="_c7a379f4-b2eb-63b4-6a89-d0fdd4da58f8"><span class="fmt-caption-label">8<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>1<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>1<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>5<span class="fmt-clause-delim">.</span></span><span class="fmt-caption-delim"><tab/></span><semx element="title" source="_6bbd7845-f24a-78d9-91b7-4247371e87eb">Geometry Topology Extension</semx></fmt-title><fmt-xref-label><span class="fmt-element-name">Clause</span> 8<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>1<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>1<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>5</fmt-xref-label>
<p id="_e0322771-ab8c-63a1-725a-5d5a051baef4" semx-id="_e0322771-ab8c-63a1-725a-5d5a051baef4">Another extension is the Geometry Topology extension. This provides query functions that return relationships between different geometries based on the topology vocabulary extension.</p>

<p id="_0dd0fffa-281b-c6bd-18bc-e5ca4f3e3ae8" semx-id="_0dd0fffa-281b-c6bd-18bc-e5ca4f3e3ae8">The Geometry Topology Extension describes three sets of geometry relation functions, akin to the properties which were previously defined in the Topology Vocabulary extension.</p>

<p id="_d95b949a-ec3d-4907-65a4-2b7c6cc566e4" semx-id="_d95b949a-ec3d-4907-65a4-2b7c6cc566e4">The Simple Features relations, the Egenhofer relations and the RCC8 relations as query functions are merely defined on 2D relations and warrant an examination concerning their suitability and upgradability to 3D.</p>

<p id="_a57663dc-d93c-815d-30d1-1cd6ce0164f2" semx-id="_a57663dc-d93c-815d-30d1-1cd6ce0164f2">It might be desirable to even use different sets of relations which will be available in GeoSPARQL 3D, which are better suited for the specific needs for 3D geometries.</p>

<p id="_653a4a00-4440-845b-9f64-adec9d8b2e80" semx-id="_653a4a00-4440-845b-9f64-adec9d8b2e80">Hence, the extension of the Geometry Topology extension with 3D topology functionality might be needed and its compatibility with the already available 2D topological relations would need to be examined.</p>
</clause>

<clause id="_3aeedff1-c38d-9c78-042d-bdf6346376b0" obligation="normative" semx-id="_3aeedff1-c38d-9c78-042d-bdf6346376b0">
<title id="_1a937a86-a0fb-b110-d24a-3bedc933ab0e" semx-id="_5183de5e-f31d-2d1a-d697-6dbffdacd2e0">RDFS Entailment Extension</title><fmt-title depth="4" id="_3f57027d-91cf-a002-0e92-a6cc0e41cd0b"><span class="fmt-caption-label">8<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>1<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>1<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>6<span class="fmt-clause-delim">.</span></span><span class="fmt-caption-delim"><tab/></span><semx element="title" source="_1a937a86-a0fb-b110-d24a-3bedc933ab0e">RDFS Entailment Extension</semx></fmt-title><fmt-xref-label><span class="fmt-element-name">Clause</span> 8<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>1<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>1<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>6</fmt-xref-label>
<p id="_11f51975-cbd7-99e1-7b3c-4fcbeae7074b" semx-id="_11f51975-cbd7-99e1-7b3c-4fcbeae7074b">The RDFS Entailment extension provides rules for reasoning over geometries. Based on specific statements, additional information can be inferred. This kind of logical inference can also be applied to geometry. GeoSPARQL includes logic for reasoning over simple features geometries.</p>

<p id="_25267d07-8f67-1d82-d0e9-4af81e8577e6" semx-id="_25267d07-8f67-1d82-d0e9-4af81e8577e6">All elements of the RDFS entailment extensions do not provide 3D capabilities either.</p>
</clause>

<clause id="_db90132e-62e4-0e84-314c-4dc79cd3971a" obligation="normative" semx-id="_db90132e-62e4-0e84-314c-4dc79cd3971a">
<title id="_8dcf8490-d575-4704-e0cc-264cf71e1cd5" semx-id="_f9d684ee-7a41-ddf1-80f0-c568523b4541">Geometry Class Hierarchy</title><fmt-title depth="4" id="_7948d484-7ab6-8f5c-1201-70c4ef73e420"><span class="fmt-caption-label">8<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>1<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>1<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>7<span class="fmt-clause-delim">.</span></span><span class="fmt-caption-delim"><tab/></span><semx element="title" source="_8dcf8490-d575-4704-e0cc-264cf71e1cd5">Geometry Class Hierarchy</semx></fmt-title><fmt-xref-label><span class="fmt-element-name">Clause</span> 8<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>1<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>1<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>7</fmt-xref-label>
<p id="_da1ae0e8-95dc-b0d6-9545-b995b02835b8" semx-id="_da1ae0e8-95dc-b0d6-9545-b995b02835b8">As described in the Geometry Extension part, the Geometry class hierarchy will need to be extended to include 3D primitives and 3D specific types so that reasoners may take advantage of 3D types in reasoning tasks. This may need to be done for 3D serializations that are not equal to WKT and GML, since only for these two serializations, the RDFS Entailment was defined in GeoSPARQL 1.1.</p>
</clause>

<clause id="_22b8510b-9135-d7b3-7555-b70553b29337" obligation="normative" semx-id="_22b8510b-9135-d7b3-7555-b70553b29337">
<title id="_a059cc8d-d710-3f22-2d91-286241756833" semx-id="_b9332127-c365-d139-b78a-9f652354bcb1">Query Rewrite Extension</title><fmt-title depth="4" id="_58c0cc0e-e8cd-839c-e0d4-736eda31acea"><span class="fmt-caption-label">8<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>1<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>1<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>8<span class="fmt-clause-delim">.</span></span><span class="fmt-caption-delim"><tab/></span><semx element="title" source="_a059cc8d-d710-3f22-2d91-286241756833">Query Rewrite Extension</semx></fmt-title><fmt-xref-label><span class="fmt-element-name">Clause</span> 8<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>1<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>1<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>8</fmt-xref-label>
<p id="_e8ad691d-2de2-99c1-7a13-aa0b21e0ff0d" semx-id="_e8ad691d-2de2-99c1-7a13-aa0b21e0ff0d">GeoSPARQL allows queries such as whether “Feature A” is located within “Feature B” using its vocabulary. The Query Rewrite extension specifies a RIF <eref bibitemid="Boley_13" citeas="" id="_d41bc618-a99a-fe53-e620-1efac1e063d9"><localityStack><locality type="anchor"><referenceFrom>ROE</referenceFrom></locality></localityStack></eref><fmt-xref target="Boley_13">Boley/13</fmt-xref> rule that enables query rewriting. However, this extension does not support the rewriting of 3D queries.</p>

<p id="_5bd7a3d9-b466-d26c-8fdb-f180d78487d8" semx-id="_5bd7a3d9-b466-d26c-8fdb-f180d78487d8">3D compatibility of the Query Rewrite extension would depend similarly on the 3D relations defined, since the Query Rewrite Extension would merely apply the graph patterns defined in the other extensions.</p>
</clause>
</clause>

<clause id="_6f0e5b8f-db48-02f9-327f-68d29e6603a8" obligation="normative" semx-id="_6f0e5b8f-db48-02f9-327f-68d29e6603a8">
<title id="_76b20c8a-ee2d-9ffd-5ff5-b79341e17e25" semx-id="_2f195ffb-820a-cf9c-3880-f7c159e0f5e2">Adoption of GeoSPARQL 1.1</title><fmt-title depth="3" id="_aa4cc4f4-73c9-a554-8378-58cd98650b53"><span class="fmt-caption-label">8<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>1<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>2<span class="fmt-clause-delim">.</span></span><span class="fmt-caption-delim"><tab/></span><semx element="title" source="_76b20c8a-ee2d-9ffd-5ff5-b79341e17e25">Adoption of GeoSPARQL 1.1</semx></fmt-title><fmt-xref-label><span class="fmt-element-name">Clause</span> 8<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>1<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>2</fmt-xref-label>
<p id="_569934cb-8bd0-8926-8230-a3450bd02bce" semx-id="_569934cb-8bd0-8926-8230-a3450bd02bce">The article “A GeoSPARQL Compliance Benchmark”, Jovanovik, Homburg and Spasić <eref type="inline" bibitemid="ijgi10070487" citeas="[47]" id="_b64d9947-8c20-b108-e169-db3a3f2b5a5e"/><fmt-xref type="inline" target="ijgi10070487">[47]</fmt-xref> describes the results of a GeoSPARQL compliance benchmark. The measure of the extent of GeoSPARQL support in GeoSPARQL-enabled RDF triplestores was done in 2021. The article concludes that the GeoSPARQL standard, almost nine years after its initial release, is often only partially supported by major triplestore vendors. This raises the question of whether adding functionality to a standard increases implementation complexity, or whether new and more powerful capabilities can instead stimulate adoption by enabling new and more valuable use cases for software developers and users.</p>

<p id="_5b60a9cd-c61c-2ed3-cac5-4a88827586a6" semx-id="_5b60a9cd-c61c-2ed3-cac5-4a88827586a6">On the <link target="https://portal.ogc.org/public_ogc/compliance/implementing.php?&amp;specid=528" id="_07703e11-b445-1c25-2a69-8aea5605cd11">OGC Implementation Database</link><fmt-link target="https://portal.ogc.org/public_ogc/compliance/implementing.php?&amp;specid=528">OGC Implementation Database</fmt-link> there is no organisation compliant with the OGC GeoSPARQL standard. There are four organisations that are implementing the GeoSPARQL (v 1.0) standard.</p>
</clause>

<clause id="_1769fba0-3717-6086-468b-5c225793f275" obligation="normative" semx-id="_1769fba0-3717-6086-468b-5c225793f275">
<title id="_7d283976-c8d0-d8d3-b2c6-fadfb4ecbda2" semx-id="_c85981bd-305d-12db-a20c-48da183ee511">GeoSPARQL Aligments</title><fmt-title depth="3" id="_810acf70-63d9-0f17-372f-3cbda18951c1"><span class="fmt-caption-label">8<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>1<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>3<span class="fmt-clause-delim">.</span></span><span class="fmt-caption-delim"><tab/></span><semx element="title" source="_7d283976-c8d0-d8d3-b2c6-fadfb4ecbda2">GeoSPARQL Aligments</semx></fmt-title><fmt-xref-label><span class="fmt-element-name">Clause</span> 8<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>1<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>3</fmt-xref-label>
<p id="_d9572aa1-252d-4fc2-7ec7-cc402bdcca8f" semx-id="_d9572aa1-252d-4fc2-7ec7-cc402bdcca8f">GeoSPARQL is aligned with a range of established ontologies and vocabularies covering different aspects of geospatial information, metadata, provenance, time, sensors, and linked data. See <link target="https://docs.ogc.org/is/22-047r1/22-047r1.html#_6f67539d-8b79-4a2b-b7b9-9b4a54e710d0" id="_2f5cff4c-fe01-6fc3-d528-97174b6bf6b4">Annex E of the GeoSPARQL Standard</link><fmt-link target="https://docs.ogc.org/is/22-047r1/22-047r1.html#_6f67539d-8b79-4a2b-b7b9-9b4a54e710d0">Annex E of the GeoSPARQL Standard</fmt-link>. Alignments with additional ontologies may be relevant for a broader 3D context. As an example, ontologies such as Reloc <eref type="inline" bibitemid="gobelsRelativeLocationOntology2024" citeas="[36]" id="_1c13c0ec-57eb-866e-6839-48eaf6375ec5"/><fmt-xref type="inline" target="gobelsRelativeLocationOntology2024">[36]</fmt-xref>, BOT <xref target="doi_10.3233_SW-200385" id="_18f752be-bfc6-f943-6049-d7c5699a025f"/><fmt-xref target="doi_10.3233_SW-200385">[doi_10.3233_SW-200385]</fmt-xref> and ifcOWL <xref target="_3e4260b3605459b8ce220cfb42a3525" id="_5610cf2c-064e-e2eb-3fe4-133e3ff25f0b"/><fmt-xref target="_3e4260b3605459b8ce220cfb42a3525">[_3e4260b3605459b8ce220cfb42a3525]</fmt-xref> can be interesting.</p>
</clause>

<clause id="_e735c70f-0b73-a2e1-cacf-1ac8f466a90d" obligation="normative" semx-id="_e735c70f-0b73-a2e1-cacf-1ac8f466a90d">
<title id="_bd9c1d03-4f52-dfdd-a04c-ce12459c89aa" semx-id="_54dc3bb2-94d1-6aa0-aa74-812c0df65e8e">GeoSPARQL functionality and geometries comparison</title><fmt-title depth="3" id="_190e69ac-cea0-320a-a531-461b6612f1f9"><span class="fmt-caption-label">8<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>1<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>4<span class="fmt-clause-delim">.</span></span><span class="fmt-caption-delim"><tab/></span><semx element="title" source="_bd9c1d03-4f52-dfdd-a04c-ce12459c89aa">GeoSPARQL functionality and geometries comparison</semx></fmt-title><fmt-xref-label><span class="fmt-element-name">Clause</span> 8<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>1<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>4</fmt-xref-label>
<p id="_64411093-2e7b-7166-f932-aea6e98707be" semx-id="_64411093-2e7b-7166-f932-aea6e98707be">GeoSPARQL functionality can be compared with established spatial database extensions such as PostGIS H2GIS and SpatiaLite. The alligment offers a useful basis for comparing common spatial operations and capabilities with those defined by GeoSPARQL. The selection for spatial databases is not intended to be exhaustive. Other spatial databases, libraries and systems could also be included, depending on the scope of the comparison. The selected systems should therefore be regarded as representative reference implementations rather than a complete overview of available spatial technologies.</p>

<p id="_c6c6f683-4000-98fd-8b67-2fae0b50ae46" semx-id="_c6c6f683-4000-98fd-8b67-2fae0b50ae46">The comparison with existing open-source spatial technologies is intended to identify gaps between the functionality currently provided by GeoSPARQL and the capabilities available in established spatial databases. This provides an indication of the development potential of GeoSPARQL. The comparison can therefore be used to identify potential areas for further development and to assess which capabilities could add value to future versions of the standard.</p>

<p id="_290ae507-99ec-30b6-354d-4d006c37cf15" semx-id="_290ae507-99ec-30b6-354d-4d006c37cf15">When focusing specifically on 3D functionality, the largest differences in the comparison are found in geometry creation and editing, spatial measurements, and operations related to triangulation and topographic geometry. Also the topological functions shows some gaps between GeoSPARQL and the functionality in the spatial databases. These areas contain functionality available in the compared spatial technologies that is not represented in GeoSPARQL. They therefore provide the clearest areas for investigating potential further development of 3D functionality in GeoSPARQL. See  <xref target="annex_alignments" id="_a2cc183f-315c-7863-71aa-86127a7deef1"/><fmt-xref target="annex_alignments"><span class="fmt-element-name">Annex</span> A</fmt-xref> Annex A: Alignment GeoSPARQL and Open Source Relational Databases with Geospatial Support</p>

<p id="_b1898ddf-9a70-69f4-ec8d-5b11cd949633" semx-id="_b1898ddf-9a70-69f4-ec8d-5b11cd949633">Besides the available functions, the geometry representations used by GeoSPARQL, such as WKT and GML, can also be compared with geometry representations used in other standards and technologies, such as IFC, STEP and Open CASCADE. The comparison shows that the largest differences in 3D geometry representation occur beyond basic points, curves and surfaces. IFC, STEP and OpenCascade provide more extensive representations for solids, volumes, boolean and constructive geometry, swept and revolved geometry and 3D transformations and spatial operations. These areas therefore provide relevant starting points for investigating potential extensions of GeoSPARQL for more comprehensive 3D geometry representation and processing. See Annex B: Allignment of Geometry</p>
</clause>
</clause>
</clause>

<clause id="_76ebed1c-0fee-13d9-9a02-14c1e8d23b47" obligation="normative" semx-id="_76ebed1c-0fee-13d9-9a02-14c1e8d23b47" displayorder="16">
<title id="_58b128b6-fc12-73fc-034c-24aaee1fd9ce" semx-id="_ba97df29-dd76-9403-eb36-29cea9cab1cf">Related Work</title><fmt-title depth="1" id="_9630aa22-a85d-3530-8f2d-6d3a149764a2"><span class="fmt-caption-label">9<span class="fmt-clause-delim">.</span></span><span class="fmt-caption-delim"><tab/></span><semx element="title" source="_58b128b6-fc12-73fc-034c-24aaee1fd9ce">Related Work</semx></fmt-title><fmt-xref-label><span class="fmt-element-name">Clause</span> 9</fmt-xref-label>
<p id="_9bb9dd5d-9383-ec74-3802-8f4f7252c064" semx-id="_9bb9dd5d-9383-ec74-3802-8f4f7252c064">Various vocabularies, standards, and domain-specific approaches provide concepts for describing 3D geometries, topology, spatial relationships, and their semantics. This section reviews these related developments to understand the different ways in which 3D spatial information is currently modeled and to identify concepts and approaches that may complement or inform the further development of 3D in GeoSPARQL.</p>

<p id="_c565d69c-152c-3773-4c97-68d462419111" semx-id="_c565d69c-152c-3773-4c97-68d462419111">A substantial body of work shows that <strong>there is no single universal 3D web ontology</strong>, but several reusable, often domain‑ or format‑specific vocabularies to describe geometry, topology, scenes, and domain semantics. Two principal flavours of ontologies can be identified in existing literature:i) those meant for the generic representation of 3D objects and shapes, and ii) a growing body of domain-specific ontologies <xref target="flotynski2017ontology" id="_2277bbdd-eaa0-f831-b24d-eb7f18b724f6"/><fmt-xref target="flotynski2017ontology">[flotynski2017ontology]</fmt-xref>.</p>

<p id="_520af256-24b5-b263-4665-ee3441e895eb" semx-id="_520af256-24b5-b263-4665-ee3441e895eb">This section starts by providing a brief overview of these two flavours of ontologies. It then dives into domain-specific initiatives, considering Geo-spatial, Cultural Heritage and BIM. This section closes by enumerating various encoding formats relevant for 3D.</p>

<clause id="_0d974dd1-6718-5c25-eb8e-0825bf0db23a" obligation="normative" semx-id="_0d974dd1-6718-5c25-eb8e-0825bf0db23a">
<title id="_c7b9e3f0-be8f-ab7a-1e10-bb07d731da3f" semx-id="_890080f2-12c2-15da-a056-c7696aba69b2">3D Exchange formats</title><fmt-title depth="2" id="_2eb9838a-f15d-e6e8-4797-0260dca98bcf"><span class="fmt-caption-label">9<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>1<span class="fmt-clause-delim">.</span></span><span class="fmt-caption-delim"><tab/></span><semx element="title" source="_c7b9e3f0-be8f-ab7a-1e10-bb07d731da3f">3D Exchange formats</semx></fmt-title><fmt-xref-label><span class="fmt-element-name">Clause</span> 9<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>1</fmt-xref-label>
<p id="_a9e5df77-02a0-d474-dbd9-681e0e15e030" semx-id="_a9e5df77-02a0-d474-dbd9-681e0e15e030">A new GeoSPARQL standard should be compatible with existing approaches to encode 3D data. Semantic technologies should enable users to encode important metadata in the knowledge graph, while retaining the possibility to use already well-known 3D geometry formats either as String literals or file references. <eref type="inline" bibitemid="bonduel2019including" citeas="[10]" id="_cc80c92a-efeb-1e50-69d8-11a7eefa5221"/><fmt-xref type="inline" target="bonduel2019including">[10]</fmt-xref> shows how common widespread geometry formats could be included into knowledge graphs, which could be one starting point to investigate the integration within GeoSPARQL.</p>

<clause id="_00558516-3661-6f07-89b0-59ee83544de1" obligation="normative" semx-id="_00558516-3661-6f07-89b0-59ee83544de1">
<title id="_3ec1b393-38b0-c047-3da2-306d9eabea01" semx-id="_84bb07d1-3267-6709-120f-ea45d13d50dd">Standard for the Exchange of Product model data (STEP) and OntoSTEP</title><fmt-title depth="3" id="_ad84c334-018d-f8db-db0e-24a4faea4b5c"><span class="fmt-caption-label">9<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>1<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>1<span class="fmt-clause-delim">.</span></span><span class="fmt-caption-delim"><tab/></span><semx element="title" source="_3ec1b393-38b0-c047-3da2-306d9eabea01">Standard for the Exchange of Product model data (STEP) and OntoSTEP</semx></fmt-title><fmt-xref-label><span class="fmt-element-name">Clause</span> 9<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>1<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>1</fmt-xref-label>
<p id="_556a6527-fb22-4018-f95e-5a12fc1a1aed" semx-id="_556a6527-fb22-4018-f95e-5a12fc1a1aed"><link target="https://www.steptools.com/stds/step/" id="_a51a3471-56fa-53cd-c24a-d4ec68b2362d">STEP (Standard for the Exchange of Product model data)</link><fmt-link target="https://www.steptools.com/stds/step/">STEP (Standard for the Exchange of Product model data)</fmt-link> is an ISO standard for representing and exchanging product and engineering information, including CAD models. OntoSTEP translates STEP concepts into an ontology-based representation to support semantic integration and reasoning. OntoSTEP is a research-oriented approach that translates STEP/EXPRESS schemas and instances into OWL to enable semantic integration, querying, and reasoning. Initially developed at NIST, it has been further investigated through later implementations, but it remains a research approach rather than an established standard or widely adopted 3D ontology.</p>

<ul id="_b88012e8-2e1b-6d8c-e444-bb5c72b4e453" semx-id="_b88012e8-2e1b-6d8c-e444-bb5c72b4e453"><li id="_c169facd-0b0e-394e-fcab-9ad99ab294d2"><fmt-name id="_758725e2-725d-63db-5a5c-f6e63ce9a214">—</fmt-name><p id="_9e2cc472-593f-9b7c-7e6f-976e3d79ef4a" semx-id="_9e2cc472-593f-9b7c-7e6f-976e3d79ef4a">OWL encodings of STEP AP203/AP214/AP242 and BREP/CSG concepts for CAD models <eref type="inline" bibitemid="zhao2024envon" citeas="[39]" id="_8216f2d2-b3b8-4ebf-6af4-0bd6cf46505e"/><fmt-xref type="inline" target="zhao2024envon">[39]</fmt-xref>.</p>
</li>
<li id="_f4ece155-9908-1561-0006-cd3ee6868cb6"><fmt-name id="_7c5b8fd0-4cee-59cd-2eff-e14e83e22de1">—</fmt-name><p id="_1e38ca3d-eafb-aa0d-5acd-fb45a296da13" semx-id="_1e38ca3d-eafb-aa0d-5acd-fb45a296da13">Other proposals link geometric models to spatial properties or count non‑manifold features <eref type="inline" bibitemid="FlotynskiWalczak2017" citeas="[31]" id="_0e5f3f0f-4d14-c1f0-d24c-6550d0b3cd19"/><fmt-xref type="inline" target="FlotynskiWalczak2017">[31]</fmt-xref> <eref type="inline" bibitemid="zhao2024envon" citeas="[39]" id="_6583c9ea-4814-e99f-d2c8-21a7c6998c43"/><fmt-xref type="inline" target="zhao2024envon">[39]</fmt-xref>.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</clause>

<clause id="_75ff2731-e2ff-e8ae-5614-1533d2324b68" obligation="normative" semx-id="_75ff2731-e2ff-e8ae-5614-1533d2324b68">
<title id="_46a1e564-6267-84f6-9d6f-a1d1e28321df" semx-id="_f37f71d1-3e23-c148-cc62-2c27897997c6">STereoLithography (STL)</title><fmt-title depth="3" id="_71f3d320-c380-23cc-4902-15d528e0934b"><span class="fmt-caption-label">9<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>1<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>2<span class="fmt-clause-delim">.</span></span><span class="fmt-caption-delim"><tab/></span><semx element="title" source="_46a1e564-6267-84f6-9d6f-a1d1e28321df">STereoLithography (STL)</semx></fmt-title><fmt-xref-label><span class="fmt-element-name">Clause</span> 9<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>1<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>2</fmt-xref-label>
<p id="_605067ec-af89-9090-4973-c4228c32356b" semx-id="_605067ec-af89-9090-4973-c4228c32356b"><link target="http://www.3dsystems.com/quickparts/learning-center/what-is-stl-file" id="_9db776cf-c34d-470e-1991-2d07ff8b08a1">STL files</link><fmt-link target="http://www.3dsystems.com/quickparts/learning-center/what-is-stl-file">STL files</fmt-link> describe only the surface geometry of a three-dimensional object without any representation of color, texture or other common CAD model attributes. The STL format specifies both ASCII and binary representations and was invented by the Albert Consulting Group for 3D Systems in 1987. Nowadays it is mainly used for 3D printing purposes, which makes is a plausible candidate for support in GeoSPARQL 3D settings of movable objects such as in cultural heritage. Since the STL format does not contain a geospatial reference, the reference needs to be provided externally if STL files were to be used in a geospatial context. However, STL objects could be used in local CRS contexts as well.</p>
</clause>

<clause id="_58b80a75-392e-ae77-cd06-82fc8685eb80" obligation="normative" semx-id="_58b80a75-392e-ae77-cd06-82fc8685eb80">
<title id="_14bb4017-9693-8222-0e14-d93cb843ad50" semx-id="_665c84cd-5633-16ea-4d38-8eb2e4343dea">Wavefront OBJ Format (OBJ)</title><fmt-title depth="3" id="_fe8cce16-0844-53e7-3281-8559edb23bfc"><span class="fmt-caption-label">9<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>1<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>3<span class="fmt-clause-delim">.</span></span><span class="fmt-caption-delim"><tab/></span><semx element="title" source="_14bb4017-9693-8222-0e14-d93cb843ad50">Wavefront OBJ Format (OBJ)</semx></fmt-title><fmt-xref-label><span class="fmt-element-name">Clause</span> 9<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>1<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>3</fmt-xref-label>
<p id="_71c9e578-cddd-37e3-8ef6-07276fe0aba6" semx-id="_71c9e578-cddd-37e3-8ef6-07276fe0aba6">The <link target="https://paulbourke.net/dataformats/obj/" id="_4de35da9-0603-769c-31f2-f3643cd8bf1f">OBJ format</link><fmt-link target="https://paulbourke.net/dataformats/obj/">OBJ format</fmt-link> is a file format developed by Wavefront Technologies for the Advanced 3D Visualizer animation package. Its roots therefore lie in the world of 3D animation. The format represents vertex coordinates, texture coordinates, as well as vertex normals, parameter space vertices and faces of a 3D model. These are all elements which would need to be considered and supported in an upcoming GeoSPARQL 3D release.</p>
</clause>

<clause id="_6e93fe63-2ac1-dd29-06f8-6fae8f292837" obligation="normative" semx-id="_6e93fe63-2ac1-dd29-06f8-6fae8f292837">
<title id="_a2f495f0-4ecf-d4e9-e954-8eb5ecd293b1" semx-id="_f2686e29-0377-9521-87d8-a6c6f28d508a">Polygon File Format (PLY)</title><fmt-title depth="3" id="_56f04d76-233c-1d9c-e831-416ce3f6ff46"><span class="fmt-caption-label">9<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>1<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>4<span class="fmt-clause-delim">.</span></span><span class="fmt-caption-delim"><tab/></span><semx element="title" source="_a2f495f0-4ecf-d4e9-e954-8eb5ecd293b1">Polygon File Format (PLY)</semx></fmt-title><fmt-xref-label><span class="fmt-element-name">Clause</span> 9<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>1<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>4</fmt-xref-label>
<p id="_8835173c-4f5e-b1fa-adcb-9acd5a535c3e" semx-id="_8835173c-4f5e-b1fa-adcb-9acd5a535c3e">The <link target="https://paulbourke.net/dataformats/ply/" id="_ee400b1f-4c89-4ad5-eef3-ca9e1cefba90">Polygon File Format</link><fmt-link target="https://paulbourke.net/dataformats/ply/">Polygon File Format</fmt-link> is a format which was designed to store 3D data from 3D scanning environments, which makes it especially widespread in the Cultural Heritage application area. The format consists of a vertex list and a faces list with optional parameters per vertex. Like the OBJ format, supporting PLY would imply including support for vertices and faces of a 3D model and their relationships in a knowledge graph.</p>
</clause>
</clause>

<clause id="_5ba984d8-5dd7-7440-d2de-5cb2922b8fd2" obligation="normative" semx-id="_5ba984d8-5dd7-7440-d2de-5cb2922b8fd2">
<title id="_bb1943f6-b20f-7d22-ac8c-b9691e8209ff" semx-id="_0f8b34c6-0005-1544-2827-6a138d4ed28b">Geometry metadata and linking</title><fmt-title depth="2" id="_d6cf9bc6-9d6f-bd10-d288-04c4bc199ac8"><span class="fmt-caption-label">9<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>2<span class="fmt-clause-delim">.</span></span><span class="fmt-caption-delim"><tab/></span><semx element="title" source="_bb1943f6-b20f-7d22-ac8c-b9691e8209ff">Geometry metadata and linking</semx></fmt-title><fmt-xref-label><span class="fmt-element-name">Clause</span> 9<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>2</fmt-xref-label>
<p id="_f9734691-9847-c278-284c-74eb60856548" semx-id="_f9734691-9847-c278-284c-74eb60856548">The usefulness of 3D geometries in a knowledge graph will depend on the quality of the metadata which is used to describe the geometries. The reason is that we can expect users to select 3D geometries based on their attributes, since querying over greater 3D models is likely to be inefficient. Hence, we investigate already existing vocabularies for 3D geometry metadata representation.</p>

<clause id="_f1d5cc85-aacb-a0ca-6eb3-b243f478c403" obligation="normative" semx-id="_f1d5cc85-aacb-a0ca-6eb3-b243f478c403">
<title id="_bda03600-f6f2-1c30-62c5-8f809fb2e97c" semx-id="_b8571166-134f-d089-db84-be08175d5801">Geometry Metadata Ontology (GOM)</title><fmt-title depth="3" id="_f4e38c3a-40aa-0857-ed7f-2db70a40a903"><span class="fmt-caption-label">9<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>2<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>1<span class="fmt-clause-delim">.</span></span><span class="fmt-caption-delim"><tab/></span><semx element="title" source="_bda03600-f6f2-1c30-62c5-8f809fb2e97c">Geometry Metadata Ontology (GOM)</semx></fmt-title><fmt-xref-label><span class="fmt-element-name">Clause</span> 9<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>2<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>1</fmt-xref-label>
<p id="_19d93395-7e56-7958-3431-fbf1903b0533" semx-id="_19d93395-7e56-7958-3431-fbf1903b0533">The <link target="https://www.w3id.org/gom" id="_5077f0ce-e80f-29a2-2d36-04782af0021b">Geometry Metadata Ontology (GOM)</link><fmt-link target="https://www.w3id.org/gom">Geometry Metadata Ontology (GOM)</fmt-link> introduces classes for surfaces which are common in the 3D world, but uncommon for 3D geometries, for instance Meshes or NURBS surfaces. The ontology can serve as an inspiration for extensions in GeoSPARQL 3D and may or may not be fully or partly adopted in the process of standardization.</p>
</clause>

<clause id="_fee3833b-a86c-ea78-e4e4-dbc0c096d76f" obligation="normative" semx-id="_fee3833b-a86c-ea78-e4e4-dbc0c096d76f">
<title id="_333900e8-80a4-f0ab-9af7-42df875964f6" semx-id="_0acece0c-536b-c7a4-a267-fedee363e57c">Ontology for Managing Geometry (OMG)</title><fmt-title depth="3" id="_ba93f68b-824c-b64f-e85e-13172ad3d9f1"><span class="fmt-caption-label">9<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>2<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>2<span class="fmt-clause-delim">.</span></span><span class="fmt-caption-delim"><tab/></span><semx element="title" source="_333900e8-80a4-f0ab-9af7-42df875964f6">Ontology for Managing Geometry (OMG)</semx></fmt-title><fmt-xref-label><span class="fmt-element-name">Clause</span> 9<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>2<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>2</fmt-xref-label>
<p id="_58d6d123-0191-29b6-bac2-fea9c616b8f4" semx-id="_58d6d123-0191-29b6-bac2-fea9c616b8f4">The <link target="https://www.w3id.org/omg" id="_7ae90182-aebe-706f-3d7d-382ce6d78033">Ontology for Managing Geometry (OMG)</link><fmt-link target="https://www.w3id.org/omg">Ontology for Managing Geometry (OMG)</fmt-link> describes states and derivation of geometries which may be useful to the description of data in GeoSPARQL 3D. For example. an extrusion in 3D from a 2D geometry template would have a derivation relation from the 2D geometry which one might want to see modeled in GeoSPARQL 3D.</p>
</clause>

<clause id="_691344e2-53c4-2e06-46f0-e5ccb530bb57" obligation="normative" semx-id="_691344e2-53c4-2e06-46f0-e5ccb530bb57">
<title id="_c2be8a8e-0d15-52a5-384e-036961cc20bc" semx-id="_6a2999fa-36f0-7ddc-9478-daa1d77cf390">File Ontology for Geometry Formats (FOG)</title><fmt-title depth="3" id="_ceee5fb5-0d6d-6e21-5b71-708e559a6dc1"><span class="fmt-caption-label">9<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>2<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>3<span class="fmt-clause-delim">.</span></span><span class="fmt-caption-delim"><tab/></span><semx element="title" source="_c2be8a8e-0d15-52a5-384e-036961cc20bc">File Ontology for Geometry Formats (FOG)</semx></fmt-title><fmt-xref-label><span class="fmt-element-name">Clause</span> 9<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>2<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>3</fmt-xref-label>
<p id="_c900ad7e-09d4-4d76-aeee-8f0833d01f37" semx-id="_c900ad7e-09d4-4d76-aeee-8f0833d01f37">The <link target="https://w3id.org/fog#" id="_144acf47-af31-b850-fb90-1742763d2d87">File Ontology for Geometry Formats (FOG)</link><fmt-link target="https://w3id.org/fog#">File Ontology for Geometry Formats (FOG)</fmt-link> provides a bridge between ontology descriptions of geometries and data file descriptions of 3D contents. Since many 3D datasets are of considerable size, their inclusion into the knowledge graph as String literals might hinder the query performance of said knowledge graph. It therefore stands to reason whether to adopt the same or a similar method of geometry access that FOG provides for GeoSPARQL 3D.</p>
</clause>
</clause>

<clause id="_ac40ac06-6c49-e6f7-4440-4a018b602ed5" obligation="normative" semx-id="_ac40ac06-6c49-e6f7-4440-4a018b602ed5">
<title id="_203862dd-0885-3bfe-0890-90283dff7a82" semx-id="_d4f38761-5d67-4a41-a7d4-ed69cbea6865">Generic 3D representation and semantic models</title><fmt-title depth="2" id="_7d48d4d2-e5e2-7d12-9c9f-64e1719b6cb6"><span class="fmt-caption-label">9<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>3<span class="fmt-clause-delim">.</span></span><span class="fmt-caption-delim"><tab/></span><semx element="title" source="_203862dd-0885-3bfe-0890-90283dff7a82">Generic 3D representation and semantic models</semx></fmt-title><fmt-xref-label><span class="fmt-element-name">Clause</span> 9<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>3</fmt-xref-label>
<p id="_a7f97fef-d125-a230-f1fb-b8789872903b" semx-id="_a7f97fef-d125-a230-f1fb-b8789872903b">This section highlights 3D representations and in particular 3D formats which may need to be considered for a GeoSPARQL 3D extension. We describe the features of each format, their use cases and their potential challenges.</p>

<clause id="_83d2f25a-d730-bec1-9a20-8ec34f7e0481" obligation="normative" semx-id="_83d2f25a-d730-bec1-9a20-8ec34f7e0481">
<title id="_200bd13b-5342-78a4-11af-dc592e454b00" semx-id="_80fd8834-7dba-c2f8-81c0-44bbffed945d">X3D Ontology</title><fmt-title depth="3" id="_1522e4eb-affa-2f68-fa7a-5819d509ef1a"><span class="fmt-caption-label">9<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>3<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>1<span class="fmt-clause-delim">.</span></span><span class="fmt-caption-delim"><tab/></span><semx element="title" source="_200bd13b-5342-78a4-11af-dc592e454b00">X3D Ontology</semx></fmt-title><fmt-xref-label><span class="fmt-element-name">Clause</span> 9<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>3<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>1</fmt-xref-label>
<p id="_11b74ac4-42c1-2963-8564-bba2a8efef2d" semx-id="_11b74ac4-42c1-2963-8564-bba2a8efef2d">Extensible 3D (X3D) Graphics is an open standard to declaratively representing interactive 3D computer graphics for publishing, viewing, printing and archiving 3D models on the Web. X3D was eventually approved by ISO (ISO/IEC 19775-19777) and become an computer graphics industry standard. It was first proposed in 1997 and has since been subject to various updates. More recently it has been developed towards the Semantic Web, in order to gain interoperability and enable intelligent 3D operations and reasoning. Development has been hosted by the Web3D Consortium.</p>

<p id="_54e67cc7-9d5c-dd3c-bb21-f34f0f001e80" semx-id="_54e67cc7-9d5c-dd3c-bb21-f34f0f001e80">Currently in its version 4, the X3D Ontology maps the full X3D standard (X3DUOM) into OWL 2, providing classes and properties for nodes, geometry, appearance, animation, and scene graph structure, enabling query and reasoning over X3D scenes <eref type="inline" bibitemid="FlotynskiWalczak2017" citeas="[31]" id="_019494ab-0d6c-7a29-204c-04dbef677711"/><fmt-xref type="inline" target="FlotynskiWalczak2017">[31]</fmt-xref> <eref type="inline" bibitemid="georgieva2023transforming" citeas="[33]" id="_a015bdd9-1852-b072-6e8f-8164efebb86c"/><fmt-xref type="inline" target="georgieva2023transforming">[33]</fmt-xref> <xref target="Sikos2017" id="_d854a0b1-29b6-d40e-64de-08dbcc578288"/><fmt-xref target="Sikos2017">[Sikos2017]</fmt-xref> <eref type="inline" bibitemid="BrutzmanFlotynski2020" citeas="[34]" id="_4e75cb22-8d8e-26e6-aa3d-0736f256852d"/><fmt-xref type="inline" target="BrutzmanFlotynski2020">[34]</fmt-xref> <eref type="inline" bibitemid="flotynski2020recent" citeas="[35]" id="_ffe32f04-c3d9-2b1e-4549-26dadb38b741"/><fmt-xref type="inline" target="flotynski2020recent">[35]</fmt-xref> <xref target="_975906" id="_65c6ef24-c43b-436f-0a41-3a26450abc06"/><fmt-xref target="_975906">[_975906]</fmt-xref>.  X3D is regarded as a comprehensive and formally grounded 3D ontology, adding fundamental concepts and roles of the 3D modelling industry not originally covered by X3D.</p>

<p id="_57d3f953-f67a-d9c3-0a9f-f0c408c8f89f" semx-id="_57d3f953-f67a-d9c3-0a9f-f0c408c8f89f">The X3D Ontology has also been used to transform non-semantic 3D file formats into RDF with languages such as RML or XSLT <eref type="inline" bibitemid="georgieva2023transforming" citeas="[33]" id="_36a36807-f5da-797a-73f4-ef91b712fb93"/><fmt-xref type="inline" target="georgieva2023transforming">[33]</fmt-xref> <eref type="inline" bibitemid="BrutzmanFlotynski2020" citeas="[34]" id="_751986c1-7ed0-6a9a-c2c9-186f5bafd1c3"/><fmt-xref type="inline" target="BrutzmanFlotynski2020">[34]</fmt-xref>.</p>

<p id="_e14bf7e1-dd39-7a8b-0177-affc86ece7d6" semx-id="_e14bf7e1-dd39-7a8b-0177-affc86ece7d6">The X3D format <eref type="inline" bibitemid="feichtenhofer2020x3d" citeas="[6]" id="_3838d9ee-1575-d3cd-5847-37be6c0f305d"/><fmt-xref type="inline" target="feichtenhofer2020x3d">[6]</fmt-xref>, <eref type="inline" bibitemid="brutzman2010x3d" citeas="[7]" id="_f7a48f28-ee65-7033-68d1-2e59aefd944e"/><fmt-xref type="inline" target="brutzman2010x3d">[7]</fmt-xref>, X3DOM <eref type="inline" bibitemid="behr2009x3dom" citeas="[5]" id="_ac5cd239-6f6b-eac8-4501-fbefc2f9fec0"/><fmt-xref type="inline" target="behr2009x3dom">[5]</fmt-xref> and its Ontology version <eref type="inline" bibitemid="brutzman2020x3d" citeas="[4]" id="_0352fa0d-bc0f-4e16-7249-aedf7b5995e4"/><fmt-xref type="inline" target="brutzman2020x3d">[4]</fmt-xref> provide a data format and a blueprint for how 3D data is handled in computer vision approaches. X3D is an interesting format to investigate for 3D components which need to be modeled within a GeoSPARQL 3D specification. It is a likely candidate for a literal format and might be integrated using interlinking approaches to the already existing X3D vocabulary. However, since X3D was invented with computer vision approaches in mind it does not define any geospatial aspects that could be considered by a GeoSPARQL 3D implementation.</p>
</clause>

<clause id="_59592e98-f08e-c1c6-aa62-3ee58121908e" obligation="normative" semx-id="_59592e98-f08e-c1c6-aa62-3ee58121908e">
<title id="_b9a4fa7a-d0d5-9736-31c3-718b2ea65b66" semx-id="_846cdc09-140c-634f-8f5c-2c9628831206">VRML / Java3D spatial ontologies</title><fmt-title depth="3" id="_bd32d0bd-36f8-33b8-90b2-911d3856921f"><span class="fmt-caption-label">9<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>3<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>2<span class="fmt-clause-delim">.</span></span><span class="fmt-caption-delim"><tab/></span><semx element="title" source="_b9a4fa7a-d0d5-9736-31c3-718b2ea65b66">VRML / Java3D spatial ontologies</semx></fmt-title><fmt-xref-label><span class="fmt-element-name">Clause</span> 9<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>3<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>2</fmt-xref-label>
<p id="_665254ca-d88d-6ebb-fb67-07104bf1079b" semx-id="_665254ca-d88d-6ebb-fb67-07104bf1079b">Application ontologies abstracting VRML <eref type="inline" bibitemid="bell1995virtual" citeas="[37]" id="_698aed37-4423-9523-3486-e9244a07b2f4"/><fmt-xref type="inline" target="bell1995virtual">[37]</fmt-xref> &amp; Java3D node types plus a separate spatial ontology for topological, directional and metric relations <eref type="inline" bibitemid="pacheco2018creating" citeas="[41]" id="_e1cd73fe-26da-e12d-497e-63b2ad23dc08"/><fmt-xref type="inline" target="pacheco2018creating">[41]</fmt-xref>.</p>
</clause>

<clause id="_5381f98c-bd2b-22cc-0c48-afac9e1be04b" obligation="normative" semx-id="_5381f98c-bd2b-22cc-0c48-afac9e1be04b">
<title id="_b7ea7aa7-3113-4db3-c236-08acbd87584f" semx-id="_737e710e-3992-8aaf-fd89-b6b5068b3dc4">Common Shape Ontology</title><fmt-title depth="3" id="_a6f246d6-fe77-b2cf-af80-f4a2eca2934f"><span class="fmt-caption-label">9<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>3<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>3<span class="fmt-clause-delim">.</span></span><span class="fmt-caption-delim"><tab/></span><semx element="title" source="_b7ea7aa7-3113-4db3-c236-08acbd87584f">Common Shape Ontology</semx></fmt-title><fmt-xref-label><span class="fmt-element-name">Clause</span> 9<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>3<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>3</fmt-xref-label>
<p id="_729ba4d7-5be7-fd9f-d502-e3cad6f11fff" semx-id="_729ba4d7-5be7-fd9f-d502-e3cad6f11fff">– represents manifold/non‑manifold shapes, point sets, hierarchical model groups, positions, orientations, keyframe animation <eref type="inline" bibitemid="FlotynskiWalczak2017" citeas="[31]" id="_682f3ea8-12d3-7791-71a4-136512c78e5d"/><fmt-xref type="inline" target="FlotynskiWalczak2017">[31]</fmt-xref>.</p>
</clause>
</clause>

<clause id="_e0ff604b-7b10-cb6c-63d2-e316fa4020f8" obligation="normative" semx-id="_e0ff604b-7b10-cb6c-63d2-e316fa4020f8">
<title id="_4b192cb2-d539-a1d0-37d0-a95505f819ac" semx-id="_43326ac8-ac1a-e244-9e68-1935d2915bce">Domain‑specific 3D ontologies</title><fmt-title depth="2" id="_6ce3711d-03df-6e3f-f8a3-61d83d0464de"><span class="fmt-caption-label">9<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>4<span class="fmt-clause-delim">.</span></span><span class="fmt-caption-delim"><tab/></span><semx element="title" source="_4b192cb2-d539-a1d0-37d0-a95505f819ac">Domain‑specific 3D ontologies</semx></fmt-title><fmt-xref-label><span class="fmt-element-name">Clause</span> 9<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>4</fmt-xref-label>
<clause id="_765ae778-aaf0-e05b-20a1-a12e5331f60d" obligation="normative" semx-id="_765ae778-aaf0-e05b-20a1-a12e5331f60d">
<title id="_2419aa23-2ba5-8fbf-3cd3-322b8371ff52" semx-id="_245f46c1-9b19-5468-a133-0b7ec0405136">Geospatial and 3D City Model (CityGML)</title><fmt-title depth="3" id="_cefdb425-b8ba-099c-bc58-9c0d15a51263"><span class="fmt-caption-label">9<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>4<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>1<span class="fmt-clause-delim">.</span></span><span class="fmt-caption-delim"><tab/></span><semx element="title" source="_2419aa23-2ba5-8fbf-3cd3-322b8371ff52">Geospatial and 3D City Model (CityGML)</semx></fmt-title><fmt-xref-label><span class="fmt-element-name">Clause</span> 9<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>4<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>1</fmt-xref-label>
<p id="_10b44881-ae51-c2b4-ea92-6bea907da97a" semx-id="_10b44881-ae51-c2b4-ea92-6bea907da97a"><strong>CityGML and OntoCityGML</strong></p>

<p id="_a6c18fae-1263-0ad7-7a39-d353234a2872" semx-id="_a6c18fae-1263-0ad7-7a39-d353234a2872">CityGML <eref type="inline" bibitemid="kolbe2005citygml" citeas="[22]" id="_d3b4d588-3b7d-0aae-74fa-8ebd68ad12a6"/><fmt-xref type="inline" target="kolbe2005citygml">[22]</fmt-xref> is an open standardized data model for the exchange of 3D models of cities and landscapes. Since its standardization by OGC, CityGML has seen a wide variety of proposed extensions and alignments to other data standards.</p>

<p id="_2cb7e826-144b-2ad1-f84d-911a8655b17a" semx-id="_2cb7e826-144b-2ad1-f84d-911a8655b17a"><strong>CityJSON and CityRDF</strong></p>

<p id="_22007b0c-a761-efa5-5308-dcfe0bf6ac52" semx-id="_22007b0c-a761-efa5-5308-dcfe0bf6ac52"><eref type="inline" bibitemid="ijgi12090351" citeas="[23]" id="_b9f2f833-2c15-daaf-5279-db9c17b56f5c"/><fmt-xref type="inline" target="ijgi12090351">[23]</fmt-xref> proposed the extension of the CityGML and its accompanying serialization CityJSON as an ontology model. Currently, the CityRDF standardization group works towards the standardization of this CityGML data model in RDF.</p>

<p id="_39646573-38f9-77e4-3408-958dd3fc62e4" semx-id="_39646573-38f9-77e4-3408-958dd3fc62e4">The proposed CityRDF model, even though focused on CityGML and 3D building contents, contains valuable data descriptions which could be generalized in a GeoSPARQL 3D approach. One such element is the description of the appearance of a 3D-modeled building using colors or textures, so-called surface materials. In addition, CityGML includes relations of 3D primitives between each other, which could serve as an inspiration for similary-named functions or properties in the GeoSPARQL 3D ontology model.</p>

<p id="_6ce20153-3716-1a70-9294-16a5ec430dd5" semx-id="_6ce20153-3716-1a70-9294-16a5ec430dd5"><eref type="inline" bibitemid="ijgi12090351" citeas="[23]" id="_a86c830c-52ca-30ab-b96e-826abb921d63"/><fmt-xref type="inline" target="ijgi12090351">[23]</fmt-xref> describes the CityGML Ontology, which is an ontology model to formalize the CityGML standard towards a representation in OWL. It shows a workflow of identification, classification and selection of the relevant data points needed to describe 3D city models in OWL as derived from building and city models. In addition, it introduces the concept of Level of Information Need, defining the granularity of the information on the various levels of the building and the types of information retrieval — direct/indirect, i.e. information which is commonly obtained directly from a dataset or using a calculation or reasoning approach. The paper exemplifies the concept in a study case for property unit cost and indoor daylight. The contribution outlined here became the basis of the CityGML ontology which can serve as one broad application area for GeoSPARQL 3D.</p>

<p id="_861a961f-3e17-8bf8-7089-618e79c73378" semx-id="_861a961f-3e17-8bf8-7089-618e79c73378">Following on this publication <eref type="inline" bibitemid="chadzynski2023semantic" citeas="[18]" id="_6ac2333b-14d9-a25c-6a45-aff07c3a4c61"/><fmt-xref type="inline" target="chadzynski2023semantic">[18]</fmt-xref> presents a system architecture and a set of interfaces that can represent city modeling approaches using dynamic geospatial knowledge graphs. The paper proves the viability of a transition between a SQL based system for 3D building management to a SPARQL based system using a SQL2SPARQL Transformer component. It therefore proves the feasibility of employing linked open data technologies for 3D building data and in particular the CityGML model in practice.</p>

<p id="_920deb34-d394-effc-e63d-4bb02f3ed185" semx-id="_920deb34-d394-effc-e63d-4bb02f3ed185"><eref type="inline" bibitemid="kutzner2020citygml30" citeas="[21]" id="_67d5aafb-bd96-8e50-1a0c-b962284ac700"/><fmt-xref type="inline" target="kutzner2020citygml30">[21]</fmt-xref> introduces CityGML 3.0 with a newly added Core module including a new space concept and Level Of Detail concept. The given publication sets the standard especially for Level of Detail in 3D buildings, which may be considered for adoption as GeoSPARQL 3D concepts too. In addition, CityGML 3.0 proposes the addition of 3D point clouds for the representation of 3D goemetires of physical spaces and space boundaries. A mapping from IFC to CityGML 3.0 is also proposed by these authors.</p>

<p id="_1811c2bf-0b9d-6694-e189-dc4aeb4eb1a6" semx-id="_1811c2bf-0b9d-6694-e189-dc4aeb4eb1a6">CityGML 3.0 therefore represents many of the elements that a GeoSPARQL 3D ontology would need to implement on a more abstract level and can give ideas about future query functions to be implemented in the GeoSPARQL query language.</p>

<p id="_c4d48ec0-b02b-15d7-9730-bf30bc8d49a2" semx-id="_c4d48ec0-b02b-15d7-9730-bf30bc8d49a2">In addition, a master thesis <eref type="inline" bibitemid="hansson2024citygml" citeas="[20]" id="_270ddbca-018b-d854-60fc-2e721bdafb05"/><fmt-xref type="inline" target="hansson2024citygml">[20]</fmt-xref> proved the feasibility of not only converting CityGML to a knowledge graph representation, but also its application in triple stores such as Ontop or 3DCityDB. The mapping of CityGML to the knowledge graph was tested using a set of SPARQL queries retrieving different components of building parts. While the thesis could construct a knowledge graph representation from CityGML, it points out that the incorporation of sptaial operations is left to future work and could provide a possible field for GeoSPARQL 3D adoption.</p>

<p id="_fd8273eb-c014-ad6d-7cc9-db3c050cef7d" semx-id="_fd8273eb-c014-ad6d-7cc9-db3c050cef7d">Finally, <eref type="inline" bibitemid="CHADZYNSKI2021100106" citeas="[24]" id="_1e432d7e-42ec-3e12-db03-1807d36bbfd8"/><fmt-xref type="inline" target="CHADZYNSKI2021100106">[24]</fmt-xref> proposed OntoCityGML, an extension go the CityGML ontolog <link target="https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2666546821000574?via%3Dihub" id="_0766568d-28e0-6214-48af-d8ab39f0c549"/><fmt-link target="https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2666546821000574?via%3Dihub"/> Semantic 3D City Database — An enabler for a dynamic geospatial knowledge graph</p>

<p id="_2669fee3-da69-f119-507c-563a3575e00d" semx-id="_2669fee3-da69-f119-507c-563a3575e00d">Other details and history of Onto CityGML.</p>

<p id="_403d67c2-4e48-ad2f-e8e3-798bf253fd0a" semx-id="_403d67c2-4e48-ad2f-e8e3-798bf253fd0a">CityRDF working group <link target="https://github.com/ogcincubator/cityrdf" id="_f167cd0b-9ff6-5344-1f60-92bb53025645"/><fmt-link target="https://github.com/ogcincubator/cityrdf"/></p>
</clause>

<clause id="_9c06f219-dd34-0a1f-302d-c843347dad77" obligation="normative" semx-id="_9c06f219-dd34-0a1f-302d-c843347dad77">
<title id="_ccf1abed-fb29-e7c5-6b02-f35168bb2721" semx-id="_ed4f2c44-2b14-e6bb-9898-32d0326cb972">Cultural Heritage</title><fmt-title depth="3" id="_41662cd1-0eab-e695-d8ca-2595d5b75d62"><span class="fmt-caption-label">9<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>4<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>2<span class="fmt-clause-delim">.</span></span><span class="fmt-caption-delim"><tab/></span><semx element="title" source="_ccf1abed-fb29-e7c5-6b02-f35168bb2721">Cultural Heritage</semx></fmt-title><fmt-xref-label><span class="fmt-element-name">Clause</span> 9<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>4<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>2</fmt-xref-label>
<p id="_f9c56628-565f-904a-deac-224c3ee41e3b" semx-id="_f9c56628-565f-904a-deac-224c3ee41e3b">In the research domain of cultural heritage, 3D models of either cultural heritage artifacts (possibly georeferenced), 3D models of archaeological sites or simply 3D models of ancient buildings are becoming increasinly common.</p>

<p id="_75cabe6f-d280-4ee8-efbe-cca0bc9f0f44" semx-id="_75cabe6f-d280-4ee8-efbe-cca0bc9f0f44">Building-conservation ontology for 3D annotations. The conservation and restoration of historical monuments require the integration of information from many different disciplines and techniques. This study presents a pipeline that combines semantic, spatial, and morphological data into one 3D information model. The approach was tested on Saint Maurice Church in Caromb, France, allowing conservation experts to better visualize, correlate, and retrieve information about materials, damage, and other conservation observations. <eref type="inline" bibitemid="messaoudi2018heritage" citeas="[40]" id="_20ff4e41-a7ef-6eb9-d9d1-1846f99ac272"/><fmt-xref type="inline" target="messaoudi2018heritage">[40]</fmt-xref>;</p>

<p id="_8028c343-b4d7-caf2-7ce5-4b6343f363c6" semx-id="_8028c343-b4d7-caf2-7ce5-4b6343f363c6">CIDOC CRM–based models for 3D CH objects and provenance. This paper proposes an ontology-based approach for managing and using 3D content without requiring domain experts to have programming or 3D modelling skills. The actual 3D elements can be created by graphic designers and then linked to domain-specific concepts by developers. Ontologies and automated reasoning can subsequently determine which 3D elements are relevant based on the specified domain information, while semantic queries allow the content to be explored and analysed. The focus is therefore on connecting 3D graphics with domain knowledge rather than on creating the 3D models themselves. <eref type="inline" bibitemid="walczak2019inference" citeas="[42]" id="_ac105b9a-7a78-ef62-d37b-a3037486732f"/><fmt-xref type="inline" target="walczak2019inference">[42]</fmt-xref> <eref type="inline" bibitemid="amico2106ontological" citeas="[43]" id="_ca3b00fc-f154-2261-286b-5449fdf3373b"/><fmt-xref type="inline" target="amico2106ontological">[43]</fmt-xref> <eref type="inline" bibitemid="гончар2021ontological" citeas="[44]" id="_1c968e5d-94ab-25f1-dd12-7b723246f707"/><fmt-xref type="inline" target="гончар2021ontological">[44]</fmt-xref>.</p>

<p id="_150eff84-261f-6211-7ddd-7e0085b48dd4" semx-id="_150eff84-261f-6211-7ddd-7e0085b48dd4"><strong>Use Cases</strong></p>

<p id="_b43ddf5d-de28-76b5-629d-acda0d2d51ea" semx-id="_b43ddf5d-de28-76b5-629d-acda0d2d51ea">Use Cases in the Cultural Heritage domain include, but are not limited to, the following main interests:</p>

<p id="_86aa7092-f0ee-2a7a-4115-3ccc6316dff5" semx-id="_86aa7092-f0ee-2a7a-4115-3ccc6316dff5"><strong>Visualization:</strong> The visualization of 3D models for the presentation of such 3D models, for example in the context of a museum. 3D models may be styled with a particular set of textures or modeled with a specific set of colours to highlight certain relevant aspects. The visualization of 3D models is currently standardized by the IIIF 3D working group <eref type="inline" bibitemid="Haynes2023iiif3d" citeas="[13]" id="_3e249edd-d15c-c96d-d2c1-40a602c0e9bf"/><fmt-xref type="inline" target="Haynes2023iiif3d">[13]</fmt-xref>, which aims to create viewing parameter descriptions that 3D viewers may implement, similar to the specifications of IIIF 2D for images.</p>

<p id="_dd823c47-2693-8ac5-c8a7-64f38aacb021" semx-id="_dd823c47-2693-8ac5-c8a7-64f38aacb021"><strong>Object Annotation:</strong> 3D models are seen as the subject of a research question in absence of the original artifact for political, practical or other restrictive reasons. Out of all known methods of the representation of cultural heritage artifacts, 3D models provide the most detail when being delivered as a digital artifact and are therefore very often preferred in a research context. Researchers mark noteworthy aspects of the cultural artifact as 3D annotations  <eref type="inline" bibitemid="bestpractices3d" citeas="[11]" id="_c93d21bc-4a46-27bd-005c-fe42fd2de06c"/><fmt-xref type="inline" target="bestpractices3d">[11]</fmt-xref> which may include surface descriptions, volumes of the 3D model or 3D models which are created and placed adjacent to the to-be-annotated 3D model <eref type="inline" bibitemid="mara2024annotation" citeas="[14]" id="_0c314ccd-737e-9880-4881-07560f8aaf1d"/><fmt-xref type="inline" target="mara2024annotation">[14]</fmt-xref>.</p>

<p id="_e20d2277-21fd-7b9c-98bc-4e2d9c5178f5" semx-id="_e20d2277-21fd-7b9c-98bc-4e2d9c5178f5"><strong>Relation of Objects:</strong> Objects of a specific collections always exist in a spatio-temporal context. It is important to relate these representations via meaningful relations, so that relevant objects of a collection can be retrieved more easily</p>

<p id="_484ae74e-60aa-aa4b-5518-f624f5107366" semx-id="_484ae74e-60aa-aa4b-5518-f624f5107366"><strong>AI Applications in Cultural Heritage:</strong> Usage for annotated areas on 3D models or their derivations for machine learning classifications <eref type="inline" bibitemid="Stotzner_2023_ICCV" citeas="[12]" id="_0e6a1d13-2f84-f822-abcf-97ef7c448194"/><fmt-xref type="inline" target="Stotzner_2023_ICCV">[12]</fmt-xref> <eref bibitemid="_0.2312_gch.20231157" citeas="" id="_1e38146a-63c0-5a08-93c1-f3930cd1732e"/><fmt-xref target="_0.2312_gch.20231157">10.2312/gch.20231157</fmt-xref></p>

<p id="_105e16a3-56d5-68af-4dc0-b1a270583c60" semx-id="_105e16a3-56d5-68af-4dc0-b1a270583c60"><strong>Knowledge Graphs as Metadata descriptions:</strong> With the advent of more 3D models being published, the relevance of their creation parameters <eref type="inline" bibitemid="homburgheritagescience2021" citeas="[15]" id="_ee1f0ecd-8712-3453-c5c9-cd2aed282ccb"/><fmt-xref type="inline" target="homburgheritagescience2021">[15]</fmt-xref>, their contents and their object metadata increases for the usecases of filtering them and also for the possibly automated selection of suitable cultural heritage metadata for e.g. machine learning classifications. Currently, many metadata standards fulfil parts of the description chain and a unified vocabulary to described data types seems to be missing.</p>

<p id="_0d57a2f8-e9e8-98c2-c038-543e07f417c0" semx-id="_0d57a2f8-e9e8-98c2-c038-543e07f417c0">Research applications making use of 3D models in Cultural Heritage</p>

<p id="_c6d363fa-0211-2e82-7f80-e7ae96fc11d1" semx-id="_c6d363fa-0211-2e82-7f80-e7ae96fc11d1">This section discusses research projects with 3D contents based on the technologies they use as elaborated in the previous section.</p>

<clause id="_d46026bf-3573-3c82-0699-fff9d93e931e" obligation="normative" semx-id="_d46026bf-3573-3c82-0699-fff9d93e931e">
<title id="_a5ac1cf5-b369-9cde-15f5-1a9f5f499bb6" semx-id="_a73a1574-e6c6-144c-a325-5e5863d98106">3D models of cuneiform tablets</title><fmt-title depth="4" id="_d6f80580-9d6e-27bd-8dde-20fa2f247aa1"><span class="fmt-caption-label">9<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>4<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>2<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>1<span class="fmt-clause-delim">.</span></span><span class="fmt-caption-delim"><tab/></span><semx element="title" source="_a5ac1cf5-b369-9cde-15f5-1a9f5f499bb6">3D models of cuneiform tablets</semx></fmt-title><fmt-xref-label><span class="fmt-element-name">Clause</span> 9<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>4<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>2<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>1</fmt-xref-label>
<p id="_eefbe329-6814-405c-8d6a-689cc0a4a6e6" semx-id="_eefbe329-6814-405c-8d6a-689cc0a4a6e6">Cuneiform tablets from ancient Iran provide an interesting research area, since they combine a 3D artifact with textual imprints that are of interest to a variety of research communities including Assyriologsts, Digital Humanists, Computational Linguists and last but not least Computer Scientists. The creation of 3D models of cuneiform tablets provides the best accessibility to the specificities of the original artifact in its absence and 3D scans have been used by computer scientists as the basis for certain machine learning application tasks, even though to this day only as a provision for 2D renderings of their surfaces. Interests of the research community include the description of interesting features such as cuneiform signs on cuneiform tablet surfaces and their connection to other text contents or other cuneiform artifacts. Knowledge graphs provide machine learning approaches  <eref type="inline" bibitemid="Stotzner_2023_ICCV" citeas="[12]" id="_9e1c2650-e76d-e7ae-fe80-8d09e1bc2b93"/><fmt-xref type="inline" target="Stotzner_2023_ICCV">[12]</fmt-xref> <eref type="inline" bibitemid="stotzner2023r" citeas="[2]" id="_0dd8bfb9-2fb9-f1a9-0f27-966cbd2ec02d"/><fmt-xref type="inline" target="stotzner2023r">[2]</fmt-xref> with unique opportunities to connect different kinds of data using a unified data format.</p>

<p id="_ef9416a4-581a-5716-bdff-c8d760794981" semx-id="_ef9416a4-581a-5716-bdff-c8d760794981">To describe 3D meshes, several vocabularies have been developed in the context of the cuneiform studies project:</p>

<ul id="_2fab5627-3fff-4322-4aec-c125d690cae3" semx-id="_2fab5627-3fff-4322-4aec-c125d690cae3"><li id="_8d80f64e-32a9-a56f-5bf6-7aa6f13c17be"><fmt-name id="_ae992e9e-c677-e83a-7880-3972000571e4">—</fmt-name><p id="_02435646-d16f-d74e-91c9-48eda952524d" semx-id="_02435646-d16f-d74e-91c9-48eda952524d"><link target="https://situx.github.io/mesh-sparql/" id="_6cc69276-bd84-4a7b-1128-9c57665b153d">MeshSPARQL</link><fmt-link target="https://situx.github.io/mesh-sparql/">MeshSPARQL</fmt-link>: A vocabulary to describe essential mesh elements</p>
</li>
<li id="_d607945d-2522-6d36-9e62-c14b32addaf3"><fmt-name id="_e1ff0014-b143-1b18-874f-de687758b331">—</fmt-name><p id="_111edc20-97f6-3243-cde6-e202311b7c39" semx-id="_111edc20-97f6-3243-cde6-e202311b7c39"><link target="https://www.gigamesh.eu/ont/" id="_7e911226-ed69-1f4d-0523-5f893591f4e6">Gigamesh Metadata Vocabulary</link><fmt-link target="https://www.gigamesh.eu/ont/">Gigamesh Metadata Vocabulary</fmt-link>: A vocabulary which describes metadata of a 3D model. The metadata can be generated using the Gigamesh Software Framework</p>
</li>
<li id="_fb502446-fb50-611d-217e-adb384f55022"><fmt-name id="_dbcb7def-fcea-03ec-2061-b93ce5661be4">—</fmt-name><p id="_a21f7d48-9135-e8eb-222b-bb14e8d7e10d" semx-id="_a21f7d48-9135-e8eb-222b-bb14e8d7e10d"><link target="https://mainzed.pages.gitlab.rlp.net/homepages/mainzedmetadata/" id="_bfab5c6a-7e6a-ef21-f439-63e02cc2f062">3DCAP Vocabularies</link><fmt-link target="https://mainzed.pages.gitlab.rlp.net/homepages/mainzedmetadata/">3DCAP Vocabularies</fmt-link>: An ontology model to describe the creation of a 3D model. It has been applied to different scanning softwares</p>
</li>
</ul>
</clause>

<clause id="_2ace6dc4-63b2-68c3-f2df-076648c31585" obligation="normative" semx-id="_2ace6dc4-63b2-68c3-f2df-076648c31585">
<title id="_0b674be7-ddbc-cd50-fd14-307e77f7f471" semx-id="_8f3cc101-423b-3900-331e-c27ae4a4b8f9">CARARE Metadata Schema</title><fmt-title depth="4" id="_0e227d37-581d-3ca9-3768-6c689f4b6209"><span class="fmt-caption-label">9<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>4<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>2<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>2<span class="fmt-clause-delim">.</span></span><span class="fmt-caption-delim"><tab/></span><semx element="title" source="_0b674be7-ddbc-cd50-fd14-307e77f7f471">CARARE Metadata Schema</semx></fmt-title><fmt-xref-label><span class="fmt-element-name">Clause</span> 9<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>4<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>2<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>2</fmt-xref-label>
<p id="_cd3edcbc-f443-341a-dd18-e364fd718b0f" semx-id="_cd3edcbc-f443-341a-dd18-e364fd718b0f">The CARARE metadata schema <eref type="inline" bibitemid="d2013carare" citeas="[9]" id="_ea811ba8-7d65-c309-5a49-7219d7613359"/><fmt-xref type="inline" target="d2013carare">[9]</fmt-xref> is a harvesting schema intended for delivering metadata about an organisation’s online collections, heritage assets and their digital resources. The strength of the schema lies with its ability to support the full range of descriptive information about monuments, building, landscape areas and their representations. The CARARE metadata schema builds on existing standards and best practice from a number of different countries in Europe and the rest of the world.</p>
</clause>

<clause id="_e7bc8ba1-ca56-4db3-ac16-f28f25a1fe8c" obligation="normative" semx-id="_e7bc8ba1-ca56-4db3-ac16-f28f25a1fe8c">
<title id="_604f5282-ba37-f57c-8cac-770898d82d23" semx-id="_3b118455-6b0d-c3bb-7ab4-ef935b5e4b10">IIIF 3D</title><fmt-title depth="4" id="_311ec6ef-6346-7285-88d2-eb64ce79cecf"><span class="fmt-caption-label">9<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>4<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>2<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>3<span class="fmt-clause-delim">.</span></span><span class="fmt-caption-delim"><tab/></span><semx element="title" source="_604f5282-ba37-f57c-8cac-770898d82d23">IIIF 3D</semx></fmt-title><fmt-xref-label><span class="fmt-element-name">Clause</span> 9<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>4<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>2<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>3</fmt-xref-label>
<p id="_d6e9d55d-df4d-a8a7-0f20-1d59a12b2ab5" semx-id="_d6e9d55d-df4d-a8a7-0f20-1d59a12b2ab5">The International Image Interoperability Framework (IIIF) standard <eref type="inline" bibitemid="snydman2015international" citeas="[3]" id="_23f294c2-fe0c-6e8a-df9c-333498099e08"/><fmt-xref type="inline" target="snydman2015international">[3]</fmt-xref> is currently developing a <link target="https://iiif.io/community/groups/3d/tsg/" id="_930b371b-2ec1-95c7-495d-24816e29aadf">specification</link><fmt-link target="https://iiif.io/community/groups/3d/tsg/">specification</fmt-link> for the standardization of 3D viewers. The viewer specification includes the visualization of 3D models including lighting positions, a scene graph and annotations in the form of additional 3D objects added to a 3D scene. A GeoSPARQL 3D standard might need to be compatible with, or include a compatibility statement of, converting 3D objects encoded in GeoSPARQL to the IIIF viewer specification. A prototype implementation might be necessary to comply with this specification.</p>
</clause>
</clause>

<clause id="_872f32b4-1fa7-4485-f828-d4d539e57882" obligation="normative" semx-id="_872f32b4-1fa7-4485-f828-d4d539e57882">
<title id="_3d61092e-e411-03df-7c81-1bd7b1f61a69" semx-id="_64811ded-7bc2-bf76-4405-76b1f61c31b6">ChgOnto</title><fmt-title depth="3" id="_4f32b951-010d-f813-c660-76e1f752208a"><span class="fmt-caption-label">9<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>4<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>3<span class="fmt-clause-delim">.</span></span><span class="fmt-caption-delim"><tab/></span><semx element="title" source="_3d61092e-e411-03df-7c81-1bd7b1f61a69">ChgOnto</semx></fmt-title><fmt-xref-label><span class="fmt-element-name">Clause</span> 9<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>4<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>3</fmt-xref-label>
<p id="_e675eaaf-bb61-f5f6-b9ca-437944bb628a" semx-id="_e675eaaf-bb61-f5f6-b9ca-437944bb628a">Grottoes &amp; heritage ontology. This paper focuses on enhancing 3D models of Chinese grottoes by adding semantic information. It develops a knowledge graph that links 3D elements—such as caves, Buddha statues, walls, and decorations—to their properties and spatial relationships, making the 3D models more meaningful, understandable, and useful for cultural heritage documentation and analysis.for grotto components, spatial relations, with GeoSPARQL + CIDOC CRM <eref type="inline" bibitemid="yang2023knowledge" citeas="[45]" id="_321ff89a-aab4-cef4-21e6-fb38bb877405"/><fmt-xref type="inline" target="yang2023knowledge">[45]</fmt-xref>.</p>
</clause>

<clause id="_97d04bec-b551-cc41-5e3f-c139b38673c1" obligation="normative" semx-id="_97d04bec-b551-cc41-5e3f-c139b38673c1">
<title id="_2210d7a1-0c53-d41d-68f1-ef746b6b6da3" semx-id="_28725066-f818-fe31-f930-895653f746c9">OntPreHer3D</title><fmt-title depth="3" id="_39847be7-a3e2-0682-0bb8-b4de5e748b9d"><span class="fmt-caption-label">9<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>4<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>4<span class="fmt-clause-delim">.</span></span><span class="fmt-caption-delim"><tab/></span><semx element="title" source="_2210d7a1-0c53-d41d-68f1-ef746b6b6da3">OntPreHer3D</semx></fmt-title><fmt-xref-label><span class="fmt-element-name">Clause</span> 9<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>4<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>4</fmt-xref-label>
<p id="_8d73d732-21ee-069f-16c7-adc00f2851cd" semx-id="_8d73d732-21ee-069f-16c7-adc00f2851cd">OntPreHer3D is an extension of the CIDOC CRM ontology <eref type="inline" bibitemid="doerr2007cidoc" citeas="[53]" id="_f6529a51-3ab0-d26f-4fe3-7ad45de08424"/><fmt-xref type="inline" target="doerr2007cidoc">[53]</fmt-xref>. It is designed to support the FAIR preservation and documentation of 3D cultural heritage models. The ontology captures relationships between 3D models, their components, and real-world counterparts, while also documenting the reasoning, uncertainties, and decisions behind hypothetical reconstructions. A case study of the Speyer Synagogue demonstrates how differences in modelling processes can affect final 3D models, highlighting the importance of detailed documentation for the long-term preservation, reproducibility, and reuse of digital cultural heritage.  <link target="https://peercommunityjournal.org/articles/10.24072/pcjournal.608/" id="_56ae95f3-b987-1c9d-5039-dcaa65991a99">OntPreHer3D: Ontology for Preservation of Cultural Heritage 3D Models</link><fmt-link target="https://peercommunityjournal.org/articles/10.24072/pcjournal.608/">OntPreHer3D: Ontology for Preservation of Cultural Heritage 3D Models</fmt-link></p>
</clause>

<clause id="_c1dce9fa-3cfd-d347-fb3f-369ae0f0ffbc" obligation="normative" semx-id="_c1dce9fa-3cfd-d347-fb3f-369ae0f0ffbc">
<title id="_0f1b99e7-cd3e-ec69-4959-102d7226ac4f" semx-id="_8e4284ce-a892-7fa9-74ab-dcaff94b3d5c">BIM and Buildings</title><fmt-title depth="3" id="_582c4182-5417-0c4c-0288-dcf58f99f2ee"><span class="fmt-caption-label">9<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>4<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>5<span class="fmt-clause-delim">.</span></span><span class="fmt-caption-delim"><tab/></span><semx element="title" source="_0f1b99e7-cd3e-ec69-4959-102d7226ac4f">BIM and Buildings</semx></fmt-title><fmt-xref-label><span class="fmt-element-name">Clause</span> 9<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>4<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>5</fmt-xref-label>
<clause id="_0a64fc0c-e46e-e1b5-10c8-649da0a56c72" obligation="normative" semx-id="_0a64fc0c-e46e-e1b5-10c8-649da0a56c72">
<title id="_bb34cca9-8b8b-eabf-3914-6e666fa895eb" semx-id="_fccccbbb-5a43-db58-fac6-4615134f14bc">Industry Foundation Classes (IFC)</title><fmt-title depth="4" id="_492e03fe-a796-5067-0b73-c959f923d885"><span class="fmt-caption-label">9<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>4<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>5<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>1<span class="fmt-clause-delim">.</span></span><span class="fmt-caption-delim"><tab/></span><semx element="title" source="_bb34cca9-8b8b-eabf-3914-6e666fa895eb">Industry Foundation Classes (IFC)</semx></fmt-title><fmt-xref-label><span class="fmt-element-name">Clause</span> 9<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>4<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>5<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>1</fmt-xref-label>
<p id="_81eb77da-55a8-e333-8977-c2acef1b3830" semx-id="_81eb77da-55a8-e333-8977-c2acef1b3830">BIM is a paradigm in which object‐model definitions — with machine‑interpretable semantics — are exchanged, rather than relying on CAD drawings that convey only graphical semantics. The predominant open exchange standard is Industry Foundation Classes (IFC) <eref type="inline" bibitemid="van2021future" citeas="[55]" id="_3aecc07c-1120-330c-a79c-01e5eba3cdb6"/><fmt-xref type="inline" target="van2021future">[55]</fmt-xref>. <strong>IFC is unique in the sense that it allows expressing both geometrical as well as non-geometrical semantics.</strong></p>

<p id="_957e3437-28b8-431f-03bc-5e3215f8a6c4" semx-id="_957e3437-28b8-431f-03bc-5e3215f8a6c4"><strong>Non-geometrical: taxonomy of objects (building elements, relational constructs, scheduling information) and related properties</strong></p>

<p id="_27b67ef8-1a03-4ef9-3685-f95854e92fd6" semx-id="_27b67ef8-1a03-4ef9-3685-f95854e92fd6"><strong>Geometrical: appromximately 250 constructs related to geometry; even standards with only a geometric focus such as geosparql or collada do not reach that breadth in definitions</strong></p>

<p id="_75531a80-56f5-c78c-da73-db26a55d054d" semx-id="_75531a80-56f5-c78c-da73-db26a55d054d">IFC has its idiosyncrasies and challenges, but is <strong>unrivaled in terms of market adoption in a highly fragmented industry</strong>.</p>

<clause id="_27eb706e-73f2-af5b-c15d-b05e999a71dd" obligation="normative" semx-id="_27eb706e-73f2-af5b-c15d-b05e999a71dd">
<title id="_8c114d2f-0918-179d-58ec-6fad84481d02" semx-id="_647f1d72-fcd3-285c-ba62-e4884c89954d">Product model</title><fmt-title depth="5" id="_295b7612-fb53-5286-5e3b-73a9b377d8e5"><span class="fmt-caption-label">9<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>4<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>5<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>1<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>1<span class="fmt-clause-delim">.</span></span><span class="fmt-caption-delim"><tab/></span><semx element="title" source="_8c114d2f-0918-179d-58ec-6fad84481d02">Product model</semx></fmt-title><fmt-xref-label><span class="fmt-element-name">Clause</span> 9<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>4<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>5<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>1<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>1</fmt-xref-label>
<p id="_fdb8c0c7-cd14-9004-6520-4882dce50e0f" semx-id="_fdb8c0c7-cd14-9004-6520-4882dce50e0f">In IFC, a construction work is decomposed into a set of products. These products can have <strong>multiple representations</strong>. For example, a wall can be described as a solid body as well as a two-dimensional axis. These representations facilitate different views on the same data: an editable line segment or an easily visualized volume. The Object-relational nature of the IFC EXPRESS schema allows intricate relationships such as a representation context that communicates additional intent for the representation or presentation styles that can be granularly assigned to individual faces.</p>

<p id="_fc9bbef5-85ff-ed2f-0d7e-dcaaf2e2c238" semx-id="_fc9bbef5-85ff-ed2f-0d7e-dcaaf2e2c238">At the same time, such a product separates the <strong>placement</strong> (an hierarchical transformation) from the actual geometry definition. The consequence of this is that in spite of its object-relational nature, IFC product representations cannot be used for building-level topological relationships between solids, because even if two solids are touching in 3D, the fact the the placement is externalized out of the geometry definition (or the fact that faces are constructed procedurally and do not exist explicitly), means that the two faces cannot be opposite oriented twins. As such, relational geometric constructs such as space boundaries are provided as additional supplementary geometries. (Identifying faces in procedural geometries is also challenging (toplogical naming problem), so using geometric semantic overlays to annotate potentially procedural geometries makes sense)</p>

<p id="_49fd5dbf-a3e6-80eb-4d30-2bb16dcbc81a" semx-id="_49fd5dbf-a3e6-80eb-4d30-2bb16dcbc81a">In principle, the IFC schema has been designed in a modular fashion with independent modules for, for example, geometry, materials and meta-data. However in other cases, <strong>semantics and geometry are intertwined</strong> such as tapered extrusions (lofts) where the begin and end profile of a duct carry important semantics.</p>

<p id="_48a521f2-2f62-c8b4-e31f-2d131f775f9e" semx-id="_48a521f2-2f62-c8b4-e31f-2d131f775f9e">IFC also allows for <strong>decomposition</strong>, where a whole is aggregated into multiple parts for richer semantics. This allows for example to connect materials and meta-data to the frame and the glazing separately, while still being able to identify the aggregate as a single window. This is not used as frequently, partially due to inability to efficiently instantiate such aggregates as geometry instances.</p>
</clause>

<clause id="_fafd8186-b5e4-7c58-57f1-c8071037c1a0" obligation="normative" semx-id="_fafd8186-b5e4-7c58-57f1-c8071037c1a0">
<title id="_18907d13-4b20-dcbd-75dc-c331e2e8f15a" semx-id="_9f025cad-c256-b7cc-48ae-dcab7888c277">Evolving views on geometry</title><fmt-title depth="5" id="_ad318d5a-779e-a6b2-7644-f40109445113"><span class="fmt-caption-label">9<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>4<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>5<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>1<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>2<span class="fmt-clause-delim">.</span></span><span class="fmt-caption-delim"><tab/></span><semx element="title" source="_18907d13-4b20-dcbd-75dc-c331e2e8f15a">Evolving views on geometry</semx></fmt-title><fmt-xref-label><span class="fmt-element-name">Clause</span> 9<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>4<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>5<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>1<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>2</fmt-xref-label>
<p id="_b5b594cc-f739-2e75-ddae-417943d1fbe2" semx-id="_b5b594cc-f739-2e75-ddae-417943d1fbe2">IFC is heavily influenced by the ISO 10303 (STEP) <eref type="inline" bibitemid="pratt2001introduction" citeas="[57]" id="_77b80611-8bcc-c471-1ebc-c52ff44e1a31"/><fmt-xref type="inline" target="pratt2001introduction">[57]</fmt-xref> family of standards, but over time adopted its own geometric paradigms:</p>

<ul id="_1f20c54d-a022-857e-c71b-f364daddcb2c" semx-id="_1f20c54d-a022-857e-c71b-f364daddcb2c"><li id="_71a18d61-ce8e-41cd-2cc6-0999864b37eb"><fmt-name id="_2588be4e-b7b5-4dad-2973-1ecf537472b3">—</fmt-name><p id="_b6fc174a-96d8-a896-8f0b-6f7190eab8ad" semx-id="_b6fc174a-96d8-a896-8f0b-6f7190eab8ad">Procedural geometry and boolean operations became less prominent with the adoption of ReferenceView in IFC4. Tessellated geometry definitions were added for more compact exchange.</p>
</li>
<li id="_86437d7a-bf49-7f62-3668-5a63183fa026"><fmt-name id="_d608b57d-7968-2ba7-8b9e-9bae3c8de2fd">—</fmt-name><p id="_7dd70655-fee0-f126-8f5e-7b171aa0b68f" semx-id="_7dd70655-fee0-f126-8f5e-7b171aa0b68f"><strong>Infrastructure definitions</strong> were added with precise mathematical transition curves and a composition of a horizontal, vertical and cant (inclination) profile.</p>
</li>
<li id="_022b3d51-52d3-3a85-2a10-83c64d50874e"><fmt-name id="_91d7f568-49f7-1e4b-4599-e4540574d400">—</fmt-name><p id="_06f4c383-7719-ef13-bf6e-bcc98e544f42" semx-id="_06f4c383-7719-ef13-bf6e-bcc98e544f42">IFC5 <eref type="inline" bibitemid="wang2026ifc5" citeas="[56]" id="_fb80f54b-35ea-9c91-f076-d7ca7d7afa95"/><fmt-xref type="inline" target="wang2026ifc5">[56]</fmt-xref> with an <strong>explicit</strong> (most likely triangulated) geometry schema at the core, with semantic overlays to encode the same procedural semantics as a non-mandatory or use-case specific layer. Heavily inspired by USD with layer-based composition for collaborative exchange.</p>
</li>
</ul>

<p id="_2252286b-33a3-ca1a-7fea-79e63e625496" semx-id="_2252286b-33a3-ca1a-7fea-79e63e625496">Especially the handling of <strong>tolerances</strong> means that the standard cannot effectively prescribe a consistent outcome in all cases. Tolerances are needed for BRep model with non-linear underlying geometry and/or fixed precision coordinate values, e.g higher degree nurbs curves are typically intersected with numerical approximation, so a vertex that connects two of such curves needs to have seen as a sphere with the local tolerance as its radius. This tolerance is also applied to boolean operations: an subtraction volume can be slightly inwards of the first operand but is still expected to pierce through the volume and increase surface genus. This contrasts with the desire of using IFC as a legal basis in contracts. NB Tolerances stand in the way of using existing approaches for SFA geometry predicates such as PostGIS+SFCGAL which is based on arbitrary precision boolean logic as implemented in CGAL without tolerances.</p>
</clause>

<clause id="_fe76caa4-d4ab-7bf0-dee6-02afbd4b4cca" obligation="normative" semx-id="_fe76caa4-d4ab-7bf0-dee6-02afbd4b4cca">
<title id="_2fe0aff9-45c2-c28d-68ec-ca8686fc4195" semx-id="_d9446379-e6b4-8e37-e033-12938865c661">Use cases</title><fmt-title depth="5" id="_fbfa8d59-c3b7-8b4e-2348-70f74e94fdca"><span class="fmt-caption-label">9<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>4<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>5<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>1<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>3<span class="fmt-clause-delim">.</span></span><span class="fmt-caption-delim"><tab/></span><semx element="title" source="_2fe0aff9-45c2-c28d-68ec-ca8686fc4195">Use cases</semx></fmt-title><fmt-xref-label><span class="fmt-element-name">Clause</span> 9<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>4<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>5<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>1<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>3</fmt-xref-label>
<p id="_813ea182-009b-f32a-3fc3-b575e8451aa3" semx-id="_813ea182-009b-f32a-3fc3-b575e8451aa3">The most successful use case on BIM data is <strong>coordination and visualization</strong> where multiple aspect models are geometrically overlaid in order to find issues, which are then communicated to the authoring software where they are addressed. This approach works, because it respects that individual disciplines all have their own specialistic software.</p>

<p id="_5fcba1ec-f987-6a4c-5a77-dd4b8d43b362" semx-id="_5fcba1ec-f987-6a4c-5a77-dd4b8d43b362"><strong>Design to design</strong> workflows are much harder to realize, although some Model View Definitions have been developed on top of IFC that enable the transfer of design intent in specific and constrained scenarios, such as precast concrete and structural steel.</p>

<p id="_410ba142-810e-42da-c9cf-2659720e4bb7" semx-id="_410ba142-810e-42da-c9cf-2659720e4bb7"><strong>Long-term preservation</strong> of building information is difficult because of the fact that IFC models are difficult to mutate, because they are so explicit and don’t contain the vendor-specific design intelligence. Therefore native software cannot always re-import IFC models, but also the native models degrade over time because of the need to migrate to newer editions of the software. Software that can directly operate on IFC to make modifications is still experimental.</p>

<p id="_4dc3aa8e-b290-0212-7e15-c1b8320d1808" semx-id="_4dc3aa8e-b290-0212-7e15-c1b8320d1808"><strong>BIM-GIS integration</strong> is challenging because it requires familiarity with both domains on where to draw the line between euclidean and non-euclidean geometries and acceptable error metrics.</p>

<p id="_723a3718-0168-0337-b371-bcbfb57c8fcb" semx-id="_723a3718-0168-0337-b371-bcbfb57c8fcb"><strong>Simulations</strong> on IFC building models are often challenging because the ‘bag of individual elements’ does not provide a good foundation higher order topological representations required for flow-of-energy type of simulations. For e.g thermal simulation a topological view of space boundaries is required. They have been added as secondary set of ternary relationships, but usage of more specific-purpose and simpler schemas sees still more usage in industry. In general, IFC models are created for a specific purpose and wide-spread usage of those models in nieghbouring domaisn remain challenging because modelling for those neighbouring purposes requires alignment on the worldviews and levels of detail that is often beyond the scope in which such models are procured.</p>
</clause>

<clause id="_4a7f53de-8077-675c-a577-60f7debbf15e" obligation="normative" semx-id="_4a7f53de-8077-675c-a577-60f7debbf15e">
<title id="_9a460bed-5af1-786e-787e-75f0e437dccc" semx-id="_ff94d4f8-a74f-362a-f125-b40ebeac46a9">Implications and questions:</title><fmt-title depth="5" id="_0c830014-1a02-b4f4-4526-4fb619ee8f67"><span class="fmt-caption-label">9<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>4<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>5<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>1<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>4<span class="fmt-clause-delim">.</span></span><span class="fmt-caption-delim"><tab/></span><semx element="title" source="_9a460bed-5af1-786e-787e-75f0e437dccc">Implications and questions:</semx></fmt-title><fmt-xref-label><span class="fmt-element-name">Clause</span> 9<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>4<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>5<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>1<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>4</fmt-xref-label>
<ul id="_664e9ba2-7987-3fc8-a4b9-d33a9cb59407" semx-id="_664e9ba2-7987-3fc8-a4b9-d33a9cb59407"><li id="_ec70cef1-8a72-ae9c-04b4-f5ea7b5aa483"><fmt-name id="_ed9e22db-4dc9-fc97-977f-8ea223f096f9">—</fmt-name><p id="_585048cf-2b8f-5448-d957-a8064ad84146" semx-id="_585048cf-2b8f-5448-d957-a8064ad84146">Euclidean / non-euclidean; is a CRS required?</p>
</li>
<li id="_b82c1061-b0f8-30cc-5864-a311148c93f3"><fmt-name id="_272effd0-6efa-88f6-b2ba-151ae34def7b">—</fmt-name><p id="_61381fcb-2ce8-7ddb-f9e3-76618223013b" semx-id="_61381fcb-2ce8-7ddb-f9e3-76618223013b">Separate representation+placement → enables efficient reinstantiation, but hinders topological relationships because you require the pair of placement+geometry to locate in space</p>
</li>
<li id="_a8bd05a3-6750-98b9-2531-bcb09793b5d2"><fmt-name id="_0570dc9f-9ff3-5888-4d4b-837ac271a437">—</fmt-name><p id="_9860721d-38ea-c0ae-0e5a-efeb12ca8aa4" semx-id="_9860721d-38ea-c0ae-0e5a-efeb12ca8aa4">Geometry as leaf-values or object-relational model : cannot encapsulate geometry into a single literal, but allows for richer semantics</p>
</li>
<li id="_11dd4d77-6874-3dec-bb4a-7ddad740399d"><fmt-name id="_b14d7177-1d9e-5a19-e99e-34d71c10ac97">—</fmt-name><p id="_7f8c820d-7a28-8392-a733-9a3d60810827" semx-id="_7f8c820d-7a28-8392-a733-9a3d60810827">BRep model (topology + geometry + orientation + location) vs polyhedral model (e.g halfedge) vs explicit loops of point coordinates</p>
</li>
<li id="_0c8dd2d8-9931-6f4c-3ee9-ffa16a4a7408"><fmt-name id="_7fe78ba2-4122-c1e7-f7f4-529508765bfb">—</fmt-name><p id="_3b0f6558-50b6-26dc-5e4c-f59f3ce7d91b" semx-id="_3b0f6558-50b6-26dc-5e4c-f59f3ce7d91b">Procedural vs implicit (e.g constraints) vs explicit (polyhedra)</p>
</li>
<li id="_5f39910b-95ba-5512-5a29-f4b76272bc21"><fmt-name id="_2d6106f6-0910-33c8-b75d-a1bb49b5b139">—</fmt-name><p id="_ea427c29-2771-10e5-ba2b-af4977cfe7af" semx-id="_ea427c29-2771-10e5-ba2b-af4977cfe7af">Tolerances</p>
</li>
<li id="_d6b0b270-d6b4-3d8c-65bf-6f7875fcbd01"><fmt-name id="_cf8ae83f-d246-dc77-75d9-536c0a4c2816">—</fmt-name><p id="_5953fd2c-8478-c7a4-d195-09aa1a8f0a42" semx-id="_5953fd2c-8478-c7a4-d195-09aa1a8f0a42">Decomposition inside or outside of the ‘geometry ontology’</p>
</li>
<li id="_87fa424d-8a69-c2eb-e1f9-7dc22073b85f"><fmt-name id="_c8ef9ba1-4e05-4c8c-3b67-a6ab97cb5644">—</fmt-name><p id="_59a5552b-14ff-5657-6401-3ffdc1163972" semx-id="_59a5552b-14ff-5657-6401-3ffdc1163972">Are infra geometries (hor + ver alignment + cant, for positioning and sweeps) in scope?</p>
</li>
</ul>
</clause>
</clause>

<clause id="_dd34fea6-4d1d-7fa1-fc80-efec80a52167" obligation="normative" semx-id="_dd34fea6-4d1d-7fa1-fc80-efec80a52167">
<title id="_0b6aaf09-449f-c080-df27-f157e87e4247" semx-id="_1a691d86-2add-5f12-b0b7-16605f3ecd66">BOT (Building Topology Ontology)</title><fmt-title depth="4" id="_976268f5-5a68-9cd2-6647-0ed32e5e029a"><span class="fmt-caption-label">9<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>4<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>5<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>2<span class="fmt-clause-delim">.</span></span><span class="fmt-caption-delim"><tab/></span><semx element="title" source="_0b6aaf09-449f-c080-df27-f157e87e4247">BOT (Building Topology Ontology)</semx></fmt-title><fmt-xref-label><span class="fmt-element-name">Clause</span> 9<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>4<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>5<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>2</fmt-xref-label>
<p id="_32874763-e384-c99d-1972-0959b43e04ec" semx-id="_32874763-e384-c99d-1972-0959b43e04ec">AEC — Building domain ontology for storeys, spaces, elements + 3D mesh links; used with separate geometry vocabularies <eref type="inline" bibitemid="flotynski2020bot" citeas="[38]" id="_a64ce6dd-5f8d-fcb8-3a4a-3d8a2014ff28"/><fmt-xref type="inline" target="flotynski2020bot">[38]</fmt-xref>. |</p>
</clause>

<clause id="_f3afaa9d-156d-347d-c3a3-e939a6df4a9b" obligation="normative" semx-id="_f3afaa9d-156d-347d-c3a3-e939a6df4a9b">
<title id="_23eabf12-8eeb-d8b2-9aa0-d1da43bcb7cb" semx-id="_8e1e54c0-3042-a9f6-1e71-fc1f2eed1c95">Linked Building Server</title><fmt-title depth="4" id="_40c81eed-ecbf-8c19-fe43-dd523faa2bce"><span class="fmt-caption-label">9<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>4<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>5<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>3<span class="fmt-clause-delim">.</span></span><span class="fmt-caption-delim"><tab/></span><semx element="title" source="_23eabf12-8eeb-d8b2-9aa0-d1da43bcb7cb">Linked Building Server</semx></fmt-title><fmt-xref-label><span class="fmt-element-name">Clause</span> 9<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>4<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>5<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>3</fmt-xref-label>
<p id="_205ef361-b717-56c1-b59f-56b3a867b581" semx-id="_205ef361-b717-56c1-b59f-56b3a867b581">Over the last years, Building Information Modelling (BIM) has been increasingly adopted in building projects for its benefits to collaboration and associated risk mitigation. Recent work is trying to make this data available over the web (web-based BIM applications). Most notable in making building data web-ready is the linked building data (LBD) initiative. This group has worked on a number of vocabularies and ontologies, thereby starting from a linked data approach (RDF graphs), aiming at representing building data on the web. <link target="https://github.com/LBDserver" id="_a854d34a-a959-1cc9-00c1-e098edee8df1">Linked Building Data Server</link><fmt-link target="https://github.com/LBDserver">Linked Building Data Server</fmt-link></p>
</clause>
</clause>

<clause id="_d5d7fbb7-c525-9399-a7ad-1ec3558523ba" obligation="normative" semx-id="_d5d7fbb7-c525-9399-a7ad-1ec3558523ba">
<title id="_dc023edc-91d8-79fe-a5ca-5fcdb362d053" semx-id="_2de6fe90-d5e5-c8cf-4980-20ac9f9b6830">BIMSPARQL</title><fmt-title depth="3" id="_2c56e54f-adfc-0a35-1054-a81a9ac98133"><span class="fmt-caption-label">9<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>4<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>6<span class="fmt-clause-delim">.</span></span><span class="fmt-caption-delim"><tab/></span><semx element="title" source="_dc023edc-91d8-79fe-a5ca-5fcdb362d053">BIMSPARQL</semx></fmt-title><fmt-xref-label><span class="fmt-element-name">Clause</span> 9<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>4<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>6</fmt-xref-label>
<p id="_d3a3eb4a-ebcf-e06c-2b23-619d04a61f72" semx-id="_d3a3eb4a-ebcf-e06c-2b23-619d04a61f72">This initiative proposes extending SPARQL with functions for querying IFC building data to simplify queries and derive information from 3D geometry for BIM requirement checking. The functions are represented as RDF vocabularies and implemented using a combination of declarative rules and procedural programming. Evaluation with realistic requirement-checking scenarios demonstrates that the approach supports spatial and logical reasoning and can combine building and regulatory data from multiple sources. <eref type="inline" bibitemid="zhang2018bimsparql" citeas="[48]" id="_5a50642c-ce6e-3000-8424-acf39324e09b"/><fmt-xref type="inline" target="zhang2018bimsparql">[48]</fmt-xref></p>

<figure id="img_BIMSPARQL" anchor="img_BIMSPARQL" semx-id="_898d72fd-6b30-5197-3270-35a3024ea980" autonum="3">
<name id="_d5d4ecc7-015c-7a32-c0f8-59ee4dee4007" semx-id="_28bf7c80-9c6a-fd06-f465-4d68978d9417">IMSPARQL.</name><fmt-name id="_0dc8c8e2-84f8-b9ad-3d12-99e3104b4e20"><span class="fmt-caption-label"><span class="fmt-element-name">Figure</span> 3</span><span class="fmt-caption-delim"> — </span><semx element="name" source="_d5d4ecc7-015c-7a32-c0f8-59ee4dee4007">IMSPARQL.</semx></fmt-name><fmt-xref-label><span class="fmt-element-name">Figure</span> 3</fmt-xref-label>
<image id="_4ee25f94-5173-cafe-ddf4-44c38de95536" src="/home/runner/work/geosparql3dwhitepaper/geosparql3dwhitepaper/site/documents/document/_tmp_images_b23e76d0-eda9-450a-84ad-5c91026cd3da/a6b823d1-a142-472f-a885-317f8a1b78d5.png" extracted="true" mimetype="image/png" height="auto" width="auto" filename="figures/BIMSPARQL_Geometry_Functions.png" semx-id="_4ee25f94-5173-cafe-ddf4-44c38de95536"/></figure>
</clause>

<clause id="_f06494e2-774e-292e-c159-ad1bdd49a50c" obligation="normative" semx-id="_f06494e2-774e-292e-c159-ad1bdd49a50c">
<title id="_2a08904c-a5e6-8bf5-3125-1b3a094edae4" semx-id="_4aaa2a11-1249-540b-b7ec-783d6a68db07">stSPARQL</title><fmt-title depth="3" id="_afba5e61-793d-17bd-95ed-fd2fb850f0c8"><span class="fmt-caption-label">9<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>4<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>7<span class="fmt-clause-delim">.</span></span><span class="fmt-caption-delim"><tab/></span><semx element="title" source="_2a08904c-a5e6-8bf5-3125-1b3a094edae4">stSPARQL</semx></fmt-title><fmt-xref-label><span class="fmt-element-name">Clause</span> 9<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>4<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>7</fmt-xref-label>
<p id="_cd2c5c68-aa38-9831-2e4b-4efd7c6d5dd1" semx-id="_cd2c5c68-aa38-9831-2e4b-4efd7c6d5dd1">In Modeling and Querying Metadata in the Semantic Sensor Web: the Model stRDF and the Query Language stSPARQL <eref type="inline" bibitemid="koubarakis2010modeling" citeas="[49]" id="_b02e7410-5657-312b-7fea-dc501a36d58b"/><fmt-xref type="inline" target="koubarakis2010modeling">[49]</fmt-xref>, Koubarakis and Kyzirakos describe the extension of SPARQL with temporal information. These paterns can also be used for GeoSPARQL.</p>
</clause>

<clause id="_bedd1079-6cf8-ea0d-f216-2f319eb13938" obligation="normative" semx-id="_bedd1079-6cf8-ea0d-f216-2f319eb13938">
<title id="_efdc0676-29a8-2897-8f87-1ca5a8faa6b9" semx-id="_0f7db0b6-83e3-15ee-63e5-d5e89150890d">OntoBREP and OntoSTEP</title><fmt-title depth="3" id="_83772f6a-a2c8-a557-d0d9-490d35f70c96"><span class="fmt-caption-label">9<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>4<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>8<span class="fmt-clause-delim">.</span></span><span class="fmt-caption-delim"><tab/></span><semx element="title" source="_efdc0676-29a8-2897-8f87-1ca5a8faa6b9">OntoBREP and OntoSTEP</semx></fmt-title><fmt-xref-label><span class="fmt-element-name">Clause</span> 9<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>4<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>8</fmt-xref-label>
<p id="_ae28848b-bb30-5bc8-0913-5199560f0fd5" semx-id="_ae28848b-bb30-5bc8-0913-5199560f0fd5">OntoBREP is an ontology that can be used to describe CAD by representing boundary representations (B-Rep) using OWL. Geometric entities are represented semantically. For example, a circle is described by its coordinate frame and radius instead of a set of polygons. The ontology represents geometric constraints between objects and their individual faces or edges, enabling CAD geometry to be linked with additional knowledge and used for applications such as shape recognition and robot task planning. <eref type="inline" bibitemid="ontobrep" citeas="[50]" id="_23d4d98f-92fc-45a6-1437-afd723422964"/><fmt-xref type="inline" target="ontobrep">[50]</fmt-xref></p>

<p id="_cfdf9792-8cc4-f547-53ff-9808a780b925" semx-id="_cfdf9792-8cc4-f547-53ff-9808a780b925">OntoSTEP <eref type="inline" bibitemid="barbau2012ontostep" citeas="[54]" id="_2208935b-440b-93d0-6595-fd38ff942477"/><fmt-xref type="inline" target="barbau2012ontostep">[54]</fmt-xref> translates STEP schemas and their instances, originally defined using EXPRESS, into OWL. This creates a semantically representation of product and CAD information. The approach enables reasoning, inference, and querying over existing CAD data and provides a way to combine information represented using different modeling languages. <eref type="inline" bibitemid="ontostep" citeas="[51]" id="_391d84af-0023-0b69-1f29-b302414a5b3a"/><fmt-xref type="inline" target="ontostep">[51]</fmt-xref></p>

<p id="_dbdbeb96-a71e-2d5e-76b5-f81a682d72b0" semx-id="_dbdbeb96-a71e-2d5e-76b5-f81a682d72b0">OntoBREP and OntoSTEP represent approaches in which geometry are brought into the Semantic Web at a relatively deep semantic level. This results in potentially large and complex knowledge graphs.</p>

<p id="_8d0c0ee9-7c78-bd9e-f65a-6383bf7859db" semx-id="_8d0c0ee9-7c78-bd9e-f65a-6383bf7859db">Different approaches for representing geometry in the Semantic Web have been investigated <eref type="inline" bibitemid="c63f82e83d7249f18ab6e4b7afe049cb" citeas="[52]" id="_2273f390-b4f7-29d0-436c-b5da277c0280"/><fmt-xref type="inline" target="c63f82e83d7249f18ab6e4b7afe049cb">[52]</fmt-xref> ranging from integrating geometric structures into the knowledge graph to linking semantic resources to external geometry representations or files</p>

<figure id="img_Approaches_of_Geometry_in_Linked_Data" anchor="img_Approaches_of_Geometry_in_Linked_Data" semx-id="_50a63714-ab3f-999a-89d8-842d11eb422a" autonum="4">
<name id="_1e5d064c-9651-f834-ae87-ebaf54aefcc9" semx-id="_80629575-9d77-53be-0995-52fb560ae8ea">Approaches_of_Geometry_in_Linked_Data.</name><fmt-name id="_4261235d-5b02-de49-faf2-95672afca5af"><span class="fmt-caption-label"><span class="fmt-element-name">Figure</span> 4</span><span class="fmt-caption-delim"> — </span><semx element="name" source="_1e5d064c-9651-f834-ae87-ebaf54aefcc9">Approaches_of_Geometry_in_Linked_Data.</semx></fmt-name><fmt-xref-label><span class="fmt-element-name">Figure</span> 4</fmt-xref-label>
<image id="_37438478-0c32-01e4-3b39-7a1e66d87802" src="/home/runner/work/geosparql3dwhitepaper/geosparql3dwhitepaper/site/documents/document/_tmp_images_b23e76d0-eda9-450a-84ad-5c91026cd3da/2fdaad85-3d6d-43fa-97d0-3857d7266f6d.png" extracted="true" mimetype="image/png" height="auto" width="auto" filename="figures/Approaches_of_Geometry_in_Linked_Data.png" semx-id="_37438478-0c32-01e4-3b39-7a1e66d87802"/></figure>

<figure id="img_Implementation_of_approach_1" anchor="img_Implementation_of_approach_1" semx-id="_b5ec2875-5864-7785-dba0-a8b23b5730e6" autonum="5">
<name id="_ffe82581-b258-74a9-0663-735eea15dd0f" semx-id="_dbb863a1-9204-3459-369a-d53b54e0a43e">Implementation_of_approach_1.</name><fmt-name id="_98454532-87bc-259e-2f3a-10dee27449be"><span class="fmt-caption-label"><span class="fmt-element-name">Figure</span> 5</span><span class="fmt-caption-delim"> — </span><semx element="name" source="_ffe82581-b258-74a9-0663-735eea15dd0f">Implementation_of_approach_1.</semx></fmt-name><fmt-xref-label><span class="fmt-element-name">Figure</span> 5</fmt-xref-label>
<image id="_68477eb6-9a88-85ba-0957-029c9cacca7e" src="/home/runner/work/geosparql3dwhitepaper/geosparql3dwhitepaper/site/documents/document/_tmp_images_b23e76d0-eda9-450a-84ad-5c91026cd3da/3fe1212f-4f7d-45e1-99c6-b0be6612a6c9.png" extracted="true" mimetype="image/png" height="auto" width="auto" filename="figures/Implementation_of_approach_1.png" semx-id="_68477eb6-9a88-85ba-0957-029c9cacca7e"/></figure>
</clause>

<clause id="_bc07d95a-9a85-8936-5591-0b7f33f9bd44" obligation="normative" semx-id="_bc07d95a-9a85-8936-5591-0b7f33f9bd44">
<title id="_c940017f-8c8d-27d4-e6e7-874e4c57304a" semx-id="_11c96f0d-47fe-a993-6be9-6148a9110d67">ENVON</title><fmt-title depth="3" id="_f66cb8ad-f115-7c45-2727-2f27a2b3efa1"><span class="fmt-caption-label">9<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>4<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>9<span class="fmt-clause-delim">.</span></span><span class="fmt-caption-delim"><tab/></span><semx element="title" source="_c940017f-8c8d-27d4-e6e7-874e4c57304a">ENVON</semx></fmt-title><fmt-xref-label><span class="fmt-element-name">Clause</span> 9<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>4<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>9</fmt-xref-label>
<p id="_e500da87-7aeb-29e9-ec38-832c0e2427fe" semx-id="_e500da87-7aeb-29e9-ec38-832c0e2427fe">Robotics / manufacturing ontology that integrates contextual, topologic, geometric info for 3D environments; BFO‑based 3D shape ontology; BREP/CSG distinctions <eref type="inline" bibitemid="zhao2024envon" citeas="[39]" id="_9dfea217-8b55-b0a8-cd8d-5a0779dc9428"/><fmt-xref type="inline" target="zhao2024envon">[39]</fmt-xref>.</p>

<p id="_b91f2a7d-cbd4-dd9a-b665-81ed67db20bd" semx-id="_b91f2a7d-cbd4-dd9a-b665-81ed67db20bd"><strong>Use of domain specific ontologies</strong> Domain-specific 3D ontologies are typically expressed using  <strong>RDF/OWL</strong>, defining concepts and relationships that capture the semantics of a particular application domain <eref type="inline" bibitemid="FlotynskiWalczak2017" citeas="[31]" id="_838a662b-8b49-d39b-967f-5ad677d28541"/><fmt-xref type="inline" target="FlotynskiWalczak2017">[31]</fmt-xref> <eref type="inline" bibitemid="pittarello2006semantic" citeas="[46]" id="_2160ef50-a4a6-52f2-9363-9159db1e9ad6"/><fmt-xref type="inline" target="pittarello2006semantic">[46]</fmt-xref> <eref type="inline" bibitemid="walczak2019inference" citeas="[42]" id="_00e046d5-1187-57ae-dc3e-20cf281f860f"/><fmt-xref type="inline" target="walczak2019inference">[42]</fmt-xref>.</p>

<p id="_4169559d-aed7-ec31-0d26-9a8f43502c48" semx-id="_4169559d-aed7-ec31-0d26-9a8f43502c48">Geometry, topology, scene structure, and domain semantics are often represented by separate but complementary ontologies that can be combined at query time for integrated queries, such as BOT with geometry and sensor (SOSA/SSN or SAREF) ontologies in building applications <eref type="inline" bibitemid="zhao2024envon" citeas="[39]" id="_e3a70d24-bcc5-e8df-9de4-740480a9b5e1"/><fmt-xref type="inline" target="zhao2024envon">[39]</fmt-xref> <eref type="inline" bibitemid="flotynski2020bot" citeas="[38]" id="_f69c7715-ec58-0f45-7754-1ea0be8bdeb9"/><fmt-xref type="inline" target="flotynski2020bot">[38]</fmt-xref>.</p>
</clause>

<clause id="_f35362c6-b744-b00e-4da5-5fa784d19aeb" obligation="normative" semx-id="_f35362c6-b744-b00e-4da5-5fa784d19aeb">
<title id="_1044c053-0253-329a-8b67-eecfeb66ff98" semx-id="_3fab2b54-7fb8-4438-1863-66d3a603d01b">Location ontologies</title><fmt-title depth="3" id="_316504eb-d3e1-2f4b-6b9b-3176d2153589"><span class="fmt-caption-label">9<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>4<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>10<span class="fmt-clause-delim">.</span></span><span class="fmt-caption-delim"><tab/></span><semx element="title" source="_1044c053-0253-329a-8b67-eecfeb66ff98">Location ontologies</semx></fmt-title><fmt-xref-label><span class="fmt-element-name">Clause</span> 9<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>4<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>10</fmt-xref-label>
<clause id="_d5e8d647-f59a-d419-4648-497c57d6a9e8" obligation="normative" semx-id="_d5e8d647-f59a-d419-4648-497c57d6a9e8">
<title id="_792d59a9-2f2f-5495-d45a-1a0bc6a71f3a" semx-id="_957e7336-6bde-2671-0b6a-928b8106a736">Relative Location Ontology (Reloc)</title><fmt-title depth="4" id="_904d2d69-beee-e38b-8885-8a68c7a3685f"><span class="fmt-caption-label">9<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>4<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>10<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>1<span class="fmt-clause-delim">.</span></span><span class="fmt-caption-delim"><tab/></span><semx element="title" source="_792d59a9-2f2f-5495-d45a-1a0bc6a71f3a">Relative Location Ontology (Reloc)</semx></fmt-title><fmt-xref-label><span class="fmt-element-name">Clause</span> 9<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>4<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>10<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>1</fmt-xref-label>
<p id="_80add904-ac06-6fb3-7b86-6be0bd44e812" semx-id="_80add904-ac06-6fb3-7b86-6be0bd44e812">The <link target="https://annegoebels.github.io/reloc/" id="_7e19c7a1-235c-1c87-62b9-c9b2292ebfa2">Relative Location Ontology (RELOC)</link><fmt-link target="https://annegoebels.github.io/reloc/">Relative Location Ontology (RELOC)</fmt-link> <eref type="inline" bibitemid="Goebel2024" citeas="[28]" id="_1aa4ac14-5205-72c6-080f-9c212113ab45"/><fmt-xref type="inline" target="Goebel2024">[28]</fmt-xref> enables the expression of the spatial relationship between two objects using directional-topological terms. The ontology is designed to be compatible with the natural language-based approach of describing relative locations, enabling the conversion of existing location descriptions into structured spatial graph representations. The ontology provides directional, axis-related properties (e.g., longitudinal axis: front, center, rear) and topological properties (e.g., meet, contained in, intersect). These properties are finally merged into combined properties (e.g., containedInFront) to reflect the spatial information of statements like ‘A is in the front part of B’. The application context of the RELOC Ontology is the processing of existing damage documentation of bridges.</p>
</clause>

<clause id="_60d965fa-2614-add3-06e7-9e95a3b3712c" obligation="normative" semx-id="_60d965fa-2614-add3-06e7-9e95a3b3712c">
<title id="_ece123ba-2bad-4246-2e67-d32bfe4ab093" semx-id="_e2fb511b-3b6a-8a6d-4079-806903f2dd27">InfraspatialOT</title><fmt-title depth="4" id="_f3f4aa2e-25bd-a0de-a38f-c331942a9af4"><span class="fmt-caption-label">9<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>4<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>10<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>2<span class="fmt-clause-delim">.</span></span><span class="fmt-caption-delim"><tab/></span><semx element="title" source="_ece123ba-2bad-4246-2e67-d32bfe4ab093">InfraspatialOT</semx></fmt-title><fmt-xref-label><span class="fmt-element-name">Clause</span> 9<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>4<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>10<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>2</fmt-xref-label>
<p id="_de94757f-20e6-69a8-3b9a-a87d3eb12333" semx-id="_de94757f-20e6-69a8-3b9a-a87d3eb12333">The InfraspatialOT ontology, described in the paper: <link target="https://www.researchgate.net/publication/394086571_infraspatialOT_An_Ontology_for_the_Representation_of_Spatial_Relationships_in_Road_Infrastructure" id="_b6ecff4f-6371-aebd-32d9-1b7ed268481f">infraspatialOT: An Ontology for the Representation of Spatial Relationships in Road Infrastructure</link><fmt-link target="https://www.researchgate.net/publication/394086571_infraspatialOT_An_Ontology_for_the_Representation_of_Spatial_Relationships_in_Road_Infrastructure">infraspatialOT: An Ontology for the Representation of Spatial Relationships in Road Infrastructure</fmt-link> <eref type="inline" bibitemid="Heise2024infraspatialOT" citeas="[59]" id="_2af9a822-e079-21c8-84d0-8ee7fa8929ed"/><fmt-xref type="inline" target="Heise2024infraspatialOT">[59]</fmt-xref>, provides a semantic model for representing road infrastructure elements and their spatial relationships to reference elements. It distinguishes between localisation, which describes where an infrastructure element is located along a reference element, and positioning, which captures its relative spatial position, including horizontal and vertical relationships. Positioning relations can further specify whether a distance is involved. This structure enables road infrastructure elements and their spatial relationships to be represented and queried consistently using semantic technologies such as SPARQL.</p>
</clause>
</clause>

<clause id="_45a83025-5d0a-d26b-d07d-da1136adcea1" obligation="normative" semx-id="_45a83025-5d0a-d26b-d07d-da1136adcea1">
<title id="_e642f46d-784e-c1c3-82c2-21665cf18c3e" semx-id="_02b3a0da-fc03-10e6-d9b9-1fb10604df1a">Medicine and Chmistry</title><fmt-title depth="3" id="_4451eb16-102c-7d06-d34b-7e3a07b70118"><span class="fmt-caption-label">9<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>4<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>11<span class="fmt-clause-delim">.</span></span><span class="fmt-caption-delim"><tab/></span><semx element="title" source="_e642f46d-784e-c1c3-82c2-21665cf18c3e">Medicine and Chmistry</semx></fmt-title><fmt-xref-label><span class="fmt-element-name">Clause</span> 9<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>4<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>11</fmt-xref-label>
<clause id="_ca9a363a-67fe-de62-88f1-1530c9e7c595" obligation="normative" semx-id="_ca9a363a-67fe-de62-88f1-1530c9e7c595">
<title id="_ed98ed7c-ad7a-5e91-4513-f99f517427a8" semx-id="_286ac80c-3bf7-ca18-4b41-b49b02a32457">3D Microscopy Metadata Standards (3D-MMS)</title><fmt-title depth="4" id="_bc3fa9c5-889d-8a5b-a8d2-eca54168a592"><span class="fmt-caption-label">9<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>4<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>11<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>1<span class="fmt-clause-delim">.</span></span><span class="fmt-caption-delim"><tab/></span><semx element="title" source="_ed98ed7c-ad7a-5e91-4513-f99f517427a8">3D Microscopy Metadata Standards (3D-MMS)</semx></fmt-title><fmt-xref-label><span class="fmt-element-name">Clause</span> 9<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>4<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>11<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>1</fmt-xref-label>
<p id="_f4bbc8e4-b69d-8703-ee7f-14443cdf12bd" semx-id="_f4bbc8e4-b69d-8703-ee7f-14443cdf12bd">The 3D Microscopy Metadata Standards (3D-MMS) was developed by the BRAIN Initiative to establish a consistent way of describing 3D microscopy datasets, particularly datasets of brain tissue. Its purpose is to provide enough information for someone who did not generate the data to understand, find, interpret, reuse, and potentially integrate the dataset with other datasets. The 3D-MMS-metadata can be used to describe how the image volume is oriented and scaled, so that a position inside the microscopy dataset has meaningful spatial coordinates. <eref type="inline" bibitemid="ropelewski_standard_2022" citeas="[19]" id="_d320493b-9bbf-dd65-22b1-c8f4d7534132"/><fmt-xref type="inline" target="ropelewski_standard_2022">[19]</fmt-xref></p>
</clause>
</clause>
</clause>

<clause id="_6892c536-53a9-17f7-2ea0-06cd0a782949" obligation="normative" semx-id="_6892c536-53a9-17f7-2ea0-06cd0a782949">
<title id="_28bea750-313a-e879-4a10-075eb44e6dbb" semx-id="_0dbd9414-734c-2d34-6488-f9bbc7a69de9">3D Topology</title><fmt-title depth="2" id="_ecf9c066-a7ce-f472-dd96-41b2a16b99bc"><span class="fmt-caption-label">9<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>5<span class="fmt-clause-delim">.</span></span><span class="fmt-caption-delim"><tab/></span><semx element="title" source="_28bea750-313a-e879-4a10-075eb44e6dbb">3D Topology</semx></fmt-title><fmt-xref-label><span class="fmt-element-name">Clause</span> 9<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>5</fmt-xref-label>
<clause id="_f50cf49c-ad97-e55b-8f48-8b04b3b9f020" obligation="normative" semx-id="_f50cf49c-ad97-e55b-8f48-8b04b3b9f020">
<title id="_81f385bd-24f3-a533-991e-bd7160469e46" semx-id="_fac1a493-55ef-876f-aa7a-6f96583bb880">Topological Relationships and Their Use</title><fmt-title depth="3" id="_610f51ce-35a8-b86d-b75f-43275913115e"><span class="fmt-caption-label">9<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>5<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>1<span class="fmt-clause-delim">.</span></span><span class="fmt-caption-delim"><tab/></span><semx element="title" source="_81f385bd-24f3-a533-991e-bd7160469e46">Topological Relationships and Their Use</semx></fmt-title><fmt-xref-label><span class="fmt-element-name">Clause</span> 9<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>5<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>1</fmt-xref-label>
<p id="_d3988e6c-627b-eb80-ddc5-4b8d583a93a3" semx-id="_d3988e6c-627b-eb80-ddc5-4b8d583a93a3">The book chapter <link target="https://repository.tudelft.nl/record/uuid:09075b7e-e019-45f1-becc-cf8d7992b63b" id="_b5ab5d3d-f384-1f3f-1f50-fa6d52c45f4b">Topological Relationships and Their Use</link><fmt-link target="https://repository.tudelft.nl/record/uuid:09075b7e-e019-45f1-becc-cf8d7992b63b">Topological Relationships and Their Use</fmt-link> <eref type="inline" bibitemid="Zlatanova2017" citeas="[58]" id="_a2ec8460-b28e-6fbf-b7dd-7cb4597b0a3d"/><fmt-xref type="inline" target="Zlatanova2017">[58]</fmt-xref> highlights that topological relationships are well established for simple 2D objects, while 3D topological operations remain less developed. It identifies the need to support more complex 3D objects, including solids with holes and tunnels, and suggests reusing existing spatial operators as a starting point for extending topological analysis to 3D.</p>
</clause>

<clause id="_2386c5a7-66f1-d94d-2c15-aa410a3007bc" obligation="normative" semx-id="_2386c5a7-66f1-d94d-2c15-aa410a3007bc">
<title id="_00693726-2404-842f-c27a-e825c4f0b593" semx-id="_9e87d49f-32af-e9cb-10fd-707392f19e3d">Extension of RCC*-9 to Complex and Three-Dimensional Features and Its Reasoning System</title><fmt-title depth="3" id="_de52af13-5e2f-21ee-c892-bd906f31446d"><span class="fmt-caption-label">9<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>5<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>2<span class="fmt-clause-delim">.</span></span><span class="fmt-caption-delim"><tab/></span><semx element="title" source="_00693726-2404-842f-c27a-e825c4f0b593">Extension of RCC*-9 to Complex and Three-Dimensional Features and Its Reasoning System</semx></fmt-title><fmt-xref-label><span class="fmt-element-name">Clause</span> 9<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>5<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>2</fmt-xref-label>
<p id="_2102e410-53a6-429a-c9af-852e27115ddd" semx-id="_2102e410-53a6-429a-c9af-852e27115ddd">The work described in the paper <link target="https://www.mdpi.com/2220-9964/13/1/25" id="_649247b3-ea18-8ec0-b163-34f6300c45de">Extension of RCC*-9 to Complex and Three-Dimensional Features and Its Reasoning System</link><fmt-link target="https://www.mdpi.com/2220-9964/13/1/25">Extension of RCC*-9 to Complex and Three-Dimensional Features and Its Reasoning System</fmt-link> <eref type="inline" bibitemid="ijgi13010025" citeas="[60]" id="_2afdb6a7-b52f-831d-09e7-08d873f65414"/><fmt-xref type="inline" target="ijgi13010025">[60]</fmt-xref>, defines semantics of 3D bodies, regions, and lines, which can support the definition and interpretation of 3D spatial relations and operations. It provides a formal topological foundation that can be interesting for GeoSPARQL 3D.</p>
</clause>
</clause>

<clause id="_e994a16e-2ab1-9e28-d02d-b179d878943d" obligation="normative" semx-id="_e994a16e-2ab1-9e28-d02d-b179d878943d">
<title id="_b28a7625-697c-61dd-06ff-ab29a7799b1e" semx-id="_d995697b-a6a9-1723-be00-e74916d2ac17">Implementations</title><fmt-title depth="2" id="_65e19819-3285-4901-bc1d-f21aa3309997"><span class="fmt-caption-label">9<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>6<span class="fmt-clause-delim">.</span></span><span class="fmt-caption-delim"><tab/></span><semx element="title" source="_b28a7625-697c-61dd-06ff-ab29a7799b1e">Implementations</semx></fmt-title><fmt-xref-label><span class="fmt-element-name">Clause</span> 9<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>6</fmt-xref-label>
<p id="_d8df9043-17eb-c2ee-86bc-e9bd4c8595a9" semx-id="_d8df9043-17eb-c2ee-86bc-e9bd4c8595a9">The related work above primarily address 3D geometry semantics. A complementary aspect is the computational processing of the 3D representations. Implementations of 3D geometry processing provide the algorithms and data structures required to perform operations such as intersection, containment, distance calculation, Boolean operations, and topology analysis. This section  reviews established geometry-processing libraries and systems, including CGAL, SFCGAL, OpenCASCADE, and Topologic, and considers their relevance to the implementation of 3D spatial operations in GeoSPARQL.</p>

<clause id="_443955e4-586b-5a5f-00be-f2a83b362420" obligation="normative" semx-id="_443955e4-586b-5a5f-00be-f2a83b362420">
<title id="_44a10355-1598-6ca1-483c-8f5de4f2fb5e" semx-id="_4c6e408c-851f-29b1-7bfb-d74ae10edbc9">CGAL → SFCGAL → PostGIS</title><fmt-title depth="3" id="_0e95e159-1eb8-2a87-4642-f246643ffba9"><span class="fmt-caption-label">9<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>6<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>1<span class="fmt-clause-delim">.</span></span><span class="fmt-caption-delim"><tab/></span><semx element="title" source="_44a10355-1598-6ca1-483c-8f5de4f2fb5e">CGAL → SFCGAL → PostGIS</semx></fmt-title><fmt-xref-label><span class="fmt-element-name">Clause</span> 9<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>6<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>1</fmt-xref-label>
<p id="_cddd092b-3ae4-5f46-ed16-b1512fc08fd0" semx-id="_cddd092b-3ae4-5f46-ed16-b1512fc08fd0">CGAL (Computational Geometry Algorithms Library) provides data structures and algorithms for computational geometry, including operations on geometric objects that can serve as a computational foundation for spatial processing. SFCGAL builds on CGAL to provide additional support for 3D and volumetric geometries and operations, including Boolean operations and geometric predicates. SFCGAL is integrated with PostGIS, a spatial database extension for PostgreSQL, allowing such geometry-processing capabilities to be used within a database environment. Together, these technologies illustrate how 3D geometric representations can be connected to computational geometry algorithms and exposed through a spatial database system, providing a relevant implementation perspective for 3D spatial operations in GeoSPARQL.</p>
</clause>

<clause id="_37b610e4-0550-c345-24ad-c58d9cf6c849" obligation="normative" semx-id="_37b610e4-0550-c345-24ad-c58d9cf6c849">
<title id="_784153f6-40e4-d10d-b1f1-2c35e20f3a5d" semx-id="_8accff95-3327-b0bc-3ad2-74f3b5a8ed2b">OpenCASCADE</title><fmt-title depth="3" id="_6b61cce0-81be-4a6e-1e6e-0fc9762f8df6"><span class="fmt-caption-label">9<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>6<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>2<span class="fmt-clause-delim">.</span></span><span class="fmt-caption-delim"><tab/></span><semx element="title" source="_784153f6-40e4-d10d-b1f1-2c35e20f3a5d">OpenCASCADE</semx></fmt-title><fmt-xref-label><span class="fmt-element-name">Clause</span> 9<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>6<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>2</fmt-xref-label>
<p id="_4ebc0ddc-54f1-5a21-77fb-853d7fcd7231" semx-id="_4ebc0ddc-54f1-5a21-77fb-853d7fcd7231">OpenCASCADE is an open-source software platform for 3D geometric modelling and CAD applications. It provides data structures and algorithms for representing and processing geometric and topological entities, including points, curves, surfaces, solids, and B-Rep models. The platform supports operations such as geometric transformations, Boolean operations, intersections, and shape analysis. Its explicit representation of geometry and topology makes OpenCASCADE relevant for the computational processing of 3D solids and for investigating how B-Rep-based geometry could be supported by a GeoSPARQL 3D implementation.</p>

<clause id="_d2d9939d-373a-4b0c-62c0-ed29edec0ec2" obligation="normative" semx-id="_d2d9939d-373a-4b0c-62c0-ed29edec0ec2">
<title id="_b247fd47-f72a-bcad-529c-f1310acf57ea" semx-id="_641c7189-d449-bb21-d032-aac3758c14e3">OpenCASCADE-inspired BRep ontology</title><fmt-title depth="4" id="_93c6daeb-7067-2e3e-7834-168cc5731537"><span class="fmt-caption-label">9<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>6<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>2<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>1<span class="fmt-clause-delim">.</span></span><span class="fmt-caption-delim"><tab/></span><semx element="title" source="_b247fd47-f72a-bcad-529c-f1310acf57ea">OpenCASCADE-inspired BRep ontology</semx></fmt-title><fmt-xref-label><span class="fmt-element-name">Clause</span> 9<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>6<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>2<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>1</fmt-xref-label>
<p id="_b604b536-5355-e0a1-a020-e554381ac891" semx-id="_b604b536-5355-e0a1-a020-e554381ac891">Perzylo, A., Somani, N., Rickert, M., &amp; Knoll, A. (2015, September). An ontology for CAD data and geometric constraints as a link between product models and semantic robot task descriptions. In 2015 IEEE/RSJ international conference on intelligent robots and systems (IROS) (pp. 4197-4203). IEEE.</p>

<p id="_652a41a0-f049-2fec-811d-748a911d3374" semx-id="_652a41a0-f049-2fec-811d-748a911d3374"><link target="https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/abstract/document/7353971" id="_8c545676-8c6b-38e9-c665-d53beabdfcee"/><fmt-link target="https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/abstract/document/7353971"/></p>

<p id="_02488788-19f0-1344-37b7-c50b0196fe37" semx-id="_02488788-19f0-1344-37b7-c50b0196fe37"><eref type="inline" bibitemid="perzylo2015ontology" citeas="[25]" id="_a08ef7cc-91f2-38fc-9a01-401c73420231"/><fmt-xref type="inline" target="perzylo2015ontology">[25]</fmt-xref></p>
</clause>

<clause id="_b472cc51-6600-fc92-3a31-f9c15a682833" obligation="normative" semx-id="_b472cc51-6600-fc92-3a31-f9c15a682833">
<title id="_9c20c872-1989-e99f-3d72-e030c3799c7c" semx-id="_4eaae107-8626-d982-db97-aad0aa37f7bf">Topologic</title><fmt-title depth="4" id="_10d31b6a-b225-3b8c-3ecb-0eb4cff060a5"><span class="fmt-caption-label">9<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>6<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>2<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>2<span class="fmt-clause-delim">.</span></span><span class="fmt-caption-delim"><tab/></span><semx element="title" source="_9c20c872-1989-e99f-3d72-e030c3799c7c">Topologic</semx></fmt-title><fmt-xref-label><span class="fmt-element-name">Clause</span> 9<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>6<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>2<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>2</fmt-xref-label>
<p id="_4c0ad35a-c94a-aa8d-efca-3ee23ac8a4f5" semx-id="_4c0ad35a-c94a-aa8d-efca-3ee23ac8a4f5">Jabi, W., &amp; Chatzivasileiadi, A. (2021, January). Topologic: exploring spatial reasoning through geometry, topology, and semantics. In Formal Methods in Architecture: Proceedings of the 5th International Symposium on Formal Methods in Architecture (5FMA), Lisbon 2020 (pp. 277-285). Cham: Springer International Publishing.</p>

<p id="_56d23cb4-c290-6845-bbee-91e1a7a1c72b" semx-id="_56d23cb4-c290-6845-bbee-91e1a7a1c72b"><link target="https://topologic.app/" id="_11822280-5a39-286d-679e-5558742e4035"/><fmt-link target="https://topologic.app/"/></p>

<p id="_c8cbf404-346b-c7ba-3669-29233b92d573" semx-id="_c8cbf404-346b-c7ba-3669-29233b92d573"><eref type="inline" bibitemid="jabi2021topologic" citeas="[16]" id="_94d52b1a-2d52-4e69-a661-b7ea876cfdbd"/><fmt-xref type="inline" target="jabi2021topologic">[16]</fmt-xref></p>
</clause>
</clause>

<clause id="_6eb005bb-91f4-b0da-cc3f-4338d4e883e1" obligation="normative" semx-id="_6eb005bb-91f4-b0da-cc3f-4338d4e883e1">
<title id="_323d70c6-6b7c-a009-b1d0-879d5da68ca7" semx-id="_3548ce53-772d-38a0-4c63-7b254a8cc529">BRep vs mesh/polyhedron</title><fmt-title depth="3" id="_3207fdc0-5c78-daa1-682a-09beb1229ae2"><span class="fmt-caption-label">9<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>6<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>3<span class="fmt-clause-delim">.</span></span><span class="fmt-caption-delim"><tab/></span><semx element="title" source="_323d70c6-6b7c-a009-b1d0-879d5da68ca7">BRep vs mesh/polyhedron</semx></fmt-title><fmt-xref-label><span class="fmt-element-name">Clause</span> 9<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>6<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>3</fmt-xref-label>
<p id="_65b85ff0-b45a-5416-8794-7c9d0b79d16f" semx-id="_65b85ff0-b45a-5416-8794-7c9d0b79d16f">BRep - Curved surfaces - Topology: connected components as shells, solids with inner voids, etc. - Clean APIs due to inheritance: e.g  <tt>fn extrude(Topo) -&gt; Topo</tt>, for vertex → edge; edge → face; face → solid; solid → solid - Extra indirections: edge → vertex[] → point - Depending on implementation can be inefficient, e.g outer wire of face not explicitly marked need to be checked wrt infinite point - Data integrity and validation a bit harder</p>

<p id="_c8ad6be2-1ea0-27b1-fcc8-ae67aff8863d" semx-id="_c8ad6be2-1ea0-27b1-fcc8-ae67aff8863d">Mesh/polyhedron - Potentially fewer indirections - Triangle meshes robust and well understood - Many different data models though, e.g half-edge (only manifold), indexed faceset (no adjacency info), winged/quad/radial edge</p>

<p id="_3a70d39f-619f-4aeb-1648-1bf2ae1ed9c9" semx-id="_3a70d39f-619f-4aeb-1648-1bf2ae1ed9c9">B-Rep and mesh/polyhedron representations differ in how 3D geometry and topology are modelled. B-Rep explicitly represents curved surfaces and topological relationships between vertices, edges, faces, shells, and solids, making it suitable for complex solid models, but it can involve more complex data structures and validation requirements. Mesh and polyhedral representations generally use simpler discrete geometries, often based on triangles, with several possible data structures for representing adjacency and topology, such as half-edge, winged-edge, and indexed face sets. For GeoSPARQL 3D, this distinction is relevant because the underlying representation can affect the precision, robustness, and implementation of spatial operations.</p>
</clause>
</clause>

<clause id="_e21ac129-f30c-b8df-06e6-68429d479df6" obligation="normative" semx-id="_e21ac129-f30c-b8df-06e6-68429d479df6">
<title id="_ee1be836-6ff6-14f3-8eb5-3af1a3233127" semx-id="_8f236277-b14f-7a55-b1fd-f4ec708bc58a">To consider</title><fmt-title depth="2" id="_32e3ce98-4a7f-ffd9-1284-69eb9311a3b9"><span class="fmt-caption-label">9<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>7<span class="fmt-clause-delim">.</span></span><span class="fmt-caption-delim"><tab/></span><semx element="title" source="_ee1be836-6ff6-14f3-8eb5-3af1a3233127">To consider</semx></fmt-title><fmt-xref-label><span class="fmt-element-name">Clause</span> 9<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>7</fmt-xref-label>
<p id="_7b7c95a0-35cb-e3eb-68ad-012cfe60a251" semx-id="_7b7c95a0-35cb-e3eb-68ad-012cfe60a251"><link target="https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10845-023-02246-6" id="_d09445c2-50cd-f86b-883f-e73f26473d83"/><fmt-link target="https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10845-023-02246-6"/> Ontology of 3D virtual modeling in digital twin: a review, analysis and thinking</p>

<p id="_8f88b2c7-eba0-b110-699c-f103ba216fcb" semx-id="_8f88b2c7-eba0-b110-699c-f103ba216fcb"><eref type="inline" bibitemid="yuchen2025ontology" citeas="[26]" id="_56fcea43-dec1-dfe6-63c7-780fa60bc1be"/><fmt-xref type="inline" target="yuchen2025ontology">[26]</fmt-xref></p>

<p id="_156375c2-c342-0889-e190-389262bbdab4" semx-id="_156375c2-c342-0889-e190-389262bbdab4">To help novice engineers understand and scheme 3D virtual modeling in digital twin for future research and applications, this paper reviews 106 digital twin 3D modeling cases with their characteristics, including deployment targets, purposes &amp; roles, collaborative models, data flows, the autonomy of 3D modeling, fidelity, twinning rates, enabling technologies, and enabling tools.</p>

<p id="_23740047-bb32-d947-6051-810c96fbcef6" semx-id="_23740047-bb32-d947-6051-810c96fbcef6">Open standard for particle-mesh data (openPMD)</p>

<p id="_ce98ff7e-104c-8366-ef19-77bfbb67b8dd" semx-id="_ce98ff7e-104c-8366-ef19-77bfbb67b8dd">The <link target="https://github.com/openPMD/openPMD-standard" id="_c75e4ec1-e8eb-b8b1-267d-dee951ceb4ee">openPMD standard</link><fmt-link target="https://github.com/openPMD/openPMD-standard">openPMD standard</fmt-link>, short for open standard for particle-mesh data files is not a file format per se. It provides guidance for meta-data and naming schemes. openPMD provides naming and attribute conventions that allow the exchange of particle and mesh based data from scientific simulations and experiments. Its primary goal is to define a minimal set/kernel of meta information that allows to share and exchange data to achieve portability between various applications and different algorithms, a unified open-access description for scientific data (publishing and archiving), and a unified description for post-processing, visualization and analysis. If outputs from programs, devices (such as cameras), simulations or post-processed data-sets, contain a minimal set of meta information as provided by openPMD, data can be exchanged between those with minimal effort, with the same tools used for visualization.</p>

<p id="_74eb7009-a5cb-a632-6331-8c965dd43fb9" semx-id="_74eb7009-a5cb-a632-6331-8c965dd43fb9"><eref type="inline" bibitemid="huebl_2018_1167843" citeas="[8]" id="_90166e36-ad49-0c6f-6749-b3fc06c66aee"/><fmt-xref type="inline" target="huebl_2018_1167843">[8]</fmt-xref></p>
</clause>

<clause id="_39c2a8f6-0b42-093b-9be4-db17681cd8f7" obligation="normative" semx-id="_39c2a8f6-0b42-093b-9be4-db17681cd8f7">
<title id="_c523a80b-296b-5ce0-a093-56292e4e8cd8" semx-id="_bf6d2ca7-436c-1252-b4ef-9a2f0db72468">Vanilla 3D geometry handling in the Semantic Web</title><fmt-title depth="2" id="_2bb5f6b9-4baf-3a47-cb3e-6b9ba450d17f"><span class="fmt-caption-label">9<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>8<span class="fmt-clause-delim">.</span></span><span class="fmt-caption-delim"><tab/></span><semx element="title" source="_c523a80b-296b-5ce0-a093-56292e4e8cd8">Vanilla 3D geometry handling in the Semantic Web</semx></fmt-title><fmt-xref-label><span class="fmt-element-name">Clause</span> 9<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>8</fmt-xref-label>
<p id="_7d446a12-9e4e-b65f-62fb-287ce3b76ecd" semx-id="_7d446a12-9e4e-b65f-62fb-287ce3b76ecd">Different ontologies handle geometry in the Semantic Web in different ways. This section describes how 3D geometry is handled by different ontologies, rather than by GeoSPARQL. It compares approaches such as BOT, OMG, FOG, BIMSPARQL, GEOM, OntoBREP, and OntoSTEP, highlighting how each ontology represents, links, or manages geometric information within the Semantic Web.</p>

<clause id="_8597f865-f595-11cc-4a35-5dd79a023628" obligation="normative" semx-id="_8597f865-f595-11cc-4a35-5dd79a023628">
<title id="_93f6b334-7921-ce01-4efc-6825297d8812" semx-id="_0a2a1613-b48b-3f81-a6a7-d630bbe82a8c">BOT (Building Topology Ontology)</title><fmt-title depth="3" id="_d9686ce7-127c-b0ac-af24-aeb9b608d84a"><span class="fmt-caption-label">9<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>8<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>1<span class="fmt-clause-delim">.</span></span><span class="fmt-caption-delim"><tab/></span><semx element="title" source="_93f6b334-7921-ce01-4efc-6825297d8812">BOT (Building Topology Ontology)</semx></fmt-title><fmt-xref-label><span class="fmt-element-name">Clause</span> 9<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>8<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>1</fmt-xref-label>
<p id="_1bd6e143-a64b-8d9f-5d34-8182756de912" semx-id="_1bd6e143-a64b-8d9f-5d34-8182756de912">The Building Topology Ontology distinguishes zones and elements. It also has topological relations as <link target="https://w3id.org/bot#containsZone" id="_e4a6c59f-022a-4dac-d3fb-bd8f383124ce">bot:containsZone</link><fmt-link target="https://w3id.org/bot#containsZone">bot:containsZone</fmt-link> and <link target="https://w3id.org/bot#hasElement" id="_89b8c7ec-c628-b35a-46a2-8808630f667e">bot:hasElement</link><fmt-link target="https://w3id.org/bot#hasElement">bot:hasElement</fmt-link>. Any <link target="https://w3id.org/bot#Zone" id="_6f12f325-e07c-ebf9-899a-9c2a180d5182">bot:Zone</link><fmt-link target="https://w3id.org/bot#Zone">bot:Zone</fmt-link> or <link target="https://w3id.org/bot#Element" id="_36be082a-60e0-447e-6d4c-8da03ffe0c98">bot:Element</link><fmt-link target="https://w3id.org/bot#Element">bot:Element</fmt-link> can be assigned a 3D Model using some existing data format for 3D models. Two properties are defined for this. The <link target="https://w3id.org/bot#hasSimple3DModel" id="_e3ceccb2-0cc6-6bf3-3914-34b433cca712">bot:hasSimple3DModel</link><fmt-link target="https://w3id.org/bot#hasSimple3DModel">bot:hasSimple3DModel</fmt-link> datatype property links a <link target="https://w3id.org/bot#Zone" id="_b8c35d0d-030f-3838-c359-14676a50aadb">bot:Zone</link><fmt-link target="https://w3id.org/bot#Zone">bot:Zone</fmt-link> or <link target="https://w3id.org/bot#Element" id="_f201d4c6-d0a9-ae95-0e4b-18b2ffd89c2e">bot:Element</link><fmt-link target="https://w3id.org/bot#Element">bot:Element</fmt-link> to a 3D Model encoded as a literal. If the data format is textual, then the lexical form of the 3D Model literal SHOULD be encoded as a Unicode [UNICODE] string, which SHOULD be in Normal Form C [NFC]. If the data format is binary, then the lexical form of the 3D Model literal SHOULD be encoded using a base32 encoding [rfc4648]. The bot:has3DModel object property links a bot:Zone or bot:Element to some IRI that identifies a 3D Model. This 3D Model can then be described using some dedicated RDF vocabulary. Else, the 3D Model IRI could be dereferenceable, and when looking up the IRI one could retrieve a representation of the 3D Model with some existing data format for 3D models.</p>

<figure id="img_Building_Topology_Ontology_BOT_Example" anchor="img_Building_Topology_Ontology_BOT_Example" width="50%" semx-id="_88bac95b-f1ee-18ff-b685-69c46aacf87d" autonum="6">
<name id="_8f9ab312-7dd6-eefd-f02c-69173162087d" semx-id="_27c29069-7051-6326-8ad5-037dac651fad">Example of a graphical feedback from a request for all wall elements adjacent to a particular space using BOT terminology. The 3D model is described as an OBJ-formatted mesh.</name><fmt-name id="_12d8a95e-0f47-d8e9-4d26-65b941f0f940"><span class="fmt-caption-label"><span class="fmt-element-name">Figure</span> 6</span><span class="fmt-caption-delim"> — </span><semx element="name" source="_8f9ab312-7dd6-eefd-f02c-69173162087d">Example of a graphical feedback from a request for all wall elements adjacent to a particular space using BOT terminology. The 3D model is described as an OBJ-formatted mesh.</semx></fmt-name><fmt-xref-label><span class="fmt-element-name">Figure</span> 6</fmt-xref-label>
<image id="_0e96fbc4-0e77-813d-b8f1-bed3937b48de" src="/home/runner/work/geosparql3dwhitepaper/geosparql3dwhitepaper/site/documents/document/_tmp_images_b23e76d0-eda9-450a-84ad-5c91026cd3da/bed13e83-6f65-43a7-960f-32bdef436b9a.webp" extracted="true" mimetype="image/jpeg" height="auto" width="50%" filename="figures/10_3233_sw-200385-fig6.jpg" semx-id="_0e96fbc4-0e77-813d-b8f1-bed3937b48de"/></figure>

<p id="_8177a07b-2e61-0345-bb87-1e6b395fcf4a" semx-id="_8177a07b-2e61-0345-bb87-1e6b395fcf4a">source: <link target="https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/pdf/10.3233/SW-200385" id="_c5b1b96c-966e-2f35-7ea0-dfea06451b77"/><fmt-link target="https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/pdf/10.3233/SW-200385"/> <xref target="_0_3233_SW-200385" id="_d6065cfd-e167-2cd4-5d62-966c1c3916ea"/><fmt-xref target="_0_3233_SW-200385">[_0_3233_SW-200385]</fmt-xref></p>
</clause>

<clause id="_f20e2837-21ff-00ab-cc16-c0372e7dde5e" obligation="normative" semx-id="_f20e2837-21ff-00ab-cc16-c0372e7dde5e">
<title id="_b749d07e-5c2d-408d-74dd-372995c3ef25" semx-id="_5724ab9f-6aba-d48f-e07a-a2489bb3db09">OMG (Ontology for Managing Geometry)</title><fmt-title depth="3" id="_c253993c-7868-752e-c5a8-b076cccb6522"><span class="fmt-caption-label">9<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>8<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>2<span class="fmt-clause-delim">.</span></span><span class="fmt-caption-delim"><tab/></span><semx element="title" source="_b749d07e-5c2d-408d-74dd-372995c3ef25">OMG (Ontology for Managing Geometry)</semx></fmt-title><fmt-xref-label><span class="fmt-element-name">Clause</span> 9<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>8<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>2</fmt-xref-label>
<p id="_33b4b0ad-dbe8-c147-c229-6b33b061fcc8" semx-id="_33b4b0ad-dbe8-c147-c229-6b33b061fcc8">The Ontology for Managing Geometry (OMG) <eref type="inline" bibitemid="bonduel2019including" citeas="[10]" id="_e278b6b5-f54b-b231-4021-3904e7e2664f"/><fmt-xref type="inline" target="bonduel2019including">[10]</fmt-xref> is an ontology for attaching geometry descriptions to their corresponding things (e.g. building objects). The OMG is designed to provide three levels of adding the geometry descriptions which can be used and combined flexibly. The levels are designed to enable the attachment of metadata and comprise of the following functionalities:</p>

<p id="_0e9907ed-d0ef-5436-306d-7a25556bb1ab" semx-id="_0e9907ed-d0ef-5436-306d-7a25556bb1ab">Level 1: Direct connections between things and their geometry descriptions using either an object property for RDF-based geometry descriptions or a datatype property for non-RDF-based geometry descriptions resp. links to external files. Level 2: Introduction of an intermediate node between the things and their geometry descriptions. By reifying the geometry description, handling of multiple geometry descriptions of the same things is empowered, metadata (as tags) can be added and dependencies between geometries can be modelled. Level 3: Introduction of an intermediate node between the reified geometry and the geometry description to add geometry states. Thus, the version history of a geometry description can be described and stored.</p>
</clause>

<clause id="_70228f81-be50-05ef-03f3-5b52cadd1809" obligation="normative" semx-id="_70228f81-be50-05ef-03f3-5b52cadd1809">
<title id="_c1d93f12-5c94-1433-e32c-5c1b11e790cc" semx-id="_53dcd761-ccbb-ac37-819e-b5cb7775745c">FOG (File Ontology for Geometry formats)</title><fmt-title depth="3" id="_8c38852d-f159-2949-5671-e6e6b4211266"><span class="fmt-caption-label">9<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>8<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>3<span class="fmt-clause-delim">.</span></span><span class="fmt-caption-delim"><tab/></span><semx element="title" source="_c1d93f12-5c94-1433-e32c-5c1b11e790cc">FOG (File Ontology for Geometry formats)</semx></fmt-title><fmt-xref-label><span class="fmt-element-name">Clause</span> 9<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>8<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>3</fmt-xref-label>
<p id="_898646de-95a6-52b0-b368-a318c7973d1e" semx-id="_898646de-95a6-52b0-b368-a318c7973d1e">The File Ontology for Geometry formats (FOG) provides geometry schema specific relations between things (e.g. building objects) and their geometry descriptions. These geometry descriptions can be (1) RDF-based (e.g. using specific ontologies such as GEOM, OntoBREP, etc.), (2) RDF literals containing embedded geometry of existing geometry formats and (3) RDF literals containing a reference to an external geometry file.</p>

<p id="_1c8debce-8c40-a3ee-93ac-f4b4d67995af" semx-id="_1c8debce-8c40-a3ee-93ac-f4b4d67995af">such as OBJ, glTF and STEP, to be embedded in RDF or referenced as external files. Thus, while GeoSPARQL primarily describes geospatial geometry, BOT, OMG and FOG provide approaches that are more focused on building and construction geometry and the use of existing geometry formats.</p>

<p id="_260c4108-4463-5860-5836-91e8e3f7ebf5" semx-id="_260c4108-4463-5860-5836-91e8e3f7ebf5">the File Ontology for Geometry formats (FOG), a method for embedding and exchanging different 2D and 3D geometry formats within RDF graphs. FOG allows geometry data to be stored, referenced, and automatically identified, making it easier to exchange building geometry between different software and stakeholders.</p>
</clause>

<clause id="_a9b7f723-805f-66da-a195-de0f7e1eeca8" obligation="normative" semx-id="_a9b7f723-805f-66da-a195-de0f7e1eeca8">
<title id="_17d9e172-491c-171f-5ffd-6de1cc0147fc" semx-id="_19faa218-66e6-449e-8cff-9bced2fc4265">LBD Server, IFC2LBD and IFC2LBD-NEO</title><fmt-title depth="3" id="_1ea62870-c2f5-2fa0-cd0b-a040c64fceb5"><span class="fmt-caption-label">9<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>8<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>4<span class="fmt-clause-delim">.</span></span><span class="fmt-caption-delim"><tab/></span><semx element="title" source="_17d9e172-491c-171f-5ffd-6de1cc0147fc">LBD Server, IFC2LBD and IFC2LBD-NEO</semx></fmt-title><fmt-xref-label><span class="fmt-element-name">Clause</span> 9<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>8<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>4</fmt-xref-label>
<p id="_6365c467-dfe9-b245-299e-ca9a7f39ae27" semx-id="_6365c467-dfe9-b245-299e-ca9a7f39ae27">To make BIM data accessible on the web, the Linked Building Data (LBD) initiative applies Linked Data principles, using RDF, vocabularies, and ontologies to represent building information. To make this information practically accessible, a platform is needed that combines semantic building data with 3D visualisation. The LBD Server addresses this need by allowing users to upload building data, visualise its 3D geometry, and link geometric objects to their underlying semantic information. This enables users to explore both the physical and semantic aspects of a building within a web-based environment.</p>

<figure id="img_LBD_Server_connectivity_GLTF_LD" anchor="img_LBD_Server_connectivity_GLTF_LD" semx-id="_d371d546-f328-e11f-4d58-e7d971bbd8d9" autonum="7">
<name id="_bf30a41e-0119-6cd1-ddce-02c960e4dce7" semx-id="_c9bddc16-8424-7cd8-e4ab-1fef22ca433b">LBD_Server_connectivity_GLTF_LD.</name><fmt-name id="_e0116080-b738-3b54-341a-54b0e4873f6a"><span class="fmt-caption-label"><span class="fmt-element-name">Figure</span> 7</span><span class="fmt-caption-delim"> — </span><semx element="name" source="_bf30a41e-0119-6cd1-ddce-02c960e4dce7">LBD_Server_connectivity_GLTF_LD.</semx></fmt-name><fmt-xref-label><span class="fmt-element-name">Figure</span> 7</fmt-xref-label>
<image id="_f1c236de-b803-dc78-9262-dd2df968065d" src="/home/runner/work/geosparql3dwhitepaper/geosparql3dwhitepaper/site/documents/document/_tmp_images_b23e76d0-eda9-450a-84ad-5c91026cd3da/23076471-3199-4197-a4f0-f8646600dc64.png" extracted="true" mimetype="image/png" height="auto" width="auto" filename="figures/Schema_of_connectivity_between_glTF_mesh_geometry_and_graph_data.png" semx-id="_f1c236de-b803-dc78-9262-dd2df968065d"/></figure>

<p id="_11195302-f490-6ef1-0e0d-0d75be1b5351" semx-id="_11195302-f490-6ef1-0e0d-0d75be1b5351">source: <xref target="_3e4260b3605459b8ce220cfb42a3525" id="_112177af-83e6-c2c1-3c46-d3fffc26235d"/><fmt-xref target="_3e4260b3605459b8ce220cfb42a3525">[_3e4260b3605459b8ce220cfb42a3525]</fmt-xref></p>

<p id="_fe6cac80-7bf6-3f6a-1c74-f48a83cbfc1f" semx-id="_fe6cac80-7bf6-3f6a-1c74-f48a83cbfc1f">IFC2LBD makes the semantic transformation itself a central component. It converts IFC into RDF using LBD ontologies such as BOT, PRODUCT and PROPS. The result is a graph representation that can be queried and linked on the web. The original IFC-to-LBD work explicitly aimed to produce a simpler, modular representation than directly using the full ifcOWL structure. IFC2LBD-Neo takes this further by redesigning the converter as a modular, staged pipeline. It additionally uses ontologies as BEO, bSDD and OMG/FOG. The geometry is generated separately as glTF, Fragments or Parquet outputs.</p>

<figure id="img_BIFC2LBD-NEO" anchor="img_BIFC2LBD-NEO" semx-id="_60d4cfbb-bc97-2828-9958-74d022498641" autonum="8">
<name id="_38d21d2f-80bd-c378-3436-7b9325264d7e" semx-id="_124959df-19e5-3287-c4bc-517d6381bafb">IFC2LBD Pipeline.</name><fmt-name id="_6ee99bba-f5cf-4917-6e40-6295f41a35a4"><span class="fmt-caption-label"><span class="fmt-element-name">Figure</span> 8</span><span class="fmt-caption-delim"> — </span><semx element="name" source="_38d21d2f-80bd-c378-3436-7b9325264d7e">IFC2LBD Pipeline.</semx></fmt-name><fmt-xref-label><span class="fmt-element-name">Figure</span> 8</fmt-xref-label>
<image id="_c99ff4b1-8ca2-9142-7cc7-cc04f53f7d1a" src="/home/runner/work/geosparql3dwhitepaper/geosparql3dwhitepaper/site/documents/document/_tmp_images_b23e76d0-eda9-450a-84ad-5c91026cd3da/15aebefc-b76a-4937-8ad2-6ee66fe04dc6.png" extracted="true" mimetype="image/png" height="auto" width="auto" filename="figures/IFC2LBD-NEO.png" semx-id="_c99ff4b1-8ca2-9142-7cc7-cc04f53f7d1a"/></figure>

<p id="_24b4f18b-5c85-5073-fd88-e2c11f8056fc" semx-id="_24b4f18b-5c85-5073-fd88-e2c11f8056fc">source: <link target="https://github.com/ISBE-TUe/IFC2LBD-Neo" id="_cf478f76-b5e7-47f0-519a-37a7a71d233d"/><fmt-link target="https://github.com/ISBE-TUe/IFC2LBD-Neo"/></p>

<figure id="img_File_Ontology_for_Geometry_Formats_FOG" anchor="img_File_Ontology_for_Geometry_Formats_FOG" width="50%" semx-id="_b469d725-136a-b67c-8cd3-ce1e83035a63" autonum="9">
<name id="_fbf1ecc8-00f0-7f67-ac55-b6ad2a8a9f73" semx-id="_b4d5606f-9cb1-d133-1878-4b748add22eb">Demo Application with use of File Ontology for Geometry Formats FOG</name><fmt-name id="_8e0f22b4-4cf4-d36d-2293-b569ccee7480"><span class="fmt-caption-label"><span class="fmt-element-name">Figure</span> 9</span><span class="fmt-caption-delim"> — </span><semx element="name" source="_fbf1ecc8-00f0-7f67-ac55-b6ad2a8a9f73">Demo Application with use of File Ontology for Geometry Formats FOG</semx></fmt-name><fmt-xref-label><span class="fmt-element-name">Figure</span> 9</fmt-xref-label>
<image id="_1b68b500-bda2-2746-74b7-a99df7fd92ed" src="/home/runner/work/geosparql3dwhitepaper/geosparql3dwhitepaper/site/documents/document/_tmp_images_b23e76d0-eda9-450a-84ad-5c91026cd3da/d529c873-9958-4cc0-8f3c-c1a83829fd2e.png" extracted="true" mimetype="image/png" height="auto" width="50%" filename="figures/Screenshot_of_demo_web_application_FOG.png" semx-id="_1b68b500-bda2-2746-74b7-a99df7fd92ed"/></figure>

<p id="_3fc6fafc-a21b-562d-38f8-1a39d8f31c1b" semx-id="_3fc6fafc-a21b-562d-38f8-1a39d8f31c1b">source: <link target="https://ec-3.org/wp-content/uploads/2025/10/EC32019_166.pdf" id="_2dcbc120-0408-8967-e564-c9922f43d95e"/><fmt-link target="https://ec-3.org/wp-content/uploads/2025/10/EC32019_166.pdf"/></p>
</clause>

<clause id="_ab9415c8-4828-c1d7-d0e7-ee79af8cd0ab" obligation="normative" semx-id="_ab9415c8-4828-c1d7-d0e7-ee79af8cd0ab">
<title id="_1bebb919-6d28-faa7-35c7-5b1eb9a7093a" semx-id="_4e1d04c0-6250-234e-5421-0a8ba1826508">Routing using LADM and IndoorGML</title><fmt-title depth="3" id="_e1d5a9c8-300c-374c-5ee9-6184f39b3d5a"><span class="fmt-caption-label">9<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>8<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>5<span class="fmt-clause-delim">.</span></span><span class="fmt-caption-delim"><tab/></span><semx element="title" source="_1bebb919-6d28-faa7-35c7-5b1eb9a7093a">Routing using LADM and IndoorGML</semx></fmt-title><fmt-xref-label><span class="fmt-element-name">Clause</span> 9<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>8<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>5</fmt-xref-label>
<p id="_e9957528-4ea5-3c46-49ee-569ae648e744" semx-id="_e9957528-4ea5-3c46-49ee-569ae648e744">IndoorGML (OGC) models indoor environments as cells, such as rooms, corridors and stairwells, and derives a navigation graph in which spaces are nodes and their connections, such as doors and openings, are edges. The Land Administration Domain Model (LADM, ISO 19152) describes the rights, restrictions and responsibilities attached to spaces. The combined LADM-IndoorGML model uses these legal aspects to define which indoor spaces are accessible to which users.</p>

<p id="_b1ef6d8f-f7ce-85f3-9209-2f54a5ff6240" semx-id="_b1ef6d8f-f7ce-85f3-9209-2f54a5ff6240"><eref type="inline" bibitemid="alattas2018" citeas="[61]" id="_1a18fba5-deb7-e6da-ba40-0d1eadebc3a4"/><fmt-xref type="inline" target="alattas2018">[61]</fmt-xref> applied this combined model to building evacuation. They showed that access rights depend on time (normal hours, off-hours and crisis situations) and on the type of user, such as visitors, employees or students, and that these rights determine which routes users can take to the nearest emergency exit or safe zone.</p>
</clause>
</clause>
</clause>

<clause id="_d657f8bc-20d5-7bbc-3eab-fcde938d1743" obligation="normative" semx-id="_d657f8bc-20d5-7bbc-3eab-fcde938d1743" displayorder="17">
<title id="_1b272454-f108-545b-da8c-c55117b805e7" semx-id="_a162a270-2ca1-e1b4-0648-9269831836a5">Requirements for GeoSPARQL 3D</title><fmt-title depth="1" id="_e6aa3aff-f5f1-2baa-cb8b-3533f093bd54"><span class="fmt-caption-label">10<span class="fmt-clause-delim">.</span></span><span class="fmt-caption-delim"><tab/></span><semx element="title" source="_1b272454-f108-545b-da8c-c55117b805e7">Requirements for GeoSPARQL 3D</semx></fmt-title><fmt-xref-label><span class="fmt-element-name">Clause</span> 10</fmt-xref-label>
<p id="_44ae02d0-7b2c-9561-95e2-859aeb53f848" semx-id="_44ae02d0-7b2c-9561-95e2-859aeb53f848">This section provides an overview of feedback received on the current version of the GeoSPARQL standard (version 1.1) regarding 3D usage. It helps to identify some of the barriers to use, and to outline requirements that have not been addressed that may encourage greater uptake.</p>

<clause id="_9a7d02a0-4539-3f18-e57a-24835028a410" obligation="normative" semx-id="_9a7d02a0-4539-3f18-e57a-24835028a410">
<title id="_046b4efd-dfd0-d284-d0e8-ed603c9bfe5f" semx-id="_fa234c9b-638d-9532-cd94-09e1404beccd">Extension 1: 3D representations</title><fmt-title depth="2" id="_e695db5f-4157-fe8d-5e35-56896cf17e83"><span class="fmt-caption-label">10<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>1<span class="fmt-clause-delim">.</span></span><span class="fmt-caption-delim"><tab/></span><semx element="title" source="_046b4efd-dfd0-d284-d0e8-ed603c9bfe5f">Extension 1: 3D representations</semx></fmt-title><fmt-xref-label><span class="fmt-element-name">Clause</span> 10<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>1</fmt-xref-label>
<p id="_f65b084e-2ccf-42f4-4d3b-6193e54eddfa" semx-id="_f65b084e-2ccf-42f4-4d3b-6193e54eddfa"><strong>GitHub Issue URI</strong></p>

<ul id="_dab6cad3-543b-4180-3032-ba4b3d509c95" semx-id="_dab6cad3-543b-4180-3032-ba4b3d509c95"><li id="_2674ff94-a64f-9d22-82b9-1a999223ff3d"><fmt-name id="_091daeee-99e9-7220-35cd-f83f7380fa62">—</fmt-name><p id="_7cc7752f-e4b6-1ea3-c3c7-b5c0866a2871" semx-id="_7cc7752f-e4b6-1ea3-c3c7-b5c0866a2871"><link target="https://github.com/opengeospatial/ogc-geosparql/issues/583" id="_3b7d26b6-7cca-f4ef-127b-b694d6d8f9bf"/><fmt-link target="https://github.com/opengeospatial/ogc-geosparql/issues/583"/></p>
</li>
</ul>

<p id="_cb183c4a-a35b-3d2e-ca94-3291bb615d04" semx-id="_cb183c4a-a35b-3d2e-ca94-3291bb615d04"><strong>Category</strong></p>

<p id="_48b0d88e-2762-ec60-df06-ee3184246a98" semx-id="_48b0d88e-2762-ec60-df06-ee3184246a98">Semantic improvement</p>

<p id="_25adae48-18d6-c4dd-e52f-cd6c40ef2373" semx-id="_25adae48-18d6-c4dd-e52f-cd6c40ef2373"><strong>Description</strong></p>

<p id="_b8b6eac1-3ae5-1e02-dcd4-165395446ba2" semx-id="_b8b6eac1-3ae5-1e02-dcd4-165395446ba2">GeoSPARQL primarily focuses on spatial representation and querying rather than visualization, formats such as glTF and 3D Tiles could provide a complementary representation and delivery mechanism for the resulting 3D data. GeoSPARQL should include ways to represent 3D data in a knowledge graph. 3D data should be included in common 3D formats and 3D data should be includable as a text literal and a file representation. Where 3D data is intended for web-based visualization, formats should preferably be selected that are well supported by commonly used JavaScript and web-mapping libraries. This would facilitate the visualization and delivery of 3D knowledge-graph data on the web and help establish a practical bridge between BIM and GIS, enabling 3D information originating from BIM to be queried, linked, and visualized together with geospatial data.</p>

<p id="_7293c023-2c2a-1204-df2b-d9a8735be5ef" semx-id="_7293c023-2c2a-1204-df2b-d9a8735be5ef">Some common formats which could be considered for inclusion are:</p>

<ul id="_c4343939-c403-fbd2-33d6-ed948a6d59ad" semx-id="_c4343939-c403-fbd2-33d6-ed948a6d59ad"><li id="_ad7e807a-7cf0-4290-8f9c-dfb74b8e394a"><fmt-name id="_4e2941d4-8aa9-f6d8-67d1-415a743f2686">—</fmt-name><p id="_c50cf6d2-6be4-617e-e363-d6550dbd3401" semx-id="_c50cf6d2-6be4-617e-e363-d6550dbd3401"><link target="https://paulbourke.net/dataformats/ply/" id="_cc2f6998-0e3c-eca8-b8cb-d3da143b8d22">Polygon File Format (PLY)</link><fmt-link target="https://paulbourke.net/dataformats/ply/">Polygon File Format (PLY)</fmt-link></p>
</li>
<li id="_87301f53-61eb-eff7-e0bf-e58349d16fbb"><fmt-name id="_67683c6a-95e6-5c4b-d270-effd70bb3d6e">—</fmt-name><p id="_7456b28b-e1e9-d95a-706f-4465fd31a589" semx-id="_7456b28b-e1e9-d95a-706f-4465fd31a589"><link target="https://www.loc.gov/preservation/digital/formats/fdd/fdd000507.shtml" id="_c2fc0d85-331d-522d-95af-f8d4a0953652">Wavefront OBJ Format (OBJ)</link><fmt-link target="https://www.loc.gov/preservation/digital/formats/fdd/fdd000507.shtml">Wavefront OBJ Format (OBJ)</fmt-link></p>
</li>
<li id="_db7af188-79ac-65e7-b4fb-bb3cf524f5ea"><fmt-name id="_cb9ec0fa-1d89-a007-9f1e-de62d3ccd3f1">—</fmt-name><p id="_8dcd9248-f8a6-5772-2418-8b1bb09aec54" semx-id="_8dcd9248-f8a6-5772-2418-8b1bb09aec54"><link target="https://registry.khronos.org/glTF/specs/2.0/glTF-2.0.html" id="_4b72b93e-7e58-01f5-88cf-de902a1de1e9">GLTF Format (GLTF)</link><fmt-link target="https://registry.khronos.org/glTF/specs/2.0/glTF-2.0.html">GLTF Format (GLTF)</fmt-link></p>
</li>
<li id="_738ebbda-53f6-0004-e6a5-4870fe76ceab"><fmt-name id="_7c216849-ec69-b8b7-5c5e-eb932ee5bcd2">—</fmt-name><p id="_05c1ef13-3ec8-64a5-1865-59e3489b6239" semx-id="_05c1ef13-3ec8-64a5-1865-59e3489b6239"><link target="https://www.web3d.org" id="_38c0b11a-a479-b3f7-c795-e1f2843dc0d8">X3D Format</link><fmt-link target="https://www.web3d.org">X3D Format</fmt-link></p>
</li>
<li id="_f80d7a87-f61f-0911-04c3-ecfc64334d96"><fmt-name id="_6585287e-36ff-01c1-4ad6-34ebcab6c06e">—</fmt-name><p id="_1e0bf9a0-9a4a-f535-f142-52395564ac54" semx-id="_1e0bf9a0-9a4a-f535-f142-52395564ac54"><link target="https://openusd.org/dev/spec.html" id="_30209ce0-13f2-27c3-dfb6-a27a08468a1c">openUSD</link><fmt-link target="https://openusd.org/dev/spec.html">openUSD</fmt-link></p>
</li>
</ul>

<figure id="img_LBD_Server" anchor="img_LBD_Server" semx-id="_c5946f1c-5a62-c47e-1a5d-c8ede07af6b3" autonum="10">
<name id="_d7e75dc4-2523-15b4-0a46-ad3a06f4dafb" semx-id="_6992fe12-2151-4c5b-00af-e0cdb13487f6">Linked Building Data Server using Semantic Graphs and GLTF-Models</name><fmt-name id="_9bcf5f0c-006e-2e84-fd2e-23d0f9b93160"><span class="fmt-caption-label"><span class="fmt-element-name">Figure</span> 10</span><span class="fmt-caption-delim"> — </span><semx element="name" source="_d7e75dc4-2523-15b4-0a46-ad3a06f4dafb">Linked Building Data Server using Semantic Graphs and GLTF-Models</semx></fmt-name><fmt-xref-label><span class="fmt-element-name">Figure</span> 10</fmt-xref-label>
