<p id="_f968149d-92b0-06c2-2ea3-342f81dc1e69" semx-id="_f968149d-92b0-06c2-2ea3-342f81dc1e69"><strong>Category</strong></p>

<p id="_3aca5b3e-9b5c-a9c3-139f-9e0762144480" semx-id="_3aca5b3e-9b5c-a9c3-139f-9e0762144480">Semantic improvement</p>

<p id="_f50a3832-c06c-6ed4-81dc-cddf6ea8dc01" semx-id="_f50a3832-c06c-6ed4-81dc-cddf6ea8dc01"><strong>Description</strong></p>

<p id="_1d1556d1-b2b5-090a-1d67-1170b5f8538d" semx-id="_1d1556d1-b2b5-090a-1d67-1170b5f8538d">GeoSPARQL should include ways to define multi-component 3D geometries, whereas each component expresses its own semantics. For example, parts of a building could have different semantics according to the function of the building components and would be classified as such in an RDF graph.</p>

<p id="_b78b44f3-ddb5-968b-2df6-d12f43bdb4a9" semx-id="_b78b44f3-ddb5-968b-2df6-d12f43bdb4a9">Existing standards such as IFC, CityGML, SML already provide mechanisms for decomposing 3D objects into 3D parts. GeoSPARQL could build on these existing relationships, allowing the geometries of individual components to be queried and analysed together with their semantics. This would enable queries that combine spatial and semantic information, for example, identifying all building components within a specific area that are made of lead, or finding all walls that are part of a particular building and intersect a given spatial region.</p>

<figure id="img_IFC_To_CityGML_Use_case_Brdige" anchor="img_IFC_To_CityGML_Use_case_Brdige" semx-id="_0bdf013c-ffa0-3ccc-d03f-eaffb07b4157" autonum="18">
<name id="_2efa2e1d-9de9-3f55-d1d5-68ee5ca799d3" semx-id="_756fab39-0059-def4-66a9-ffed856393f0">Model of a bridge where the bridge parts &quot;Pier&quot; and &quot;Deck&quot; contain the geometries of the bridge.</name><fmt-name id="_e4d12009-784a-0750-c72b-a498344d53c4"><span class="fmt-caption-label"><span class="fmt-element-name">Figure</span> 18</span><span class="fmt-caption-delim"> — </span><semx element="name" source="_2efa2e1d-9de9-3f55-d1d5-68ee5ca799d3">Model of a bridge where the bridge parts &quot;Pier&quot; and &quot;Deck&quot; contain the geometries of the bridge.</semx></fmt-name><fmt-xref-label><span class="fmt-element-name">Figure</span> 18</fmt-xref-label>
<image id="_8008376e-7981-bca5-7378-5675330278cf" src="/home/runner/work/geosparql3dwhitepaper/geosparql3dwhitepaper/site/documents/document/_tmp_images_b23e76d0-eda9-450a-84ad-5c91026cd3da/020f8a22-4066-4ccf-96c1-0c28691ddd4d.png" extracted="true" mimetype="image/png" height="auto" width="auto" filename="figures/IFC_To_CityGML_Use_Case_Bridge.png" semx-id="_8008376e-7981-bca5-7378-5675330278cf"/></figure>

