<p id="_c700df7b-3fab-360f-64b9-93d6ca72e9f4" semx-id="_c700df7b-3fab-360f-64b9-93d6ca72e9f4"><strong>Category</strong></p>

<p id="_bab25b8f-26f3-3e6f-bbd2-e36d1fee4357" semx-id="_bab25b8f-26f3-3e6f-bbd2-e36d1fee4357">Semantic improvement</p>

<p id="_f61ec24e-6cec-a2f4-cb54-5a33bbb1fef8" semx-id="_f61ec24e-6cec-a2f4-cb54-5a33bbb1fef8"><strong>Description</strong></p>

<p id="_6fc180dc-9f6d-2a09-d3ba-6b32a8bf8254" semx-id="_6fc180dc-9f6d-2a09-d3ba-6b32a8bf8254">GeoSPARQL should include ways to position 3D geometries in a 3D space. Commonly 3D geometries are rotated, translated and scaled using commonly defined operators in computer graphics. Similar operations are needed for the relative positioning of 3D objects in GeoSPARQL, as properties and potentially as functions.</p>

<p id="_e3dfe8f2-ddfe-db78-c7e2-ded1b0747aa8" semx-id="_e3dfe8f2-ddfe-db78-c7e2-ded1b0747aa8"><strong>Related work</strong></p>

