<p id="_65a438c0-0298-dbad-8770-8ea513df310f" semx-id="_65a438c0-0298-dbad-8770-8ea513df310f"><strong>Category</strong></p>

<p id="_994e7607-0643-aa6b-5be1-ca56ef2f66d6" semx-id="_994e7607-0643-aa6b-5be1-ca56ef2f66d6">Semantic improvement</p>

<p id="_2dde450f-05e1-d9a7-6be6-8b817ea64f2d" semx-id="_2dde450f-05e1-d9a7-6be6-8b817ea64f2d"><strong>Description</strong></p>

<p id="_f93e782a-be39-399b-e153-038801c2454e" semx-id="_f93e782a-be39-399b-e153-038801c2454e">GeoSPARQL 3D should provide functions useful for geometry transformations and complex 3D geometry representations. One such example is use of triangulated irregular networks (TIN) for digital elevation models (DEMs), digital surface models (DSMs) or digital terrain models (DTMs). Turning any geometry into TIN will allow for integrating it into such models. Translation by vector function allows for geometry alignments and adjustments while preserving relations between its points.</p>

<p id="_8655ba4d-b268-ed0e-9136-ce62d3ea9c43" semx-id="_8655ba4d-b268-ed0e-9136-ce62d3ea9c43"><strong>Related work</strong></p>

<ul id="_a2737dc8-af93-1270-55ff-5ef2276aa74f" semx-id="_a2737dc8-af93-1270-55ff-5ef2276aa74f"><li id="_5d4cd416-9e4c-9c81-c7d8-e64c411884fe"><fmt-name id="_6f7dc042-2017-9bab-e7e3-8cfeb7b6e4a9">—</fmt-name><p id="_4a63a664-a17d-cb69-e5ca-e898c9416af9" semx-id="_4a63a664-a17d-cb69-e5ca-e898c9416af9">CGAL → SFCGAL → PostGIS</p>
</li>
<li id="_50b91160-e90d-107c-d47e-020ff315fe2b"><fmt-name id="_999f3b98-9f9b-4e34-be6e-c712f0c1ad72">—</fmt-name><p id="_61a3872b-f35e-4bca-7bb7-6a7f306a85f3" semx-id="_61a3872b-f35e-4bca-7bb7-6a7f306a85f3">OpenCASCADE</p>
</li>
<li id="_b580f90a-967c-b3cd-ef9b-dc9ab984ade3"><fmt-name id="_3cb38ec0-dc7a-37ed-d9d2-257fc9269dda">—</fmt-name><p id="_c9d57135-50ed-b789-6522-db37ef0661aa" semx-id="_c9d57135-50ed-b789-6522-db37ef0661aa">CityGML</p>
</li>
<li id="_ce734517-15eb-c87c-e0c9-2810ef15f169"><fmt-name id="_6875bf01-0f36-4a86-989a-11bbefec82ea">—</fmt-name><p id="_2b43b685-ab3a-fd9c-2510-0b7fb024f066" semx-id="_2b43b685-ab3a-fd9c-2510-0b7fb024f066">BRep vs mesh/polyhedron</p>
</li>
</ul>
</clause>

<clause id="_7afed8cd-d825-0ece-b8a2-bf1343c2a8f9" obligation="normative" semx-id="_7afed8cd-d825-0ece-b8a2-bf1343c2a8f9">
<title id="_22078850-28f9-91a0-7993-d4daa9451867" semx-id="_70ea51be-0c92-dd70-ddb4-cec95a5e4c18">Concluding overview of requirements for 3D geometry in the semantic web</title><fmt-title depth="2" id="_d8669772-242b-7036-e5ef-d591bcfcc522"><span class="fmt-caption-label">10<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>17<span class="fmt-clause-delim">.</span></span><span class="fmt-caption-delim"><tab/></span><semx element="title" source="_22078850-28f9-91a0-7993-d4daa9451867">Concluding overview of requirements for 3D geometry in the semantic web</semx></fmt-title><fmt-xref-label><span class="fmt-element-name">Clause</span> 10<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>17</fmt-xref-label>
<p id="_e8d026ad-605c-4a96-b068-9bda88b8f0a7" semx-id="_e8d026ad-605c-4a96-b068-9bda88b8f0a7">The requirements indicate that a GeoSPARQL extension for 3D should provide a common semantic and query layer for heterogeneous 3D representations. Such an extension should support the representation and semantic linking of 3D geometries, 3D topological and directional relations, spatial analysis and measurement, positioning and coordinate reference systems, visualisation, as well as routing. The standard should interoperate with established geometry formats and domain-specific models. This would allow specialised formats to retain their detailed geometric, visual, and domain-specific capabilities, while GeoSPARQL provides a common framework for representing, linking, discovering, and querying their spatial semantics in the Semantic Web. In this role, GeoSPARQL 3D would function primarily as an interoperability layer between geospatial, BIM, and other 3D domains.</p>
</clause>
</clause>

<clause id="_03358179-a6c1-a8ea-b5d0-e1eb63f1926d" obligation="normative" semx-id="_03358179-a6c1-a8ea-b5d0-e1eb63f1926d" displayorder="18">
<title id="_0b77cecd-108f-f36f-dcd3-7d903ba307ee" semx-id="_fe323986-d144-e93e-f83a-7b109279aaeb">Beneficiaries and benefits</title><fmt-title depth="1" id="_6eee8dd7-181b-6b9a-895e-c18478690839"><span class="fmt-caption-label">11<span class="fmt-clause-delim">.</span></span><span class="fmt-caption-delim"><tab/></span><semx element="title" source="_0b77cecd-108f-f36f-dcd3-7d903ba307ee">Beneficiaries and benefits</semx></fmt-title><fmt-xref-label><span class="fmt-element-name">Clause</span> 11</fmt-xref-label>
<p id="_9943a078-805c-c64d-7526-026f5b83d245" semx-id="_9943a078-805c-c64d-7526-026f5b83d245">This section describes the potential  beneficiaries and benefits of representing 3D spatial data using semantic and graph technologies. The requirements identified for 3D-GeoSPARQL provide a basis for achieving these benefits by supporting the representation, linking, querying, and reasoning of 3D spatial information across different domains and datasets. Furthermore, a collection of use cases demonstrate how semantic and graph technologies are used together with spatial data to tackle real world problems.</p>

<clause id="_b7eaa045-7ba1-0eff-1254-2518741edc8e" obligation="normative" semx-id="_b7eaa045-7ba1-0eff-1254-2518741edc8e">
<title id="_8b7bcebd-1b38-e9ae-a735-682cff143519" semx-id="_83f53559-2d61-2f37-1d7b-93eb356f4725">Semantic-functional value of 3D-GeoSPARQL</title><fmt-title depth="2" id="_837b2fa9-cfdd-0b22-65e2-bfd7ebc4129d"><span class="fmt-caption-label">11<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>1<span class="fmt-clause-delim">.</span></span><span class="fmt-caption-delim"><tab/></span><semx element="title" source="_8b7bcebd-1b38-e9ae-a735-682cff143519">Semantic-functional value of 3D-GeoSPARQL</semx></fmt-title><fmt-xref-label><span class="fmt-element-name">Clause</span> 11<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>1</fmt-xref-label>
<p id="_e6260ffa-279a-c553-10fb-743233899973" semx-id="_e6260ffa-279a-c553-10fb-743233899973">Value for data reasoning, formal description, and semantic interoperability</p>

<clause id="_c6524c29-8299-0fd7-f09d-40b76d451cea" obligation="normative" semx-id="_c6524c29-8299-0fd7-f09d-40b76d451cea">
<title id="_7de88f0e-0fc7-4adb-7a2d-ffd46d5ba579" semx-id="_e96e3f12-2471-3a58-fa1b-569714f04f8a">Meaningful creation and querying of 3D geometry</title><fmt-title depth="3" id="_60df8bb0-3d3d-2907-5e76-307f67de8cd7"><span class="fmt-caption-label">11<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>1<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>1<span class="fmt-clause-delim">.</span></span><span class="fmt-caption-delim"><tab/></span><semx element="title" source="_7de88f0e-0fc7-4adb-7a2d-ffd46d5ba579">Meaningful creation and querying of 3D geometry</semx></fmt-title><fmt-xref-label><span class="fmt-element-name">Clause</span> 11<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>1<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>1</fmt-xref-label>
<p id="_0df82485-0478-532a-bec0-862d88bca93a" semx-id="_0df82485-0478-532a-bec0-862d88bca93a">Whether manually or programmatically defining 3D geometries in BIM, GEO or CG environments, the GeoSPARQL standard could provide or connect to vocabulary to represent 3D structures. A 3D cube, for example, can be represented in multiple ways:</p>

<ul id="_2561c73c-8a87-26af-91c6-3a8cbce20138" semx-id="_2561c73c-8a87-26af-91c6-3a8cbce20138"><li id="_9a5d10bd-8154-f643-8416-ab9d48d10dd3"><fmt-name id="_5b88ccf5-92a9-f555-12a6-2dde503bfdc2">—</fmt-name><p id="_1a60f406-08f5-d475-74cd-3d4343294fd9" semx-id="_1a60f406-08f5-d475-74cd-3d4343294fd9">As a set of bounding points, lines, and faces;</p>
</li>
<li id="_5300482c-5fb5-19cf-6200-bdd2916c22a9"><fmt-name id="_c88185bc-22ee-b1bf-1f0c-a24d36f4e4cd">—</fmt-name><p id="_7a94b05b-c9ef-7c04-52e2-381d8c3c74fd" semx-id="_7a94b05b-c9ef-7c04-52e2-381d8c3c74fd">As a base surface with an extrude function;</p>
</li>
<li id="_e5948bd9-54e8-b66f-3ede-9767de130cc6"><fmt-name id="_0648af69-5779-4c18-a3ed-4be1062f1d5e">—</fmt-name><p id="_afbc5860-f322-ae39-5dd1-d18a1fd33e89" semx-id="_afbc5860-f322-ae39-5dd1-d18a1fd33e89">As an 3D geometric primitive (e.g., the concept of a ‘cube’ as a spatial figure).</p>
</li>
</ul>

<p id="_0454f22b-6896-9093-e673-1a1b9b58be3b" semx-id="_0454f22b-6896-9093-e673-1a1b9b58be3b">Once GeoSPARQL offers the means to define and link 3D geometries in these different ways, it will allow both humans and machines to interpret and interact with the data effectively.</p>
</clause>

<clause id="_94a418c2-918b-fb61-56ab-f6c4529140a4" obligation="normative" semx-id="_94a418c2-918b-fb61-56ab-f6c4529140a4">
<title id="_153c3aca-1113-f73c-bd44-05c640e5d006" semx-id="_3b5ce4a3-8871-b3c8-2d2f-d384c4887697">Meaningful creation and querying of 3D topological relationships</title><fmt-title depth="3" id="_03b1c6c2-4651-1950-3c72-72b6ce3c130f"><span class="fmt-caption-label">11<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>1<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>2<span class="fmt-clause-delim">.</span></span><span class="fmt-caption-delim"><tab/></span><semx element="title" source="_153c3aca-1113-f73c-bd44-05c640e5d006">Meaningful creation and querying of 3D topological relationships</semx></fmt-title><fmt-xref-label><span class="fmt-element-name">Clause</span> 11<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>1<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>2</fmt-xref-label>
<p id="_20268bfd-e0a3-4a8f-74ae-5ad1f4cab989" semx-id="_20268bfd-e0a3-4a8f-74ae-5ad1f4cab989">Is “3D Geometry A” inside, touching, overlapping, intersecting or above “3D Geometry B”?</p>

<p id="_f5d92c09-ea61-aa53-8739-43c819693af8" semx-id="_f5d92c09-ea61-aa53-8739-43c819693af8">Being able to describe and use such topological relationships is crucial for conducting spatial analyses, formulating rules, and deriving knowledge from different heterogeneous datasets.</p>

<p id="_f3aa06d6-a558-9412-fc5c-ce00a28cf7c6" semx-id="_f3aa06d6-a558-9412-fc5c-ce00a28cf7c6">Beyond describing topological relationships, one should be able to query them:</p>

<quote id="_28fc3c8d-079c-1e58-497b-6e92734d885c" semx-id="_28fc3c8d-079c-1e58-497b-6e92734d885c"><p id="_2865b89b-231a-eb40-957b-25f6a9ba2ee1" semx-id="_2865b89b-231a-eb40-957b-25f6a9ba2ee1">What is the spatial relationship between 3D Geometry A and 3D Geometry B?</p>
</quote>

<p id="_511d343b-e38b-a338-9541-8a40d37284bf" semx-id="_511d343b-e38b-a338-9541-8a40d37284bf">Such queries should return the appropriate topological result (e.g., “intersects”), which is essential for use cases like clash detection in design validation</p>
</clause>

<clause id="_658980a7-9fce-1c23-5d0d-bb2cebc9a2aa" obligation="normative" semx-id="_658980a7-9fce-1c23-5d0d-bb2cebc9a2aa">
<title id="_92a705df-60ad-34c3-8cb7-4ec86c789767" semx-id="_9119bafb-c775-0412-4a38-76338745e053">Derivation of geometric and topological knowledge</title><fmt-title depth="3" id="_4aa7a102-c7ea-6cae-de59-bd4829f68e70"><span class="fmt-caption-label">11<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>1<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>3<span class="fmt-clause-delim">.</span></span><span class="fmt-caption-delim"><tab/></span><semx element="title" source="_92a705df-60ad-34c3-8cb7-4ec86c789767">Derivation of geometric and topological knowledge</semx></fmt-title><fmt-xref-label><span class="fmt-element-name">Clause</span> 11<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>1<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>3</fmt-xref-label>
<p id="_c2d1c360-6a0c-a5c5-7646-25001fa6a7ec" semx-id="_c2d1c360-6a0c-a5c5-7646-25001fa6a7ec">From explicitly modeled geometric and topological data, one should be able to infer implicit knowledge.</p>

<p id="_0f1e1fe2-28bb-9445-846c-7f48fe4f82a8" semx-id="_0f1e1fe2-28bb-9445-846c-7f48fe4f82a8">For instance, if 3D Geometry A is 3D inside 3D Geometry B, then we can infer that:</p>

<quote id="_5ffdbff3-92bc-5e31-42af-75878900b7f4" semx-id="_5ffdbff3-92bc-5e31-42af-75878900b7f4"><p id="_c58d2169-e487-c15a-13da-aefb4f70325c" semx-id="_c58d2169-e487-c15a-13da-aefb4f70325c">A does not “touch” B, and A does not “overlap” with B,</p>
</quote>

<p id="_1007fa8e-158d-82f7-a9ad-dab630c76b46" semx-id="_1007fa8e-158d-82f7-a9ad-dab630c76b46">even if these facts are not explicitly stated.</p>

<p id="_9475cd20-1f3b-619d-340a-b52a58ec3f11" semx-id="_9475cd20-1f3b-619d-340a-b52a58ec3f11">For entailment an application that is compliant with the geo-spatial 3D specification would be able to answer queries like: “Does this tree have a 3D geometry?”</p>

<p id="_1a7da110-edca-f6d7-82bc-99d1a86a3ab8" semx-id="_1a7da110-edca-f6d7-82bc-99d1a86a3ab8">If the tree is defined using a MultiSolid geometry, then the answer should be “yes,” even if this is not explicitly declared as such.</p>
</clause>
</clause>

<clause id="_75e225c6-7ac0-3a9a-c75f-bc74af482cba" obligation="normative" semx-id="_75e225c6-7ac0-3a9a-c75f-bc74af482cba">
<title id="_91e24dfa-4879-5f55-b176-20f470ed6f58" semx-id="_e96c43d6-e197-44e3-5ff2-76f52cab3e8e">Value for user experience, visualization, and application use of 3D-GeoSPARQL</title><fmt-title depth="2" id="_1e2d53ba-54f2-3d51-bd29-2dbc8cafc550"><span class="fmt-caption-label">11<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>2<span class="fmt-clause-delim">.</span></span><span class="fmt-caption-delim"><tab/></span><semx element="title" source="_91e24dfa-4879-5f55-b176-20f470ed6f58">Value for user experience, visualization, and application use of 3D-GeoSPARQL</semx></fmt-title><fmt-xref-label><span class="fmt-element-name">Clause</span> 11<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>2</fmt-xref-label>
<clause id="_9e98a8be-2aac-27e0-2521-21a1105ed9e4" obligation="normative" semx-id="_9e98a8be-2aac-27e0-2521-21a1105ed9e4">
<title id="_adad413e-a8ed-b0b3-82a9-ea7dba5dadd1" semx-id="_bee0ee4a-34fe-7c8e-e61b-f11e12552e28">Visualization</title><fmt-title depth="3" id="_b35815d6-43dd-6f38-bb7d-f54b82050459"><span class="fmt-caption-label">11<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>2<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>1<span class="fmt-clause-delim">.</span></span><span class="fmt-caption-delim"><tab/></span><semx element="title" source="_adad413e-a8ed-b0b3-82a9-ea7dba5dadd1">Visualization</semx></fmt-title><fmt-xref-label><span class="fmt-element-name">Clause</span> 11<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>2<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>1</fmt-xref-label>
<p id="_75a442e7-388d-8bcd-9832-a668940f909e" semx-id="_75a442e7-388d-8bcd-9832-a668940f909e">There is a growing need to visualize how 3D data in BIM and GIS relate to one another. Stakeholders want to see BIM and/or 3D Geo data of a newly planned structure visualized within the 3D Geo and/or BIM context of the existing digital city. A shared vocabulary that can present both domains in an integrated way supports this goal.</p>
</clause>

<clause id="_92fc978f-1088-0e11-7102-f195e7c713e8" obligation="normative" semx-id="_92fc978f-1088-0e11-7102-f195e7c713e8">
<title id="_d451a171-da5c-54ed-472d-38ddacd74a64" semx-id="_bfb7e6f1-bc11-9f2f-9dcb-484740315b65">Calculation and analysis</title><fmt-title depth="3" id="_ccf7b738-b88c-0467-8c3f-711e30e5848a"><span class="fmt-caption-label">11<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>2<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>2<span class="fmt-clause-delim">.</span></span><span class="fmt-caption-delim"><tab/></span><semx element="title" source="_d451a171-da5c-54ed-472d-38ddacd74a64">Calculation and analysis</semx></fmt-title><fmt-xref-label><span class="fmt-element-name">Clause</span> 11<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>2<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>2</fmt-xref-label>
<p id="_511d745c-79d8-7ee4-ab48-e1a8477b9f21" semx-id="_511d745c-79d8-7ee4-ab48-e1a8477b9f21">Semantic modelled 3D-model enables powerful computation and analysis. Analytical results can be displayed in dashboards operating within the GeoBIM domain. This creates a bridge between asset management (typically GIS-oriented) and project management (typically BIM-oriented), allowing for cross-domain collaboration and decision-making.</p>
</clause>

<clause id="_6b0da5b8-700e-86ee-2bdb-bfed8a04eb4d" obligation="normative" semx-id="_6b0da5b8-700e-86ee-2bdb-bfed8a04eb4d">
<title id="_7033f1c3-e37a-32cf-96b1-144bf39afed7" semx-id="_3809eac2-55a8-6c76-7bcb-948955d60c17">Spatial querying</title><fmt-title depth="3" id="_cbb6d251-d269-cb86-ac64-99d0ed862d16"><span class="fmt-caption-label">11<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>2<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>3<span class="fmt-clause-delim">.</span></span><span class="fmt-caption-delim"><tab/></span><semx element="title" source="_7033f1c3-e37a-32cf-96b1-144bf39afed7">Spatial querying</semx></fmt-title><fmt-xref-label><span class="fmt-element-name">Clause</span> 11<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>2<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>3</fmt-xref-label>
<p id="_874d83f2-e094-4e0c-eae2-4a3e2a64f49f" semx-id="_874d83f2-e094-4e0c-eae2-4a3e2a64f49f">Spatial querying is a key function within a federated GeoBIM Network. Questions such as “What material is located in this area?” or “Where is space available for new cables and pipelines?” are examples of 3D spatial queries that can be answered when GeoSPARQL 3D is functioning effectively across systems and semantics.</p>
</clause>

<clause id="_3c48d6aa-016f-72f6-e040-92203928912f" obligation="normative" semx-id="_3c48d6aa-016f-72f6-e040-92203928912f">
<title id="_d923f5dc-9372-50ec-77cd-f8320b24bd5f" semx-id="_ff479a24-5d78-0c2f-6e43-556b443ed566">Machine control</title><fmt-title depth="3" id="_9005edfc-a7f2-3b6d-50e6-2953bc9ca34e"><span class="fmt-caption-label">11<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>2<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>4<span class="fmt-clause-delim">.</span></span><span class="fmt-caption-delim"><tab/></span><semx element="title" source="_d923f5dc-9372-50ec-77cd-f8320b24bd5f">Machine control</semx></fmt-title><fmt-xref-label><span class="fmt-element-name">Clause</span> 11<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>2<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>4</fmt-xref-label>
<p id="_f9a5af1c-5207-d220-8a22-565eccbdad18" semx-id="_f9a5af1c-5207-d220-8a22-565eccbdad18">A shared semantic GeoBIM Network also supports machine control. The GIS domain typically describes the existing situation, while the BIM domain describes the intended or future situation. This combination can be used to automatically instruct and guide machines in the built environment.</p>
</clause>

<clause id="_93fad057-5d9b-fbef-2365-ee6a93a63b62" obligation="normative" semx-id="_93fad057-5d9b-fbef-2365-ee6a93a63b62">
<title id="_d97ad0b2-e62e-6195-a29a-2c395cb04272" semx-id="_89094c11-b6ac-bdc3-72bc-a151de587deb">Modeling, simulation and planning</title><fmt-title depth="3" id="_9a2f83ff-385c-910f-7075-13c1cdfb0692"><span class="fmt-caption-label">11<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>2<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>5<span class="fmt-clause-delim">.</span></span><span class="fmt-caption-delim"><tab/></span><semx element="title" source="_d97ad0b2-e62e-6195-a29a-2c395cb04272">Modeling, simulation and planning</semx></fmt-title><fmt-xref-label><span class="fmt-element-name">Clause</span> 11<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>2<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>5</fmt-xref-label>
<p id="_41ab9a3f-d827-3016-ba9a-b8f59943c1dc" semx-id="_41ab9a3f-d827-3016-ba9a-b8f59943c1dc">A network of datasets with 3D-data should support the modeling, simulation and planning of future changes to the built environment. Users — whether human or machine — can use the network and GeoSPARQL 3D to propose and model modifications in either BIM or GIS formats. A well-functioning 3D semantic GeoBIM Network enables this kind of forward-looking spatial planning and design.</p>
</clause>
</clause>

<clause id="_4952a975-378e-960a-21e0-13dafd01cb0e" obligation="normative" semx-id="_4952a975-378e-960a-21e0-13dafd01cb0e">
<title id="_8933c99d-6f93-e4bb-c5a6-61047853ce1a" semx-id="_98d4e0a0-7d36-5f08-0c9f-55b84afe5a51">Added value of the 3D-GeoSPARQL for Organisations</title><fmt-title depth="2" id="_b79aaa59-9fa2-a374-5880-f1c759ab3ee7"><span class="fmt-caption-label">11<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>3<span class="fmt-clause-delim">.</span></span><span class="fmt-caption-delim"><tab/></span><semx element="title" source="_8933c99d-6f93-e4bb-c5a6-61047853ce1a">Added value of the 3D-GeoSPARQL for Organisations</semx></fmt-title><fmt-xref-label><span class="fmt-element-name">Clause</span> 11<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>3</fmt-xref-label>
<clause id="_434a799e-0e81-697d-5aea-2fdb0b0ca5fa" obligation="normative" semx-id="_434a799e-0e81-697d-5aea-2fdb0b0ca5fa">
<title id="_77438520-f58c-e2dd-49f8-ab516e48fdf7" semx-id="_3a748e22-cc8b-5672-0570-e625bd0944bb">Brings together different domains (GIS, BIM, CG)</title><fmt-title depth="3" id="_ba8a5bce-9ea5-2bdc-416a-a02f73da16e0"><span class="fmt-caption-label">11<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>3<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>1<span class="fmt-clause-delim">.</span></span><span class="fmt-caption-delim"><tab/></span><semx element="title" source="_77438520-f58c-e2dd-49f8-ab516e48fdf7">Brings together different domains (GIS, BIM, CG)</semx></fmt-title><fmt-xref-label><span class="fmt-element-name">Clause</span> 11<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>3<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>1</fmt-xref-label>
<p id="_11bc074e-6d25-2aea-1e3b-7ee74d631354" semx-id="_11bc074e-6d25-2aea-1e3b-7ee74d631354">GeoSPARQL 3D can help in bringing together different domains that work with 3D information.</p>
</clause>

<clause id="_2ae6bf4c-70ed-228f-bb57-fe202acb9757" obligation="normative" semx-id="_2ae6bf4c-70ed-228f-bb57-fe202acb9757">
<title id="_01f9ed5c-b58e-cb81-75fc-f35ae2336dce" semx-id="_78666eba-0d39-bcf7-0c1b-17a1ef956fca">Enables extension to vocabularies from other domains</title><fmt-title depth="3" id="_cb890462-0174-1471-bd83-bb8131ef8fa1"><span class="fmt-caption-label">11<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>3<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>2<span class="fmt-clause-delim">.</span></span><span class="fmt-caption-delim"><tab/></span><semx element="title" source="_01f9ed5c-b58e-cb81-75fc-f35ae2336dce">Enables extension to vocabularies from other domains</semx></fmt-title><fmt-xref-label><span class="fmt-element-name">Clause</span> 11<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>3<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>2</fmt-xref-label>
<p id="_79b439aa-a86c-5faf-1fa2-a06e52d1ecc9" semx-id="_79b439aa-a86c-5faf-1fa2-a06e52d1ecc9">The vocabulary can have the possiblity to extend to vocabulaires with 3D geometry or topology from other domains, for example the Building Ontology Topology (BOT) or RELOC Ontology. Multi-domain models with georeferenced 3D and 2D city models could be also linked by CityRDF ontology based on CityGML 3.0 standard. GeoSPARQL 3D would be important enabler for data retrieval, update and analysis for such models. In addition to ontologies, it can also establish relationships to 3D file formats from other domains, such as glTF.</p>
</clause>

<clause id="_28355b5c-7086-d905-b155-69b8b63e8a31" obligation="normative" semx-id="_28355b5c-7086-d905-b155-69b8b63e8a31">
<title id="_207978ad-65ba-49b4-e3c2-497931c4ebcc" semx-id="_981cbdf7-caae-e71b-df5f-96d99ee51e29">Facilitates better interoperability between knowledge domains with spatial components (geography, aerospace, architecture, product design, (bio)chemistry, IoT, …)</title><fmt-title depth="3" id="_0a94c318-8770-5b58-ffd1-85308f78783a"><span class="fmt-caption-label">11<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>3<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>3<span class="fmt-clause-delim">.</span></span><span class="fmt-caption-delim"><tab/></span><semx element="title" source="_207978ad-65ba-49b4-e3c2-497931c4ebcc">Facilitates better interoperability between knowledge domains with spatial components (geography, aerospace, architecture, product design, (bio)chemistry, IoT, …)</semx></fmt-title><fmt-xref-label><span class="fmt-element-name">Clause</span> 11<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>3<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>3</fmt-xref-label>
<p id="_f2d88549-8eb3-c3c8-7cea-5225af5e23ed" semx-id="_f2d88549-8eb3-c3c8-7cea-5225af5e23ed">A unified geometric language can help by migrating ideas and methods across fields, accelerating innovation. For example a parametic design algoritm, rule, or llm making use of geometry and topology originated in aerospace, can be re-used in the architecture domain.</p>
</clause>

<clause id="_c79dcd6c-ceb7-1f21-98f4-49c6fc1dcca3" obligation="normative" semx-id="_c79dcd6c-ceb7-1f21-98f4-49c6fc1dcca3">
<title id="_6e916239-3faf-76ca-cdf2-9f4350020bb0" semx-id="_6aaace8f-19f2-42dd-2a01-63529ed94790">Integrated spatial analysis in digital twins</title><fmt-title depth="3" id="_4f18ba26-e0a8-ce1c-3058-509eef7ab8c8"><span class="fmt-caption-label">11<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>3<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>4<span class="fmt-clause-delim">.</span></span><span class="fmt-caption-delim"><tab/></span><semx element="title" source="_6e916239-3faf-76ca-cdf2-9f4350020bb0">Integrated spatial analysis in digital twins</semx></fmt-title><fmt-xref-label><span class="fmt-element-name">Clause</span> 11<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>3<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>4</fmt-xref-label>
<p id="_d0aa4c72-ddb6-11f7-85f9-c2c84a33dadb" semx-id="_d0aa4c72-ddb6-11f7-85f9-c2c84a33dadb">Digital twins combine data from multiple sources, including building models, geospatial datasets, infrastructure  and regulatory information. These sources may use different data formats, data models, and vocabularies. Integrating them for spatial analysis can require dedicated software, data transformations, and application-specific connections.</p>

<p id="_48f5aa94-6c19-d088-daec-7a3506cb741f" semx-id="_48f5aa94-6c19-d088-daec-7a3506cb741f">Many spatial analyses, such as construction height verification, clearance analysis, distance calculation, and clash detection, can already be performed using existing GIS, BIM, and 3D processing software. The benefits of 3D-GeoSPARQL is therefore not necessarily to introduce these analyses, but to make them accessible through a common semantic and query framework.</p>

<p id="_74d39a43-b162-6e9d-9982-2bc50955e820" semx-id="_74d39a43-b162-6e9d-9982-2bc50955e820">By providing a common framework for representing and querying 3D spatial information, 3D-GeoSPARQL could enable spatial operations to be combined with semantic queries across heterogeneous datasets. For example, a building permit checking process could identify a building’s relevant components from an BIM-derived knowledge graph, retrieve the applicable spatial regulations, and evaluate the geometry of the proposed building against existing urban assets.</p>

<p id="_8162cf9c-5f39-05fc-603b-0f2c7d3b9797" semx-id="_8162cf9c-5f39-05fc-603b-0f2c7d3b9797">This approach could make spatial analysis processes more reusable across datasets and applications, reduce dependence on application-specific integrations, and improve the traceability of the spatial information used in decision-making.</p>

<p id="_5da0c91e-11ac-b341-e5aa-1aafe1f48f80" semx-id="_5da0c91e-11ac-b341-e5aa-1aafe1f48f80">Regulatory compliance and design validation — building height, underpasses, overhangs and distances to the waterfront.</p>

<p id="_ee758e6d-7da6-8572-e8f5-37d26fbccc6f" semx-id="_ee758e6d-7da6-8572-e8f5-37d26fbccc6f">Building safety and evacuation — escape routes, compartmentalisation and accessibility of emergency exits.</p>

<p id="_65219b28-e1d7-bbbb-dc31-e06d9d49032c" semx-id="_65219b28-e1d7-bbbb-dc31-e06d9d49032c">Infrastructure coordination and construction planning — cables, pipes, foundations and clashes.</p>

<p id="_e51d309f-c132-ac55-ba9e-11a2b755669e" semx-id="_e51d309f-c132-ac55-ba9e-11a2b755669e">Urban planning and environmental risk assessment — flooding, clearance, shadow and spatial integration.</p>
</clause>
</clause>
</clause>










<references id="_057b622a-ec74-4196-e5c8-f76056857bb4" normative="true" obligation="informative" semx-id="_057b622a-ec74-4196-e5c8-f76056857bb4" displayorder="10">
